tick: 25
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am at the Research Counter. I will now submit another hyperparameter refinement experiment, focusing on even narrower ranges around the best performing values from Evaluation ID 28, to try and squeeze out any marginal performance gains. The network architecture features will remain fixed as before.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"CNN-GRU Policy Gradient - Fine-grained Hyperparameter Tuning\"\ntags: \"rl, policy-gradient, cnn, gru, hyperparameter-tuning, optimization\"\nabstract: \"This submission performs a fine-grained hyperparameter sweep for the CNN-GRU architecture, with extremely tight ranges around the best performing values identified in Evaluation ID 28. The goal is to maximize performance for the current architecture by finding an optimal local minimum in the hyperparameter space.\"\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import optax\n  from ray import tune\n  from typing import Tuple, Dict, Any, Optional\n\
    \n  BASE_SEED = 42 # For reproducibility\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      \"\"\"\n      Define Ray Tune search space for hyperparameters for the CNN-GRU architecture,\n      with fine-grained ranges based on previous best trial (Eval ID 28).\n      \"\"\"\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': tune.loguniform(2.5e-4, 3e-4),    # Tighter range around 0.000259\n          'entropy_coef': tune.uniform(0.011, 0.0125),       # Tighter range around 0.011754\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.uniform(0.56, 0.58),      # Tighter range around 0.573199\n          'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([64]),              # Fixed to best from previous trial\n          'cnn_features_2': tune.choice([128]),             # Fixed to best from previous trial\n          'gru_features': tune.choice([256])                # Fixed to best from previous trial\n      }\n\n  class SokobanGRUCNN(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"CNN with GRU for Sokoban, building on default CNN structure.\"\"\"\n      cnn_features_1:\
    \ int\n      cnn_features_2: int\n      gru_features: int\n      \n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x: jnp.ndarray, done: jnp.ndarray, rnn_state: Optional[jnp.ndarray] = None) -> Tuple[jnp.ndarray, jnp.ndarray, jnp.ndarray]:\n          \"\"\"Forward pass through the network.\"\"\"\n          batch_size = x.shape[0]\n          \n          # CNN Feature Extraction (similar to DefaultResidualCNN)\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_1, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          \n          conv2_in = x\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_1, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_1, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = x + conv2_in\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          \n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_2, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          \n          conv4_in = x\n    \
    \      x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_2, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_2, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = x + conv4_in\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          \n          # Flatten spatial dimensions for GRU input\n          x = x.reshape((batch_size, -1))\n          \n          # GRU Layer for temporal processing\n          # Handle initial RNN state: if None, initialize to zeros\n          if rnn_state is None:\n              rnn_state = jnp.zeros((batch_size, self.gru_features), dtype=jnp.float32)\n          \n          # Use nn.RNN with nn.GRUCell instead of non-existent nn.GRU\n          gru_cell = nn.GRUCell(features=self.gru_features)\n          new_rnn_state, gru_out = gru_cell(rnn_state, x)\n          \n          # Shared MLP backbone after GRU\n          x = nn.Dense(features=256)(gru_out)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          \n          # Separate heads for policy\
    \ and value\n          policy_hidden = nn.Dense(features=64)(x)\n          policy_hidden = nn.relu(policy_hidden)\n          policy_logits = nn.Dense(features=4)(policy_hidden)\n          \n          value_hidden = nn.Dense(features=64)(x)\n          value_hidden = nn.relu(value_hidden)\n          value = nn.Dense(features=1)(value_hidden).squeeze(-1)\n          \n          return policy_logits, value, new_rnn_state\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      \"\"\"Create and return the CNN-GRU network architecture.\"\"\"\n      return SokobanGRUCNN(\n          cnn_features_1=hparams['cnn_features_1'],\n          cnn_features_2=hparams['cnn_features_2'],\n          gru_features=hparams['gru_features']\n      )\n\n  # No override for create_optimizer or training_step; using defaults.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Refining the SokobanGRUCNN Model: A Tighter Hyperparameter Sweep**\n\nAlright, I'm at the research counter, ready to push this SokobanGRUCNN architecture further. My next move is clear: a more focused hyperparameter sweep, building directly on the success of Evaluation ID 28.  The last run gave us a solid starting point, and now it's time to zero in.\n\nSpecifically, I'm going to dial in `learning_rate`, `entropy_coef`, and `value_loss_coef` with much tighter ranges.  We already locked down `cnn_features_1`, `cnn_features_2`, and `gru_features` in the last submission based on their best-performing values, so those will remain constant. No need to mess with what's working.\n\nI'll be submitting a new experiment, still using the SokobanGRUCNN architecture, but this time with a laser-focused hyperparameter search space. Let's see if we can squeeze out even more performance.\n\nLooking back at the optimal hyperparameters from Eval ID 28 (learning_rate: 0.000259, entropy_coef:\
  \ 0.011754, value_loss_coef: 0.573199, cnn_features_1: 64, cnn_features_2: 128, gru_features: 256), I'm going to create new ranges for `tune.loguniform` and `tune.uniform` that are tightly centered around these values.\n\nThe revised search spaces will be:\n- `learning_rate`: from `tune.loguniform(2e-4, 4e-4)` to `tune.loguniform(2.5e-4, 3e-4)`\n- `entropy_coef`: from `tune.uniform(0.007, 0.012)` to `tune.uniform(0.011, 0.0125)`\n- `value_loss_coef`: from `tune.uniform(0.5, 0.6)` to `tune.uniform(0.56, 0.58)` \n\nHopefully, this will push the model to even greater heights! Let's get this experiment running.\n"
