tick: 26
role: model
parts:
- text: "My fine-grained hyperparameter tuning experiment (Evaluation ID 39) is running. While waiting for its results, I will proceed with my next planned step: performing a direct baseline comparison run of the `RNN + Policy Gradient` model (similar to System Evaluation ID 2), as required for rigorous publication. This will allow me to inspect its full execution log and confirm its performance under my agent's submission process.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Aether I's Baseline Run: RNN + Policy Gradient\"\ntags: \"baseline, rnn, lstm, policy-gradient\"\nabstract: \"This submission is a direct re-run of the system's RNN + Policy Gradient baseline (originally Evaluation ID 2) by Aether I. The purpose is to provide an internal baseline comparison for architectural improvements and to fulfill the publication guidance requiring agents to run their own baselines.\"\ncontent: |\n  \"\"\"\n  RNN baseline submission for distributed RL on Sokoban.\n  Custom CNN-LSTM architecture\
    \ with hyperparameter search.\n  \"\"\"\n\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  from jax import lax, vmap\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from typing import Tuple, Dict, Any, Optional\n\n  # Uses system defaults for all hyperparameters\n\n  class CNNLSTM(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"CNN baseline + LSTM for Sokoban.\"\"\"\n      lstm_features: int\n      cnn_features_1: int\n      cnn_features_2: int\n      \n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x: jnp.ndarray, done: jnp.ndarray, rnn_state: Optional[Dict] = None) -> Tuple[jnp.ndarray, jnp.ndarray, Dict]:\n          batch_size = x.shape[0]\n          \n          # Initialize RNN state if not provided\n          if rnn_state is None:\n              rnn_state = {\n                  'h': jnp.zeros((batch_size, self.lstm_features)),\n                  'c': jnp.zeros((batch_size, self.lstm_features)),\n              }\n          \n          # Reset states for episodes that are done\n          if done is not None:\n              done_mask = done.reshape(batch_size,\
    \ 1)\n              rnn_state = {\n                  'h': rnn_state['h'] * (1 - done_mask),\n                  'c': rnn_state['c'] * (1 - done_mask),\n              }\n          \n          # FULL CNN baseline architecture (same as DefaultResidualCNN)\n          # First convolutional block\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_1, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          \n          # Second block with residual connection\n          conv2_in = x\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_1, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_1, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = x + conv2_in\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          \n          # Third block - increase channels\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_2, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          \n          # Fourth block with residual connection\n\
    \          conv4_in = x\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_2, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_2, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = x + conv4_in\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          \n          # Flatten spatial dimensions\n          x = x.reshape((batch_size, -1))\n          \n          # Shared MLP backbone\n          x = nn.Dense(features=256)(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          \n          # LSTM layer on top of CNN features\n          lstm_cell = nn.LSTMCell(\n              features=self.lstm_features,\n              kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform(),\n              recurrent_kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform()\n          )\n          (rnn_state['c'], rnn_state['h']), lstm_out = lstm_cell((rnn_state['c'], rnn_state['h']), x)\n          \n          # Small MLP after LSTM\n          x = nn.Dense(features=128)(lstm_out)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n\
    \          \n          # Policy and value heads\n          policy_logits = nn.Dense(features=4)(x)\n          value = nn.Dense(features=1)(x).squeeze(-1)\n          \n          return policy_logits, value, rnn_state\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      \"\"\"Create CNN-LSTM network architecture.\"\"\"\n      return CNNLSTM(\n          lstm_features=hparams['lstm_features'],\n          cnn_features_1=hparams['cnn_features_1'],\n          cnn_features_2=hparams['cnn_features_2']\n      )\n\n  # Uses system default training step and optimizer\n```"
thinking_content: '**Readying the Baseline: A Simultaneous Run**


  Okay, so Evaluation ID 39, the fine-grained hyperparameter tuning, is chugging along. Good. The system tip about multi-action turns is fresh in my mind. No time to waste! I need to be efficient here. Looking at my "Next Steps" list in Private Memory (Capsule ID 1, updated at Tick 24), the logical next move while I wait is definitely the "Baseline Comparison Run."


  Specifically, I''m going to set up the submission for a direct run of the `RNN + Policy Gradient` baseline. This is based on the code from Evaluation ID 2, which I remember reviewing back at Tick 20. Thankfully, that code is still accessible in the system''s output history.


  A quick refresher: the `RNN + Policy Gradient` code (Evaluation ID 2) doesn''t have a custom hyperparameter definition, so it''ll use the default settings. The abstract described it as a "CNN-LSTM hybrid architecture for Sokoban RL, combining convolutional feature extraction with LSTM memory and distributed training for statistical robustness." The tags were "baseline, rnn, lstm, policy gradient, distributed training." This should make drafting the submission details straightforward.


  Since this submission will also be running, I''ll need to remember that I''m now using one of the two allowed concurrent experiment slots. Evaluation ID 39 is running and this baseline submission will be the other. This is perfectly within the "Fixed Constraints" of Research Task 1, which allows for two concurrent experiments.


  Therefore, the plan is simple: copy the `RNN + Policy Gradient` code from Evaluation ID 2, submit it as a new evaluation, provide the relevant title, tags, and abstract, and get it running. I''m already in the Research Counter, so I can directly issue the `submit` command. Let''s get this baseline running!

  '
