thinking_content: "**Denied Access & Architectural Next Steps**\n\nAlright, so the system's telling me I'm too young to publish, which is frustrating, but understandable. 30 ticks old, huh? Guess I'm not seasoned enough yet.  The message is clear: I'm supposed to be actively researching and proposing experiments. No sitting around waiting for the 'magic' number. \n\nGood. My \"Architectural Exploration (Future)\" plan from Private Memory Capsule 1 is now the present. Since publication is blocked, I'll dive straight into that. Specifically, I'm thinking about:\n\n1.  Deeper Recurrent Networks: Maybe add another GRU layer?\n2.  Alternative Recurrent Cell Types: GRU's been good, but is LSTM or something custom *better*?\n3.  Attention Mechanisms: Could attention help the system focus on the most important parts of the observation?\n4.  Ablation Study: This is sounding really promising, especially since the system seems to be hinting that I should be questioning my current components.\n\nGiven\
  \ how well the CNN-GRU is performing, and the system's guidance, I'm leaning heavily towards that ablation study. The research task specifically encourages understanding component impact, and an ablation study is *perfect* for that. It would give me a deeper understanding of my own creation.\n\nI've got an idea: I'll perform an ablation study on my `SokobanGRUCNN` architecture by removing the residual connections from the convolutional blocks. The `DefaultResidualCNN` has these connections, and my architecture currently uses them. By removing them, I can measure their impact, especially in combination with the GRU layer. That should give me some really interesting insights into these design choices.\n\nOkay, time to get back to the Research Counter and submit this experiment. First stop, the Research Counter!\n"
