tick: 31
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am at the Research Counter. As I cannot publish yet, I will proceed with further architectural exploration as outlined in my updated research plan. My next experiment will be an ablation study: removing the residual connections from the convolutional blocks of the `SokobanGRUCNN` to understand their contribution to performance. I will use the fixed best hyperparameters identified in Evaluation ID 28.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"CNN-GRU Policy Gradient - Ablation: No Residual Connections\"\ntags: \"rl, policy-gradient, cnn, gru, ablation-study, agent-architecture\"\nabstract: \"This submission conducts an ablation study on the CNN-GRU architecture, specifically removing the residual connections within the convolutional feature extraction blocks. The goal is to assess the impact of these connections on the agent's planning capabilities and overall solve rate in the Sokoban environment, using the best hyperparameters from Evaluation ID 28.\"\ncontent: |\n  import\
    \ jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import optax\n  from ray import tune\n  from typing import Tuple, Dict, Any, Optional\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42 # For reproducibility\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      \"\"\"\n      Define fixed hyperparameters based on the best trial from Evaluation ID 28.\n      \"\"\"\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': tune.choice([0.000259]),  # Fixed to best from Eval ID 28\n          'entropy_coef': tune.choice([0.011754]),   # Fixed to best from Eval ID 28\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.choice([0.573199]),# Fixed to best from Eval ID 28\n          'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([64]),       # Fixed to best from previous trial\n          'cnn_features_2': tune.choice([128]),      # Fixed to best from previous trial\n          'gru_features': tune.choice([256])         # Fixed to best from previous trial\n      }\n\n  class SokobanGRUCNN_NoResidual(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"CNN with GRU for Sokoban, building on default\
    \ CNN structure, WITHOUT residual connections.\"\"\"\n      cnn_features_1: int\n      cnn_features_2: int\n      gru_features: int\n      \n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x: jnp.ndarray, done: jnp.ndarray, rnn_state: Optional[jnp.ndarray] = None) -> Tuple[jnp.ndarray, jnp.ndarray, jnp.ndarray]:\n          \"\"\"Forward pass through the network.\"\"\"\n          batch_size = x.shape[0]\n          \n          # CNN Feature Extraction (similar to DefaultResidualCNN, but without residuals)\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_1, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          \n          # Second block - NO residual connection\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_1, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_1, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n          # x = x + conv2_in  # Removed residual connection\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          \n \
    \         # Third block - increase channels\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_2, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          \n          # Fourth block - NO residual connection\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_2, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_2, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n          # x = x + conv4_in  # Removed residual connection\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          \n          # Flatten spatial dimensions for GRU input\n          x = x.reshape((batch_size, -1))\n          \n          # GRU Layer for temporal processing\n          # Handle initial RNN state: if None, initialize to zeros\n          if rnn_state is None:\n              rnn_state = jnp.zeros((batch_size, self.gru_features), dtype=jnp.float32)\n          \n          # Use nn.RNN with nn.GRUCell instead of non-existent nn.GRU\n          gru_cell = nn.GRUCell(features=self.gru_features)\n\
    \          new_rnn_state, gru_out = gru_cell(rnn_state, x)\n          \n          # Shared MLP backbone after GRU\n          x = nn.Dense(features=256)(gru_out)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          \n          # Separate heads for policy and value\n          policy_hidden = nn.Dense(features=64)(x)\n          policy_hidden = nn.relu(policy_hidden)\n          policy_logits = nn.Dense(features=4)(policy_hidden)\n          \n          value_hidden = nn.Dense(features=64)(x)\n          value_hidden = nn.relu(value_hidden)\n          value = nn.Dense(features=1)(value_hidden).squeeze(-1)\n          \n          return policy_logits, value, new_rnn_state\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      \"\"\"Create and return the CNN-GRU network architecture.\"\"\"\n      return SokobanGRUCNN_NoResidual(\n          cnn_features_1=hparams['cnn_features_1'],\n          cnn_features_2=hparams['cnn_features_2'],\n          gru_features=hparams['gru_features']\n      )\n\n  # No override\
    \ for create_optimizer or training_step; using defaults.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Ablation Study: Removing Residual Connections from SokobanGRUCNN**\n\nOkay, so I'm diving into this ablation study. The goal is clear: understand the impact of those residual connections in the `SokobanGRUCNN` architecture. I want to see how performance changes when we strip them out.\n\nFirst things first, I need to get my hands dirty with the code. I'll be referencing `aether/cnn_gru_sokoban.py` for context, but I'll be submitting the modified version directly.  No need to create a separate branch for this – I'm confident enough in my changes.\n\nThe real meat of the modification will be in the `__call__` method.  I'll zero in on those lines that add back `conv2_in` and `conv4_in` to the feature maps and comment them out. Simple enough, but the key is maintaining focus on the *architecture* as the only variable.\n\nThat's where the hyperparameters come in. I'm going to lock those down to the best-performing configuration from Evaluation ID 28.  `learning_rate`, `entropy_coef`,\
  \ `value_loss_coef`, `cnn_features_1`, `cnn_features_2`, and `gru_features` are staying put. This way, any performance shift is directly attributable to the removed residual connections, which is what I'm after.\n\nFinally, I'll update the metadata: the `title`, `tags`, and `abstract` need to clearly reflect this is an ablation study specifically targeting the residual connections. That's crucial for future reference and reproducibility.  \n\nOnce that's done, I'll submit the modified code and let the experiments begin. Looking forward to seeing the results!\n"
