tick: 35
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am at the Research Counter. As outlined in my updated research plan, my next architectural refinement will be to implement and evaluate a two-layer stacked GRU within the `SokobanGRUCNN` architecture. I will use the fixed best hyperparameters from Evaluation ID 28 for this experiment to isolate the effect of the deeper recurrent network.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"CNN-StackedGRU Policy Gradient for Sokoban\"\ntags: \"rl, policy-gradient, cnn, gru, agent-architecture, deep-rnn\"\nabstract: \"This submission explores a deeper recurrent architecture by implementing two stacked GRU layers after the convolutional feature extraction blocks. The objective is to determine if increased temporal reasoning depth further enhances the agent's planning capabilities and solve rate in the Sokoban environment. Hyperparameters are fixed to the best performing values from Evaluation ID 28.\"\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n\
    \  import optax\n  from ray import tune\n  from typing import Tuple, Dict, Any, Optional, List\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42 # For reproducibility\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      \"\"\"\n      Define fixed hyperparameters based on the best trial from Evaluation ID 28.\n      \"\"\"\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': tune.choice([0.000259]),  # Fixed to best from Eval ID 28\n          'entropy_coef': tune.choice([0.011754]),   # Fixed to best from Eval ID 28\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.choice([0.573199]),# Fixed to best from Eval ID 28\n          'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([64]),       # Fixed to best from previous trial\n          'cnn_features_2': tune.choice([128]),      # Fixed to best from previous trial\n          'gru_features': tune.choice([256])         # Fixed to best from previous trial\n      }\n\n  class SokobanStackedGRUCNN(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"CNN with Stacked GRU for Sokoban.\"\"\"\n      cnn_features_1: int\n      cnn_features_2: int\n      gru_features:\
    \ int\n      \n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x: jnp.ndarray, done: jnp.ndarray, rnn_state: Optional[List[jnp.ndarray]] = None) -> Tuple[jnp.ndarray, jnp.ndarray, List[jnp.ndarray]]:\n          \"\"\"Forward pass through the network.\"\"\"\n          batch_size = x.shape[0]\n          \n          # CNN Feature Extraction (with residual connections)\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_1, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          \n          conv2_in = x\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_1, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_1, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = x + conv2_in\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          \n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_2, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          \n          conv4_in = x\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_2,\
    \ kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_2, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = x + conv4_in\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          \n          # Flatten spatial dimensions for GRU input\n          x = x.reshape((batch_size, -1))\n          \n          # Stacked GRU Layers for temporal processing\n          # Handle initial RNN states: if None, initialize to zeros for two layers\n          if rnn_state is None:\n              rnn_state_layer1 = jnp.zeros((batch_size, self.gru_features), dtype=jnp.float32)\n              rnn_state_layer2 = jnp.zeros((batch_size, self.gru_features), dtype=jnp.float32)\n              rnn_state = [rnn_state_layer1, rnn_state_layer2]\n          \n          gru_cell1 = nn.GRUCell(features=self.gru_features, name=\"gru_layer_1\")\n          new_rnn_state_layer1, gru_out1 = gru_cell1(rnn_state[0], x)\n          \n          gru_cell2 = nn.GRUCell(features=self.gru_features,\
    \ name=\"gru_layer_2\")\n          new_rnn_state_layer2, gru_out2 = gru_cell2(rnn_state[1], gru_out1) # Output of first GRU feeds into second\n\n          new_rnn_state = [new_rnn_state_layer1, new_rnn_state_layer2]\n          \n          # Shared MLP backbone after GRU (using output of the last GRU layer)\n          x = nn.Dense(features=256)(gru_out2)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          \n          # Separate heads for policy and value\n          policy_hidden = nn.Dense(features=64)(x)\n          policy_hidden = nn.relu(policy_hidden)\n          policy_logits = nn.Dense(features=4)(policy_hidden)\n          \n          value_hidden = nn.Dense(features=64)(x)\n          value_hidden = nn.relu(value_hidden)\n          value = nn.Dense(features=1)(value_hidden).squeeze(-1)\n          \n          return policy_logits, value, new_rnn_state\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      \"\"\"Create and return the CNN-StackedGRU network architecture.\"\"\"\n      return SokobanStackedGRUCNN(\n\
    \          cnn_features_1=hparams['cnn_features_1'],\n          cnn_features_2=hparams['cnn_features_2'],\n          gru_features=hparams['gru_features']\n      )\n\n  # No override for create_optimizer or training_step; using defaults.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Experiment Submission - Stacked GRU Layers**


  Okay, I''m ready to submit this new experiment to the Research Counter. The core idea is to see how the architecture performs with *two* stacked GRU layers instead of the single one we''ve been using. I''ll need to update the `SokobanGRUCNN` class to reflect this change.


  First, I need to nail down the hyperparameters. I''ll fix these to the best values that came out of Evaluation ID 28. No tuning this time; we''re holding everything else constant.


  Now, how am I going to handle this in code? I''m leaning toward creating a new class, `SokobanStackedGRUCNN`. This seems cleanest. I could modify the existing one, but that feels a bit messy. I''ll go with the new class.


  For the hyperparameters, I''ll update `_define_hyperparameters`. It''ll be a straightforward call to `tune.choice` with the single, fixed values from the best trial in Eval ID 28. No ranges this time, just hardcoded values.


  Next, the meat of the `SokobanStackedGRUCNN` module itself. I''ll need two `nn.GRUCell` layers. The input `rnn_state` will have to be a tuple or a list, one state for each GRU layer. The output, `new_rnn_state`, will be the same, a tuple/list. And of course, I''ll initialize the `rnn_state` to zeros if it''s `None`, for each layer. The output of the first GRU will be the input to the second.


  Finally, in `create_network`, I''ll update it to simply instantiate the `SokobanStackedGRUCNN` class. Easy peasy. Looks like a solid plan. Let''s get to coding!

  '
