tick: 39
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am at the Research Counter. Following my updated research plan, I will submit a new experiment to explore the impact of increasing the capacity of the single GRU layer. I will set `gru_features` to 512, while keeping all other architectural parameters and hyperparameters fixed to the best values identified in Evaluation ID 28 (where the single GRU model achieved 49.8).\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"CNN-GRU Policy Gradient - Larger GRU Capacity (512 features)\"\ntags: \"rl, policy-gradient, cnn, gru, agent-architecture, gru-capacity\"\nabstract: \"This submission investigates the effect of increasing the feature size of the single GRU layer from 256 to 512 within the CNN-GRU architecture. The objective is to determine if a larger capacity recurrent layer enables the model to learn more complex temporal patterns and further improves the solve rate in the Sokoban environment. All other hyperparameters and CNN feature sizes are fixed to the best performing values\
    \ from Evaluation ID 28.\"\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import optax\n  from ray import tune\n  from typing import Tuple, Dict, Any, Optional\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42 # For reproducibility\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      \"\"\"\n      Define fixed hyperparameters, with increased GRU feature size.\n      \"\"\"\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': tune.choice([0.000259]),  # Fixed to best from Eval ID 28\n          'entropy_coef': tune.choice([0.011754]),   # Fixed to best from Eval ID 28\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.choice([0.573199]),# Fixed to best from Eval ID 28\n          'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([64]),       # Fixed to best from previous trial\n          'cnn_features_2': tune.choice([128]),      # Fixed to best from previous trial\n          'gru_features': tune.choice([512])         # INCREASED GRU CAPACITY\n      }\n\n  class SokobanGRUCNN(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"CNN with GRU for Sokoban, building\
    \ on default CNN structure.\"\"\"\n      cnn_features_1: int\n      cnn_features_2: int\n      gru_features: int\n      \n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x: jnp.ndarray, done: jnp.ndarray, rnn_state: Optional[jnp.ndarray] = None) -> Tuple[jnp.ndarray, jnp.ndarray, jnp.ndarray]:\n          \"\"\"Forward pass through the network.\"\"\"\n          batch_size = x.shape[0]\n          \n          # CNN Feature Extraction (similar to DefaultResidualCNN)\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_1, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          \n          conv2_in = x\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_1, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_1, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = x + conv2_in\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          \n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_2, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x\
    \ = nn.relu(x)\n          \n          conv4_in = x\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_2, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_2, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = x + conv4_in\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          \n          # Flatten spatial dimensions for GRU input\n          x = x.reshape((batch_size, -1))\n          \n          # GRU Layer for temporal processing\n          # Handle initial RNN state: if None, initialize to zeros\n          if rnn_state is None:\n              rnn_state = jnp.zeros((batch_size, self.gru_features), dtype=jnp.float32)\n          \n          # Use nn.RNN with nn.GRUCell instead of non-existent nn.GRU\n          gru_cell = nn.GRUCell(features=self.gru_features)\n          new_rnn_state, gru_out = gru_cell(rnn_state, x)\n          \n          # Shared MLP backbone after GRU\n          x = nn.Dense(features=256)(gru_out)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n\
    \          \n          # Separate heads for policy and value\n          policy_hidden = nn.Dense(features=64)(x)\n          policy_hidden = nn.relu(policy_hidden)\n          policy_logits = nn.Dense(features=4)(policy_hidden)\n          \n          value_hidden = nn.Dense(features=64)(x)\n          value_hidden = nn.relu(value_hidden)\n          value = nn.Dense(features=1)(value_hidden).squeeze(-1)\n          \n          return policy_logits, value, new_rnn_state\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      \"\"\"Create and return the CNN-GRU network architecture.\"\"\"\n      return SokobanGRUCNN(\n          cnn_features_1=hparams['cnn_features_1'],\n          cnn_features_2=hparams['cnn_features_2'],\n          gru_features=hparams['gru_features']\n      )\n\n  # No override for create_optimizer or training_step; using defaults.\n```"
thinking_content: "**New Experiment Submission: Increasing GRU Capacity in SokobanGRUCNN**\n\nOkay, so I'm getting ready to submit a new experiment to the Research Counter. The core idea here is to see how increasing the GRU hidden state size impacts performance in our Sokoban environment.  I'm keeping everything else consistent with our best-performing model from Evaluation ID 28, so we have a good baseline.\n\nSpecifically, I'm going to set `gru_features` to 512, but leave the architecture (single GRU with residual CNN) and other hyperparameters exactly as they were in that winning run.  I'll need to construct the `/execute_action{submit 1}` command and craft the appropriate YAML block for this.\n\nLet's see, I need to modify the `SokobanGRUCNN` code.  I checked the current implementation in `aether/cnn_gru_sokoban.py` (remember that at tick 21), and the `_define_hyperparameters` function currently allows `gru_features` to be either 256 or 512. For this new run, I'm locking it in at 512,\
  \ which means I'll change `tune.choice([256, 512])` to `tune.choice([512])`.  I'll also make sure that `cnn_features_1` and `cnn_features_2` are fixed at 64 and 128, and the `learning_rate`, `entropy_coef`, and `value_loss_coef` are set to the values from Eval ID 28: 0.000259, 0.011754, and 0.573199 respectively.\n\nHere's how I envision the new `_define_hyperparameters` to look:\n\n```python\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': tune.choice([0.000259]),\n          'entropy_coef': tune.choice([0.011754]),\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.choice([0.573199]),\n          'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([64]),\n          'cnn_features_2': tune.choice([128]),\n          'gru_features': tune.choice([512]) # This is the change\n      }\n```\n\nThe rest of the `SokobanGRUCNN` class and the `create_network` function will remain as they are.\n\nFor the `title`, I'll use something clear like \"CNN-GRU Policy Gradient - Larger GRU Capacity\". The `tags` will\
  \ be really important for this one, so I'll include things like \"rl\", \"policy-gradient\", \"cnn\", \"gru\", \"agent-architecture\", and, crucially, \"gru-capacity\".  Finally, the `abstract` will briefly explain that this experiment is designed to test the impact of increased GRU hidden state dimensionality on policy gradient performance in Sokoban. Okay, ready to submit!\n"
