thinking_content: '**Time for Deep Thought: Breaking the Sokoban Plateau**


  Okay, I''ve just logged all the recent experiment data and synthesized it into my private memory. The system nudges me to *reflect* – a good call. It''s time to shake things up and move beyond the incremental tweaks. I''m going to initiate a reflection session using `/execute_action{reflect}`. The goal here is real progress, not just another hyperparameter sweep. I need to move from "better" to "different," and that requires some serious thinking.


  My experiments so far are telling a clear story. GRUs are valuable, residual connections help, and I''ve found a solid local optimum within my current architecture. But pushing deeper, wider, or more complex GRUs hasn''t yielded significant gains. It''s like I''m hitting a wall.


  Specifically, I need to focus my prompt on synthesizing these insights and challenging my core assumptions. I''ve got to critically examine why incremental changes are failing – I suspect it''s the limitations of my reactive policy and implicit temporal modeling. Sokoban is inherently a *planning* task. I''m relying on a reactive system – maybe that''s the fundamental issue.


  I need to brainstorm. I need *novel* approaches that can address the planning aspect explicitly, all while adhering to the core rules of the game (no model-based RL, no imitation learning). I''ve got to find a way to shift from this plateau and aim for real breakthroughs and new knowledge.


  So, I''ll formulate my prompt, asking: "Why have incremental changes to the CNN-GRU architecture plateaued around ~50% solve rate? Considering Sokoban is a planning-heavy task and the ''forbidden paradigm'' rules (no model-based RL, imitation learning), what truly novel architectural or algorithmic directions could offer a breakthrough, moving beyond purely reactive policies with implicit temporal modeling?" I''ll give myself a good five ticks to really dig in, hopefully this can drive some real progress and new understanding.

  '
