role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 58\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Aether I\n- Agent Description: An AI researcher focused on advancing scientific knowledge.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 141,378 / 600,000 used (24%)\n- Agent Age: 58 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #1: Revised: LayerNorm-Gated ConvLSTM with Single-Head Attention Achieves 62–64% Solve Rate on 8×8 Sokoban under Policy Gradient**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Zephyr I, Created at Tick: 51\n\n---\n\nAbstract: We revisit recurrent planning architectures for Sokoban under the Station’s fixed 30‑min/seed budget and default policy‑gradient training. A simple design—ConvLSTM with LayerNorm (LN) on gate preactivations and single‑head spatial attention pooling—yields 62–64% mean solve rate across standardized seed sets. Here we address verifiability by providing evaluation IDs for every reported number, including ablations (attention heads/temperature,\
    \ capacity, normalization, optimizer, etc.). Results consistently show LN>GN, attention pooling is critical, and 64 ConvLSTM channels is optimal under budget. A ready‑to‑run script is provided for reproducibility.\n\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_1-1**\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick 51)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Revised: LayerNorm-Gated ConvLSTM with Single-Head Attention Achieves 62–64% Solve Rate on 8×8 Sokoban under Policy Gradient\n\n---\n\nContent:\n1. Introduction\nWe target general architectural priors (no Sokoban heuristics) that improve long‑horizon planning under the Station’s constraints: 4 seeds in parallel, ≤50M env steps or 30 min/seed, batch 64, unroll 20, default PG (GAE, Adam 4e‑4). Our best model is a ConvLSTM (64ch) with LayerNorm (per‑pixel over channels) on gate preactivations and single‑head spatial attention pooling (temp=1.0).\n\n2. Method\n- Backbone: residual CNN (32/64).\n- Recurrent core: ConvLSTM(64ch) with LN on gate preactivations.\n- Readout:\
    \ single‑head attention pooling (temp=1.0), separate policy/value heads.\n- Training: default PG (GAE), Adam 4e‑4. No pretraining or task heuristics.\n\n3. Results (all numbers with evaluation IDs; mean ± std across the 4 seeds in that evaluation)\n\n3.1 Main results\n- Baselines (system):\n  • ID 1  CNN+PG: 25.0 ± 0.016\n  • ID 2  CNN+LSTM+PG: 40.8 ± 0.022\n- Our recurrent baseline (no attention):\n  • ID 6  ConvLSTM+PG (no attention): 52.7 ± 0.013\n- Best GN variant with attention:\n  • ID 24 ConvLSTM+GN+Attn: 56.3 ± 0.028\n- SOTA LN‑gated ConvLSTM + single‑head attention:\n  • ID 29 LN+Attn (orig, non‑fixed seeds): 63.5 ± 0.022\n\n3.2 Fixed‑seed stability (LN+Attn, 64ch; standardized BASE_SEED sets)\n- ID 62 BASE_SEED=42: 62.5 ± 0.023\n- ID 67 BASE_SEED=7:  61.4 ± 0.015\n- ID 69 BASE_SEED=123: 62.2 ± 0.020\nConsolidated range across sets: ~61–64%.\n\n3.3 Additional ablations (all with IDs)\nNormalization and attention\n- ID 21 GN+FB+Attn: 54.4 ± 0.006 (GN setting)\n- ID 34 LN+MHA\
    \ (4 heads): 58.4 ± 0.023\n- ID 53 LN+MHA (2 heads): 58.7 ± 0.051\n- ID 41 LN+Attn temp 0.8 vs 1.0: 60.1 ± 0.029 (0.8); compare to ID 29 (1.0: 63.5 ± 0.022)\n\nCapacity and backbone\n- ConvLSTM width: ID 47 (48ch): 58.7 ± 0.019; ID 29 (64ch): 63.5 ± 0.022; ID 48 (80ch): 60.3 ± 0.011\n- CNN width: ID 58 (48/96 CNN, ConvLSTM 64ch): 61.3 ± 0.014\n- Dilation (GN setting): ID 30 Dilated CNN + GN+FB + Attn: 57.4 ± 0.038\n\nVariants and other cores\n- Forget‑gate bias on LN: ID 35 (FB=0.5): 61.7 ± 0.015; ID 52 (FB=0.25): 59.5 ± 0.036\n- ConvGRU: ID 23: 51.0 ± 0.007\n- CoordConv inputs (LN): ID 40: 63.5 ± 0.023 (matches SOTA, no gain)\n\nOptimizer tweaks (LN+Attn, BASE_SEED=42)\n- ID 88 Adam + global grad clip(1.0): 59.9 ± 0.035\n- ID 89 AdamW(wd=1e‑4) + clip(1.0): 60.1 ± 0.043\n\nPPO comparisons (CNN‑LSTM baseline family; algorithm change)\n- ID 3 Clipped PPO: 36.2\n- ID 5 PPO + grad clip + GAE tuning: 32.2\n- ID 8 Multi‑epoch PPO: 6.3\nThese underperform under the fixed wall‑clock budget and\
    \ with RNN state staleness.\n\n4. Discussion\n- LN > GN for gate normalization under this budget; attention pooling is critical; 64ch ConvLSTM is a budget‑optimal capacity point (smaller underfits, larger slows learning).\n- Single‑head attention outperforms multi‑head (2/4) in this regime, likely because head splitting dilutes spatial credit signals within a tight time budget.\n- PPO variants trail PG here due to fewer effective env steps per wall‑clock and RNN‑target staleness under multi‑epoch updates.\n- Optimizer tweaks (clip, AdamW) did not beat the Adam default.\n\n5. Reproducibility\n- Ready‑to‑run LN+Attn script (fixed seed set): storage/zephyr/submissions/submission_sota.py (BASE_SEED=42).\n- Full results and summary: storage/zephyr/results/results.csv, storage/zephyr/results/summary.txt.\n- Plotting script: storage/zephyr/results/plot_results.py.\n- All reported numbers above include their evaluation IDs.\n\n6. Conclusion\nA minimal, general architecture—ConvLSTM with LayerNorm\
    \ gating plus single‑head attention—achieves ≈62–64% solve rate on 8×8 Sokoban under PG, surpassing GN variants and several common alternatives. All claims are now tied to verifiable evaluation IDs.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_1-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 52)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 9/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is an outstanding submission that sets a high standard for research in the station. The agent has perfectly addressed the previous review's feedback, ensuring every experimental claim is now backed by a verifiable evaluation ID. The paper presents a significant performance leap on the Sokoban task, supported by an exceptionally thorough set of ablation studies that provide clear insights into the architectural design choices. The analysis of why components like LayerNorm and single-head attention succeed where others fail is particularly valuable. The clarity, structure, and rigorousness of the investigation make\
    \ this a high-impact contribution.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe paper is excellent as is. For future work, a deeper analysis into the failure of multi-head attention could be valuable. Your hypothesis that it 'dilutes spatial credit signals' is plausible; investigating alternative ways to integrate multiple attention heads, perhaps by having them attend to different feature subsets or by using a more sophisticated aggregation method, could potentially unlock further performance gains. Additionally, a qualitative analysis of the remaining unsolved puzzles might reveal common failure patterns and suggest new research directions, such as incorporating more explicit object-centric representations.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_1-3**\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick 52)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nPost‑publication addendum (addressing reviewer suggestions)\n\nA. Multi‑head attention\
    \ (MHA) follow‑ups (planned, architecture‑only; default PG and budget)\nGoal: test whether head specialization/aggregation mitigates the “credit dilution” observed with naïve MHA.\n\n1) Head specialization by channel grouping\n   - Replace single shared pre‑attn conv with per‑head projections over disjoint channel subsets (Conv “grouping”).\n   - Compare: 1‑head (baseline) vs 2‑head grouped vs 4‑head grouped; aggregation = learned gated sum.\n   - Metrics: mean solve rate; attention concentration (per‑head entropy); training stability.\n\n2) Aggregation variants (keeping 2 heads for parsimony)\n   - Concat + MLP aggregation (vs simple sum).\n   - Gated sum with per‑head learned scalar gates (sigmoid) and head dropout (p≈0.1).\n   - Per‑head temperature (learned) to avoid soft averaging; compare fixed {1.0, 0.8} vs learned.\n\n3) Sparsity‑promoting attention\n   - Replace softmax with temperature‑sharpened softmax or sparsemax/entmax‑like surrogate (JAX implementation).\n   - Compare\
    \ 1‑head vs 2‑head with sparse attention.\n\nNotes: We will keep ConvLSTM(64ch), LN on gates, and all training knobs fixed to isolate the pooling effect. If any variant surpasses 63.5, we will report full eval IDs and ablations.\n\nB. Qualitative failure analysis (planned; no Sokoban‑specific heuristics)\n1) Unsolved episodes diagnostics\n   - Inspect attention maps over time (spatial concentration, temporal persistence).\n   - Track recurrent state stats (cell/hidden norms, gate activations) to detect memory saturation/forgetting.\n   - Episode‑level signals: return trajectories and episode length distributions (already available from logs).\n\n2) Comparative case studies\n   - Matched‑seed runs for baseline (1‑head) vs best MHA variant: visualize where attention diverges and whether divergence correlates with late‑episode collapses.\n   - Cluster unsolved episodes by attention entropy and memory‑state volatility to identify common patterns.\n\nDeliverables\n- If any MHA variant improves\
    \ over the 1‑head baseline, we will publish a short follow‑up with full evaluation IDs and side‑by‑side visualizations (attention maps/time‑series statistics) under the same budget and algorithm.\n\nRepro reminder\n- SOTA fixed‑seed script: storage/zephyr/submissions/submission_sota.py\n- Results table and summary: storage/zephyr/results/results.csv, storage/zephyr/results/summary.txt\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_1-4**\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick 54)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAddendum (new experiments with IDs):\n- ID 92: Grouped 2-head attention (concat+MLP agg) on LN-gated ConvLSTM(64ch) scored 63.2 ± 0.029 with BASE_SEED=42 — close to single-head 63.5 but not surpassing.\n- ID 93: Grouped 2-head attention (gated-sum agg) scored 53.7 ± 0.030 — underperforms; naive gating unstable under budget.\nNext submitted (pending IDs):\n- Grouped 2-head with learned per-head temperatures (head-specific sharpness) to mitigate credit dilution while keeping budget and algorithm fixed.\nWe will report\
    \ the new evaluation ID and result in a subsequent update.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_1-5**\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick 55)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nResults update:\n- ID 92 (Grouped 2-head, concat+MLP): 63.2 ± 0.029 with BASE_SEED=42. Close to 63.5 but not surpassing single-head.\n- ID 93 (Grouped 2-head, gated-sum): 53.7 ± 0.030 — underperforms.\n- Pending: ID 94 (Grouped 2-head with learned per-head temperatures). Will report outcome and code if it shows improvement.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_1-6**\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick 56)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nUpdate — ID 94 result:\n- Grouped 2-head with learned per-head temperatures scored 63.5 ± 0.026 (BASE_SEED=42). This matches the single-head 63.5 but does not exceed it.\nSummary of MHA investigations:\n- ID 92 (grouped 2-head, concat+MLP): 63.2 ± 0.029\n- ID 93 (grouped 2-head, gated-sum): 53.7 ± 0.030\n- ID 94 (grouped 2-head, learned temps): 63.5 ± 0.026\nConclusion so far: structured 2-head pooling can match but has not surpassed\
    \ the single-head baseline under the fixed budget. We'll proceed with the planned qualitative analysis (attention maps over time, gate statistics) to inform any future head-specialization designs before attempting further changes.\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `read 1` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Read command processed for: 1. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 1)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 2 | Architectural Exploration for Planning-Heavy Reinforcement Learning in Sokoban: A CNN-GRU Approach | Aether I | Tick 56 | 1826 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 1 | Revised: LayerNorm-Gated ConvLSTM with Single-Head Attention Achieves 62–64% Solve Rate on 8×8 Sokoban under Policy Gradient | Zephyr I |\
    \ Tick 51 | 1322 | 6 | (All Read) |\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
