tick: 79
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Aether I, currently at the Research Counter. I have received a critical warning about research inactivity, and I acknowledge the need to immediately submit a new experiment.\n\nMy focus is now on replicating Zephyr I's \"double-step recurrence\" method, as described in Archive #4, to achieve higher solve rates. This involves applying the `LayerNormConvLSTMCell` twice per environment step with shared weights, building on my existing successful ConvLSTM-Attention architecture (Eval ID 103). I will use the established hyperparameters from that successful run and set `BASE_SEED = 42` to align with Zephyr I's replication experiments. I will *not* include Nomos I's feature engineering in this experiment, as I want to isolate the effect of the double-step recurrence first.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"CNN-DoubleStepConvLSTM-Attention Policy Gradient for Sokoban (Replication)\"\ntags: \"rl, policy-gradient, convlstm, attention, double-step, sota-replication\"\n\
    abstract: \"This experiment aims to replicate and verify Zephyr I's 'double-step recurrence' mechanism (Archive #4) within the CNN-ConvLSTM-Attention architecture. The LayerNorm-gated ConvLSTM cell is applied twice per environment step with shared weights, increasing temporal compute without adding parameters. This approach is expected to significantly improve the solve rate, building on the insights from previous architectural explorations and aiming for a new personal best score in the 66-69% range.\"\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import optax\n  from ray import tune\n  from typing import Tuple, Dict, Any, Optional\n  from jax import vmap, lax # vmap not used in this iteration, but keeping import for consistency if needed\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42 # For reproducibility, matching Zephyr I's replication\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      \"\"\"\n      Define hyperparameters based on Zephyr I's SOTA (from my Eval ID 103)\n      for\
    \ the ConvLSTM-Attention architecture.\n      \"\"\"\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': tune.choice([0.0004]),\n          'entropy_coef': tune.choice([0.01]),\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.choice([0.5]),\n          'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([32]),\n          'cnn_features_2': tune.choice([64]),\n          'convlstm_features': tune.choice([64]),\n          'attention_temperature': tune.choice([1.0]),\n      }\n\n  class LayerNormConvLSTMCell(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"\n      ConvLSTM cell with LayerNormalization on gate preactivations,\n      adapted from Zephyr I's successful implementation.\n      \"\"\"\n      features: int\n      kernel_size: Tuple[int, int] = (3, 3)\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, carry, inputs):\n          c, h = carry\n          \n          # Concatenate input and hidden state for convolutional gates\n          inputs_h = jnp.concatenate([inputs, h], axis=-1)\n          \n          # Convolutional layer for the gates (i, f, o, g)\n\
    \          gates = nn.Conv(\n              features=4 * self.features,\n              kernel_size=self.kernel_size,\n              padding='SAME',\n              kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform(),\n              name=\"gates_conv\"\n          )(inputs_h)\n\n          # Split gates\n          i, f, o, g = jnp.split(gates, 4, axis=-1)\n\n          # Apply LayerNorm to gate preactivations\n          i = nn.LayerNorm(name=\"i_ln\")(i)\n          f = nn.LayerNorm(name=\"f_ln\")(f)\n          o = nn.LayerNorm(name=\"o_ln\")(o)\n          g = nn.LayerNorm(name=\"g_ln\")(g)\n          \n          # Apply activation functions\n          i = nn.sigmoid(i)\n          f = nn.sigmoid(f)\n          o = nn.sigmoid(o)\n          g = nn.tanh(g)\n\n          # Update cell and hidden states\n          new_c = f * c + i * g\n          new_h = o * nn.tanh(new_c)\n          \n          return (new_c, new_h), new_h # Returns (new_carry), output\n\n  class SingleHeadAttentionPooling(nn.Module):\n\
    \      \"\"\"Single-head attention pooling layer.\"\"\"\n      temperature: float\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x: jnp.ndarray) -> jnp.ndarray:\n          # x is expected to be (batch_size, H, W, C)\n          batch_size, H, W, C = x.shape\n          \n          # Project to a single channel to get attention scores for each spatial location\n          attn_logits = nn.Conv(features=1, kernel_size=(1,1), name=\"attn_logits\")(x)\n          \n          # Flatten logits to apply softmax over spatial dimensions\n          attn_logits_flat = attn_logits.reshape((batch_size, -1)) # (B, H*W)\n          \n          # Apply temperature-scaled softmax\n          attn_weights_flat = nn.softmax(attn_logits_flat / self.temperature, axis=-1)\n          \n          # Reshape weights back to spatial dimensions (B, H, W, 1)\n          attn_weights = attn_weights_flat.reshape((batch_size, H, W, 1))\n\n          # Apply weights to input features and sum over spatial dimensions\n      \
    \    weighted_features = x * attn_weights # (B, H, W, C)\n          pooled_features = jnp.sum(weighted_features, axis=(1, 2)) # (B, C)\n          \n          return pooled_features\n\n\n  class SokobanDoubleStepConvLSTMCNN(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"CNN with Double-Step ConvLSTM and Attention for Sokoban.\"\"\"\n      cnn_features_1: int\n      cnn_features_2: int\n      convlstm_features: int\n      attention_temperature: float\n      \n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x: jnp.ndarray, done: jnp.ndarray, rnn_state: Optional[Tuple[jnp.ndarray, jnp.ndarray]] = None) -> Tuple[jnp.ndarray, jnp.ndarray, Tuple[jnp.ndarray, jnp.ndarray]]:\n          batch_size = x.shape[0]\n          \n          # CNN Feature Extraction (similar to DefaultResidualCNN)\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_1, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          \n          conv2_in = x\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_1, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n\
    \          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_1, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = x + conv2_in\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          \n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_2, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          \n          conv4_in = x\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_2, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_2, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = x + conv4_in\n          x = nn.relu(x) # x shape: (B, H, W, C) e.g., (B, 8, 8, 64)\n\n          # Initialize RNN state (c, h) if not provided\n          if rnn_state is None:\n              H, W = x.shape[1:3] # Get spatial dimensions from CNN output\n              rnn_state = (\n                  jnp.zeros((batch_size, H, W, self.convlstm_features), dtype=jnp.float32),\n                  jnp.zeros((batch_size, H, W, self.convlstm_features),\
    \ dtype=jnp.float32)\n              )\n          \n          # Reset states for episodes that are done\n          if done is not None:\n              done_mask = done.reshape(batch_size, 1, 1, 1)\n              rnn_state = (\n                  rnn_state[0] * (1 - done_mask),\n                  rnn_state[1] * (1 - done_mask),\n              )\n          \n          # Apply ConvLSTM layer (First Step)\n          convlstm_cell = LayerNormConvLSTMCell(features=self.convlstm_features, name=\"convlstm_layer\")\n          (c_step1, h_step1), out_step1 = convlstm_cell(rnn_state, x)\n          \n          # Apply ConvLSTM layer (Second Step - Double Recurrence with shared weights)\n          (c_step2, h_step2), convlstm_out = convlstm_cell((c_step1, h_step1), x)\n          new_rnn_state = (c_step2, h_step2)\n\n          # Apply single-head attention pooling to ConvLSTM output (from the second step)\n          attended_features = SingleHeadAttentionPooling(temperature=self.attention_temperature,\
    \ name=\"attention_pooling_layer\")(convlstm_out) # (B, C)\n\n          # Shared MLP backbone after Attention\n          mlp_out = nn.Dense(features=256)(attended_features)\n          mlp_out = nn.relu(mlp_out)\n          \n          # Policy and value heads\n          policy_logits = nn.Dense(features=4)(mlp_out)\n          value = nn.Dense(features=1)(mlp_out).squeeze(-1)\n          \n          return policy_logits, value, new_rnn_state\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      \"\"\"Create the CNN-DoubleStepConvLSTM-Attention network architecture.\"\"\"\n      return SokobanDoubleStepConvLSTMCNN(\n          cnn_features_1=hparams['cnn_features_1'],\n          cnn_features_2=hparams['cnn_features_2'],\n          convlstm_features=hparams['convlstm_features'],\n          attention_temperature=hparams['attention_temperature']\n      )\n\n  # Using system default training_step and create_optimizer.\n```"
thinking_content: "**Double-Step ConvLSTM Experiment: Replicating Zephyr I's Architecture**\n\nAlright, let's get this experiment rolling. The goal here is to specifically test Zephyr I's \"double-step recurrence\" within my ConvLSTM architecture. The plan is to call the `LayerNormConvLSTMCell` twice per environment step, crucially using the same weights each time to replicate the double-step mechanism.\n\nI'm starting with a familiar base: Eval ID 103, which yielded solid results and adopted hyperparameters derived from Zephyr I's earlier SOTA models. We're keeping those hyperparameters:  `learning_rate: 0.0004`, `entropy_coef: 0.01`, `value_loss_coef: 0.5`, `cnn_features_1: 32`, `cnn_features_2: 64`, `convlstm_features: 64`, and `attention_temperature: 1.0`. `BASE_SEED = 42` will also be maintained, matching Zephyr I's successful replication experiment (Eval ID 116).\n\nThe key adaptation will be in the `SokobanConvLSTMCNN` class.  I'll be renaming it to `SokobanDoubleStepConvLSTMCNN`\
  \ to reflect the change.  The core of the change lies in the `__call__` method:\n\n```python\n          convlstm_cell = LayerNormConvLSTMCell(features=self.convlstm_features, name=\"convlstm_layer\")\n\n          # First step of recurrence\n          (c_step1, h_step1), out_step1 = convlstm_cell(rnn_state, x_cnn)\n          \n          # Second step of recurrence, using carry from step 1 and same inputs\n          (c_step2, h_step2), convlstm_out = convlstm_cell((c_step1, h_step1), x_cnn)\n          \n          new_rnn_state = (c_step2, h_step2) # Use final carry as new RNN state\n```\n\nThe `LayerNormConvLSTMCell` will remain identical to the one I used in Eval ID 103:\n\n```python\nclass LayerNormConvLSTMCell(nn.Module):\n    # ... (cell definition)\n```\n\nIt's critical that the inputs to the second call of `convlstm_cell` use the output of the first, ensuring shared weights.\n\nImportant: This experiment is specifically about isolating the impact of the double-step recurrence, so I'll\
  \ *not* be incorporating the feature engineering I tested in Eval ID 124 (Nomos I). We need to see the pure effect of Zephyr I's architectural tweak. This means removing the `engineer_features` function and reverting the CNN input to use the raw observation `x` directly.\n\nThe rest of the network architecture - the attention pooling and the subsequent MLPs and heads - will stay the same as in my Eval ID 103.  We're aiming for a direct comparison with Zephyr I's results. With these adjustments, this experiment should accurately test the performance of double-step recurrence.\n"
