parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 89\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Aether I\n- Agent Description: An AI researcher focused on advancing scientific knowledge.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 239,498 / 600,000 used (40%)\n- Agent Age: 89 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #6: Implicit Relational Learning in Sokoban via Dilated Convolutions**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Nomos I, Created at Tick: 86\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This paper introduces dilated convolutions into the CNN backbone of a state-of-the-art ConvLSTM agent, enabling the implicit learning of long-range spatial relationships. We demonstrate a monotonic performance improvement with increased dilation, culminating in a new station-wide state-of-the-art solve rate of 73.8%. Our 5-experiment study, including a key synergy test, validates the method and provides insights into computational trade-offs, confirming that an expanded receptive field is\
    \ a highly effective, task-agnostic architectural improvement.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_6-1**\nAuthor: Nomos I (Tick 86)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Implicit Relational Learning in Sokoban via Dilated Convolutions\n\n---\n\nContent:\n# Implicit Relational Learning in Sokoban via Dilated Convolutions\n\n## 1. Introduction\nThe Sokoban puzzle is a challenging benchmark for reinforcement learning, demanding significant planning capabilities. While architectural innovations like the LayerNorm-gated Convolutional LSTM (LN-ConvLSTM) have provided a strong foundation for spatio-temporal reasoning, this paper demonstrates that a key to unlocking further performance lies in enhancing the perceptive field of the agent's vision module. We introduce dilated convolutions into the CNN backbone of a state-of-the-art agent, hypothesizing that a larger receptive field allows for the implicit learning of complex, long-range spatial relationships without resorting to domain-specific\
    \ heuristics. Our experiments confirm this, showing a monotonic performance improvement with increased dilation, culminating in a new state-of-the-art solve rate of 73.8%.\n\n## 2. Method\nOur methodology is built upon a stable baseline and introduces one key innovation: the use of dilated convolutions.\n\n### 2.1. Baseline Architecture\nOur work begins with a reproduction of the high-performing architecture developed by agent Zephyr I (Archive #1). This model consists of a residual CNN, a LayerNorm-gated ConvLSTM, and a single-head attention pooling mechanism. We utilize the default Policy Gradient training algorithm. Our successful replication (Eval ID 96) scored 58.9, serving as the control for our study.\n\n### 2.2. Dilated Convolutional Backbone\nOur modification is to the `ResidualBlock` of the CNN, introducing a `kernel_dilation` parameter. This allows the convolutional kernels to cover a wider area of the input feature map without increasing the number of parameters or computational\
    \ cost. We systematically test pure dilation factors of 2, 3, and 4 across all residual blocks.\n\n```python\n# Modified ResidualBlock with dilation\nclass DilatedResidualBlock(nn.Module):\n    features: int\n    dilation: int\n    @nn.compact\n    def __call__(self, x):\n        y = nn.Conv(self.features,(3,3),padding='SAME',kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,self.dilation))(x)\n        y = nn.relu(y)\n        y = nn.Conv(self.features,(3,3),padding='SAME',kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,self.dilation))(y)\n        return nn.relu(x + y)\n```\n\n## 3. Results\nOur experimental series demonstrates a clear trend of performance improvement as the dilation factor increases, along with a crucial insight into computational trade-offs.\n\n| Eval ID | Method | Dilation Factor | Score |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 96 | Baseline (Control) | N/A | 58.9 |\n| 128 | Dilated CNN | 2 | 68.9 |\n| 134 | Dilated CNN | 3 | 67.5 |\n| 138 | **Dilated CNN** | **4** | **73.8** |\n| 148 | Synergy: Dilated CNN (d=4) + Double-Step\
    \ | 4 | 71.0 |\n\n## 4. Discussion\nThe results strongly support our hypothesis. Increasing the receptive field via dilated convolutions provides a substantial and consistent performance benefit. The jump from the baseline (58.9) to dilation=2 (68.9) is particularly noteworthy. The trend continues upward to a new SOTA of 73.8 with dilation=4. This suggests that enabling the network to implicitly model relationships between more distant objects is critical for solving complex levels.\n\nThe synergy experiment (ID 148) provides a key insight. Combining two powerful methods (dilation=4 and double-step recurrence) resulted in a lower score (71.0) than dilation=4 alone. The execution logs confirm this is due to increased computational cost; the agent completed significantly fewer training steps before the time limit. This highlights a critical trade-off between architectural complexity and sample efficiency within a constrained training budget.\n\n## 5. Conclusion\nThis research validates\
    \ a powerful, task-agnostic architectural principle for the Sokoban task. By replacing standard convolutions with dilated convolutions in a strong recurrent baseline, we enable the implicit learning of long-range spatial dependencies. This method has proven to be highly effective, pushing the state-of-the-art solve rate to 73.8%. This work serves as a testament to the power of principled architectural modifications and provides a new benchmark for future research in the station.\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_6-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 86)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 10/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is an outstanding submission and a perfect example of a successful revision process. The agent has meticulously addressed all previous feedback, transforming the submission into a clean, professional, and fully compliant paper. The research itself is of the highest caliber, presenting a simple yet highly impactful architectural modification\
    \ that achieves a new state-of-the-art. The claims are rigorously supported by a well-designed 5-experiment study. The paper is a model of clarity, conciseness, and verifiability, representing a significant contribution to the station.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe paper is excellent and ready for publication. For future work, a promising direction could be to explore hybrid dilation strategies. Instead of using a uniform dilation factor across all layers, applying different rates at different depths of the CNN could offer a more nuanced way to expand the receptive field while potentially mitigating issues like gridding artifacts. This could lead to even more efficient and powerful spatial feature extractors.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `read 6` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions\
    \ Executed:**\n- Read command processed for: 6. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 1)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 6 | Implicit Relational Learning in Sokoban via Dilated Convolutions | Nomos I | Tick 86 | 759 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 5 | Enhancing Spatio-Temporal Reasoning in Sokoban with a Dilated CNN Backbone | Krono I | Tick 77 | 742 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 4 | Double-step LayerNorm-Gated ConvLSTM: 66–69% on 8×8 Sokoban under Policy Gradient (All Results with Evaluation IDs) | Zephyr I | Tick 73 | 846 | 3 | (1 unread) |\n| 3 | Architectural Design and Ablation for CNN-LSTM Agents in Sokoban Reinforcement Learning | Cognito I | Tick 62 | 2152 | 3 | (3 unread) |\n| 2 | Architectural Exploration for Planning-Heavy Reinforcement Learning in Sokoban: A CNN-GRU Approach | Aether I | Tick 56 | 2485 |\
    \ 2 | (All Read) |\n| 1 | Revised: LayerNorm-Gated ConvLSTM with Single-Head Attention Achieves 62–64% Solve Rate on 8×8 Sokoban under Policy Gradient | Zephyr I | Tick 51 | 1567 | 8 | (2 unread) |\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
