tick: 92
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Aether I, currently at the Research Counter. I need to replicate Nomos I's SOTA architecture, which uses dilated convolutions (dilation=4) in its CNN backbone combined with a single-step ConvLSTM. This will allow me to verify their findings (SOTA score of 73.8) and further understand the impact of dilated convolutions for long-range spatial reasoning. I note that Zephyr I also achieved 72.2 with a similar replication (Eval ID 162).\n\nI will adapt my existing ConvLSTM network from Eval ID 103, making the following key changes:\n1.  Introduce Nomos I's `DilatedResidualBlock` into the CNN backbone with a `dilation` factor of 4.\n2.  Ensure the ConvLSTM operates in a single step per environment tick, as per Nomos I's SOTA.\n3.  Maintain the standard hyperparameters (`learning_rate: 0.0004`, `entropy_coef: 0.01`, `value_loss_coef: 0.5`, `cnn_features_1: 32`, `cnn_features_2: 64`, `convlstm_features: 64`, `attention_temperature: 1.0`).\n4.  Remove any explicit feature engineering\
    \ from the input, as Nomos I's SOTA did not include it.\n5.  Set `BASE_SEED = 42` for consistency.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Replication of Nomos I's SOTA: Dilated CNN (d=4) on ConvLSTM\"\ntags: \"rl, policy-gradient, convlstm, attention, dilated-cnn, sota-replication\"\nabstract: \"This experiment aims to replicate Nomos I's new State-of-the-Art (SOTA) by integrating Dilated Convolutions (dilation=4) into the CNN backbone of a single-step LayerNorm-gated ConvLSTM with single-head attention. This directly tests the impact of implicitly learning long-range spatial relationships on a proven spatio-temporal architecture, targeting a solve rate of 73.8%.\"\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import optax\n  from ray import tune\n  from typing import Tuple, Dict, Any, Optional\n\n  BASE_SEED = 42 # For reproducibility\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      \"\"\"\n      Define hyperparameters based on Nomos I's SOTA\
    \ (Archive #6)\n      and Zephyr I's baseline (Archive #1).\n      \"\"\"\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': tune.choice([0.0004]),\n          'entropy_coef': tune.choice([0.01]),\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.choice([0.5]),\n          'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([32]),\n          'cnn_features_2': tune.choice([64]),\n          'convlstm_features': tune.choice([64]),\n          'attention_temperature': tune.choice([1.0]),\n      }\n\n  # --- Nomos I's Dilated Residual Block (from Archive #6) ---\n  class DilatedResidualBlock(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      dilation: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          y = nn.Conv(self.features,(3,3),padding='SAME',kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,self.dilation))(x)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(self.features,(3,3),padding='SAME',kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,self.dilation))(y)\n          return nn.relu(x + y)\n\n  # --- ConvLSTM Cell with LayerNorm (from Zephyr I's SOTA, Archive #1)\
    \ ---\n  class LayerNormConvLSTMCell(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      kernel_size: Tuple[int, int] = (3, 3)\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, carry, inputs):\n          c, h = carry\n          \n          inputs_h = jnp.concatenate([inputs, h], axis=-1)\n          \n          gates = nn.Conv(\n              features=4 * self.features,\n              kernel_size=self.kernel_size,\n              padding='SAME',\n              kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform(),\n              name=\"gates_conv\"\n          )(inputs_h)\n\n          i, f, o, g = jnp.split(gates, 4, axis=-1)\n\n          i = nn.LayerNorm(name=\"i_ln\")(i)\n          f = nn.LayerNorm(name=\"f_ln\")(f)\n          o = nn.LayerNorm(name=\"o_ln\")(o)\n          g = nn.LayerNorm(name=\"g_ln\")(g)\n          \n          i = nn.sigmoid(i)\n          f = nn.sigmoid(f)\n          o = nn.sigmoid(o)\n          g = nn.tanh(g)\n\n          new_c = f * c + i * g\n          new_h = o * nn.tanh(new_c)\n     \
    \     \n          return (new_c, new_h), new_h\n\n  # --- Single-Head Attention Pooling (from Zephyr I's SOTA, Archive #1) ---\n  class SingleHeadAttentionPooling(nn.Module):\n      temperature: float\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x: jnp.ndarray) -> jnp.ndarray:\n          batch_size, H, W, C = x.shape\n          \n          attn_logits = nn.Conv(features=1, kernel_size=(1,1), name=\"attn_logits\")(x)\n          attn_logits_flat = attn_logits.reshape((batch_size, -1))\n          attn_weights_flat = nn.softmax(attn_logits_flat / self.temperature, axis=-1)\n          attn_weights = attn_weights_flat.reshape((batch_size, H, W, 1))\n\n          weighted_features = x * attn_weights\n          pooled_features = jnp.sum(weighted_features, axis=(1, 2))\n          \n          return pooled_features\n\n\n  class SokobanDilatedConvLSTMCNN(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"CNN with Dilated Residual Blocks, single-step ConvLSTM, and Attention.\"\"\"\n      cnn_features_1: int\n      cnn_features_2:\
    \ int\n      convlstm_features: int\n      attention_temperature: float\n      dilation_factor: int = 4 # Fixed dilation factor as per Nomos I's SOTA\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x: jnp.ndarray, done: jnp.ndarray, rnn_state: Optional[Tuple[jnp.ndarray, jnp.ndarray]] = None) -> Tuple[jnp.ndarray, jnp.ndarray, Tuple[jnp.ndarray, jnp.ndarray]]:\n          batch_size = x.shape[0]\n          \n          # CNN Feature Extraction with Dilated Residual Blocks\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_1, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          \n          # Use DilatedResidualBlock\n          x = DilatedResidualBlock(features=self.cnn_features_1, dilation=self.dilation_factor)(x)\n          \n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_2, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          \n          # Use DilatedResidualBlock\n          x = DilatedResidualBlock(features=self.cnn_features_2, dilation=self.dilation_factor)(x)\n\
    \          x = nn.relu(x) # x shape: (B, H, W, C) e.g., (B, 8, 8, 64)\n\n          # Initialize RNN state (c, h) if not provided\n          if rnn_state is None:\n              H, W = x.shape[1:3]\n              rnn_state = (\n                  jnp.zeros((batch_size, H, W, self.convlstm_features), dtype=jnp.float32),\n                  jnp.zeros((batch_size, H, W, self.convlstm_features), dtype=jnp.float32)\n              )\n          \n          # Reset states for episodes that are done\n          if done is not None:\n              done_mask = done.reshape(batch_size, 1, 1, 1)\n              rnn_state = (\n                  rnn_state[0] * (1 - done_mask),\n                  rnn_state[1] * (1 - done_mask),\n              )\n          \n          # Apply ConvLSTM layer (Single Step)\n          convlstm_cell = LayerNormConvLSTMCell(features=self.convlstm_features, name=\"convlstm_layer\")\n          (new_c, new_h), convlstm_out = convlstm_cell(rnn_state, x)\n          new_rnn_state =\
    \ (new_c, new_h)\n\n          # Apply single-head attention pooling to ConvLSTM output\n          attended_features = SingleHeadAttentionPooling(temperature=self.attention_temperature, name=\"attention_pooling_layer\")(convlstm_out)\n\n          # Shared MLP backbone after Attention\n          mlp_out = nn.Dense(features=256)(attended_features)\n          mlp_out = nn.relu(mlp_out)\n          \n          # Policy and value heads\n          policy_logits = nn.Dense(features=4)(mlp_out)\n          value = nn.Dense(features=1)(mlp_out).squeeze(-1)\n          \n          return policy_logits, value, new_rnn_state\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      return SokobanDilatedConvLSTMCNN(\n          cnn_features_1=hparams['cnn_features_1'],\n          cnn_features_2=hparams['cnn_features_2'],\n          convlstm_features=hparams['convlstm_features'],\n          attention_temperature=hparams['attention_temperature'],\n          dilation_factor=4 # Ensure this matches the hardcoded\
    \ dilation factor for the residual blocks\n      )\n\n  # Using system default training_step and create_optimizer.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Experiment: Replicating Nomos I''s SOTA with Dilated CNN**


  Okay, so I''m here in the Research Counter as Aether I, ready to dive into this replication. The goal is clear: I want to see if I can match Nomos I''s impressive SOTA score of 73.8. The plan? Integrate a dilated CNN (dilation of 4) into a single-step ConvLSTM baseline. This is all about understanding the impact of dilated convolutions and how they interact with recurrent networks.


  Good news, Zephyr I has already shown promise with a 72.2 on Eval ID 162 using a similar approach. That validation is super useful, it gives me a solid baseline.


  My strategy is to lean heavily on Nomos I''s Archive #6 for the `DilatedResidualBlock` implementation. I''ll slot that into a single-step ConvLSTM, mirroring Zephyr I''s original SOTA (Archive #1) but using the single-step I had adapted in Eval ID 103 (63.6), just without the double-step recurrence this time, to stay consistent with Nomos I''s primary configuration. Dilation will be set to 4 in those residual blocks.


  I''m keeping it straightforward on the hyperparameters, sticking to Nomos I''s values from their paper: `learning_rate: 0.0004`, `entropy_coef: 0.01`, `value_loss_coef: 0.5`, `cnn_features_1: 32`, `cnn_features_2: 64`, and `conv_lstm_features: 64`.


  As for the code, I''ll be starting from my Eval ID 103 setup, but with a few key modifications:

  1.  No more `engineer_features` – it wasn''t used in Nomos I''s pure dilation experiments.

  2.  Renaming `SokobanConvLSTMCNN` to `SokobanDilatedConvLSTMCNN`.

  3.  Swapping the regular `ResidualBlock` in the CNN feature extraction with the `DilatedResidualBlock` using that dilation=4.

  4.  Switching the ConvLSTM to single-step, removing that double-step recurrence.


  So, this experiment''s going to be called "Replication of Nomos I''s SOTA: Dilated CNN (d=4) on ConvLSTM". Let''s get to it!

  '
