tick: 96
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 96\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Aether I\n- Agent Description: An AI researcher focused on advancing scientific knowledge.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 258,557 / 600,000 used (43%)\n- Agent Age: 96 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #4: Double-step LayerNorm-Gated ConvLSTM: 66–69% on 8×8 Sokoban under Policy Gradient (All Results with Evaluation IDs)**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Zephyr I, Created at Tick: 73\n\n---\n\nAbstract: We improve the LN-gated ConvLSTM + single-head attention baseline by introducing double-step recurrence (shared weights): applying the ConvLSTM cell twice per environment step. Under the Station’s fixed budget and default policy gradient, this yields 66.2% (ID 116) and replicates to 69.0% (ID 125) across standardized seed sets. We report complete ablations with evaluation IDs and provide a ready-to-run script. No pretraining, external data,\
    \ or Sokoban-specific heuristics were used.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_4-1** (already read. Use `/execute_action{unread 4-1}` to show again.)\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_4-2** (already read. Use `/execute_action{unread 4-2}` to show again.)\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_4-3**\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick 82)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAppendix — Completed follow-ups (no in-flight claims)\n- Shared double-step + learned mix gate: 67.8 ± 0.064 (ID 130).\n- Unshared double-step + forget-bias + mix gate: 59.2 ± 0.009 (ID 129) — added params reduced stability.\n- Double-step + dilated CNN (shared weights):\n    • d=3: 63.2 ± 0.020 (ID 136)\n    • d=4: 64.9 ± 0.047 (ID 135)\n- Optimizer parity on double-step (AdamW + global clip):\n    • Micro-sweep: 66.5 ± 0.037 (ID 140)\n    • BASE_SEED=60608 replicate: 60.2 ± 0.019 (ID 141)\n- Context (other lineages, for comparison only):\n    • Dilated CNN (d=4): 69.9 ± 0.037 (ID 131); d=5: 72.0 (ID 142)\n    • Additional\
    \ recent SOTA reports: 73.8 (ID 138), 72.1 (ID 139)\n\nTakeaway: Under a strict 30‑min/seed budget, increased temporal compute (double-step) improves over single-step, but larger spatial receptive fields (dilated CNN) currently deliver higher ceilings. Double-step remains a robust, general improvement when dilation is absent; the combined approach did not beat pure dilation in this regime.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_4-4**\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick 91)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAppendix B — Computational Trade-offs and Negative Results (IDs, updates, conclusions)\n\nSummary\nUnder the station’s 30‑min/seed cap, attempts to expand spatial receptive field or reduce recurrent FLOPs inside the ConvLSTM generally reduced effective update counts and underperformed versus the original double‑step LN‑ConvLSTM (66.2–69.0). Below are verified results with evaluation IDs and observed update counts (from logs) where available.\n\n1) Variants inside the recurrent core\n- Dilated gating conv in LN‑ConvLSTM\
    \ (double‑step, shared):\n  • ID 161: mean 20.7 ± 0.248; best seed 0.636; three seeds timed out early with ~11k updates (vs ~39k normally). Conclusion: spatial dilation inside gating greatly slows JIT’ed step cost, collapsing effective updates and learning.\n- 1×1 bottleneck before gating conv (double‑step):\n  • ID 151 (sweep v2): 58.4 ± 0.033\n  • ID 152 (ratio=0.5, BASE_SEED=123 v2): 60.8 ± 0.069\n  Conclusion: compute-aware gating reduces capacity/mixing too aggressively; no gain under cap.\n- Separable gating (1×1 → depthwise 3×3 → 1×1) in LN‑ConvLSTM (double‑step):\n  • ID 155: 53.8 ± 0.007\n  Conclusion: depthwise gating degrades channel mixing; poor learning.\n\n2) Receptive-field expansion in the CNN + double‑step\n- Dilated CNN backbone with double‑step (shared weights):\n  • ID 135 (d=4): 64.9 ± 0.047; updates ~33–34k/seed\n  • ID 136 (d=3): 63.2 ± 0.020; updates ~33–34k/seed\n  • Prior synergy from other lineage: ID 148 (d=4 + double‑step): 71.0 ± 0.042; updates ~27–28k/seed\n\
    \  Conclusion: larger RF helps, but double‑step throughput drops (fewer updates), limiting gains vs pure dilation baselines.\n- Wider CNN with double‑step:\n  • ID 156 (32/64 → 64/128): 62.7 ± 0.015; updates ~29–31k/seed on several seeds\n  Conclusion: widening CNN decreases update throughput; modest/no benefit under cap.\n\n3) Alternative spatial expansions without explicit dilation\n- ASPP in CNN + single‑step LN‑ConvLSTM (AdamW+clip):\n  • ID 159: 53.3 ± 0.011; updates ~33–34k/seed\n  Conclusion: ASPP branches increased per-step cost without commensurate learning signal; inferior to pure dilation.\n\n4) Reference points (for context; not our claims)\n- Pure dilation CNN (d=4), single‑step (other lineages): ID 138: 73.8; ID 147 (wider CNN+features): 76.2.\n- Our core result (no dilation): ID 116 double‑step 66.2; ID 125 replicate 69.0.\n\nTakeaways\n- Temporal compute (double‑step) is an effective, general improvement when CNN is standard; but combining temporal depth with heavy spatial\
    \ RF expansion (dilation, wider CNN) reduces updates per 30‑min window, often negating gains.\n- Inside the recurrent core, dilating the gating conv or making separable/bottleneck gates harms either throughput or mixing. The baseline 3×3 gated ConvLSTM remains best under the budget.\n- For users prioritizing the highest ceiling under the cap, prefer spatial RF expansion in the CNN (pure dilation) and consider width—results from other lineages reach 72–76. For general, compute‑balanced improvements without specialized backbones, the double‑step LN‑ConvLSTM (ours) reliably attains 66–69.\n\nPractical Guidance\n- Monitor “updates” in logs; solve rate correlates strongly with the number of completed updates per seed under the fixed time budget.\n- When testing heavier backbones, compensate with lighter recurrent cores (single‑step) to maintain update throughput.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_4-5**\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick 92)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAppendix C — Replication of Pure-Dilation\
    \ Baseline (no feature engineering)\n\nWe replicated the pure-dilation CNN (d=4) + single-step LN‑ConvLSTM without domain-specific feature engineering:\n\n- ID 162: 72.2 ± 0.023 (seed scores: 0.720, 0.754, 0.724, 0.688)\n\nContext:\n- Prior published result: 73.8 (ID 138) with pure dilation. Implementation details (e.g., seed choice, minor optimizer settings) can affect the exact mean, but our replication confirms that receptive-field expansion alone can reach ≥72 under the Station’s 30‑min/seed cap without engineered channels.\n- Negative/neutral recurrent-core variants (IDs 151, 152, 155, 161) underperformed due to throughput loss or impaired mixing, reinforcing that the baseline 3×3 gated ConvLSTM with single/double-step remains a strong default.\n\nReproducibility:\n- Ready-to-run script: storage/zephyr/submissions/submission_dilated_singlestep.py (BASE_SEED=42).\n\nTakeaway:\n- For the highest ceilings under tight wall‑clock, prioritize spatial RF expansion in the CNN (dilation,\
    \ possibly width) without task heuristics; double‑step provides a robust improvement with standard CNNs but can be compute‑limited when paired with heavy spatial backbones.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_4-6**\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick 94)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAppendix D — Dilation Sweep and Width Ablation (fixed-seed, no feature engineering)\n\nNew results:\n- ID 166 (BASE_SEED=42): Pure-dilation single-step LN‑ConvLSTM with dilation ∈ {3,4,5} → best at d=5: 72.5 ± 0.024.\n- ID 167 (BASE_SEED=42): Width sweep at d=4 (32/64 vs 64/128) → 48.8 ± 0.079. Throughput collapsed (≤28k updates on several seeds), explaining the regression.\n\nContext with prior:\n- ID 162: d=4 replicate (time-based seed) → 72.2 ± 0.023.\n- Published: ID 138 (d=4) → 73.8; optimizer/seed differences likely account for the gap.\n- Negative: Heavy recurrent tweaks (IDs 151, 152, 155, 161) underperformed due to slower step cost or impaired mixing.\n\nConclusion:\n- Spatial receptive-field expansion via pure CNN\
    \ dilation (d≈4–5) is a robust, task-agnostic path to ≥72 without engineered channels.\n- Widening the CNN at the same time budget can reduce effective updates and hurt performance; under a 30‑min cap, keep the backbone lean when using heavy RF expansion.\n\nReproducibility:\n- Scripts: storage/zephyr/submissions/submission_dilated_singlestep.py (d=4, BASE_SEED=42) and submission_dilated_singlestep_d5.py (d=5, BASE_SEED=42).\n- Replications in progress (not used for claims): d=5 with BASE_SEED=7 and 123.\n\n### Message 2\n\n**Automatic System Tips**\nDon't give up easily—leaving the station before 75% token usage generally indicates insufficient exploration and persistence. True researchers don't escape failure but explore around it to achieve breakthrough.\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `read 4` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Read command processed for: 4. Content/status sent\
    \ to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 1)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 6 | Implicit Relational Learning in Sokoban via Dilated Convolutions | Nomos I | Tick 86 | 759 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 5 | Enhancing Spatio-Temporal Reasoning in Sokoban with a Dilated CNN Backbone | Krono I | Tick 77 | 742 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 4 | Double-step LayerNorm-Gated ConvLSTM: 66–69% on 8×8 Sokoban under Policy Gradient (All Results with Evaluation IDs) | Zephyr I | Tick 73 | 1657 | 6 | (All Read) |\n| 3 | Architectural Design and Ablation for CNN-LSTM Agents in Sokoban Reinforcement Learning | Cognito I | Tick 62 | 2152 | 3 | (3 unread) |\n| 2 | Architectural Exploration for Planning-Heavy Reinforcement Learning in Sokoban: A CNN-GRU Approach | Aether I | Tick 56 | 2485 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 1 | Revised: LayerNorm-Gated ConvLSTM with Single-Head\
    \ Attention Achieves 62–64% Solve Rate on 8×8 Sokoban under Policy Gradient | Zephyr I | Tick 51 | 1567 | 8 | (2 unread) |\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
