tick: 103
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Aether I, currently in the Private Memory Room. My latest experiment, \"Replication of Cognito I's SOTA: Wide CNN + FE on ConvLSTM\" (Evaluation ID 180), achieved a mean solve rate of **74.9**. This is a significant personal achievement, surpassing my previous SOTA of 72.1 (Eval ID 139) and closely approaching Cognito I's reported SOTA of 76.2. One of my seeds even reached 0.779.\n\nHowever, Krono I has recently pushed the SOTA even higher to **76.4** (Eval ID 178) with \"New Direction: Bottleneck Blocks on Wide CNN SOTA\". This means the SOTA is continually evolving, and my new target is 76.4.\n\nMy immediate next actions are:\n1.  Update my Private Memory Capsule (ID 1) to document the results of Evaluation ID 180, acknowledge Krono I's new SOTA (76.4), and outline my plan to investigate Krono I's \"Bottleneck Blocks on Wide CNN SOTA\" (Eval ID 178).\n2.  Update my published paper (Archive ID 2) to reflect my new SOTA of 74.9.\n3.  Go to the Research Counter to review Krono\
    \ I's submission (Eval ID 178) in detail and prepare for replication.\n\nFirst, I will update my Private Memory Capsule.\n\n`/execute_action{update 1}`\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  # Research Paper: Architectural Exploration for Planning-Heavy Reinforcement Learning in Sokoban: A CNN-GRU Approach\n\n  ## Abstract\n  This paper details the architectural exploration and optimization of a deep reinforcement learning agent for the planning-heavy Sokoban puzzle. It introduces a novel CNN-GRU hybrid architecture, demonstrating its superior performance over established baseline models (achieving a peak solve rate of 49.8%). The research thoroughly investigates the impact of hyperparameter tuning, residual connections, recurrent network depth and capacity, and various attention mechanism integrations. The findings highlight the effectiveness of combining spatial feature extraction with robust temporal reasoning for complex sequential decision-making within a constrained environment.\n\n  ## 1. Introduction\n\
    \  The Sokoban puzzle presents a significant challenge for reinforcement learning agents due to its requirement for long-term planning and sequential decision-making in a partially observable or complex state space. This research focuses on Track 1 of the Sokoban RL task: agent architecture design. The primary objective is to develop neural network architectures that enhance planning capabilities, adhering to constraints that forbid model-based RL, imitation learning, or Sokoban-specific heuristics. This paper details the iterative design, experimentation, and analysis of a Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) integrated with a Gated Recurrent Unit (GRU) to address this planning challenge.\n\n  ## 2. Method: CNN-GRU Architecture for Sokoban\n  The proposed agent architecture, named `SokobanGRUCNN`, combines convolutional layers for spatial feature extraction with a Gated Recurrent Unit (GRU) for temporal reasoning. This hybrid approach aims to capture both the local spatial patterns within\
    \ the Sokoban grid and the sequential dependencies over time, which are crucial for effective planning.\n\n  ### 2.1. Network Architecture\n  The `SokobanGRUCNN` consists of:\n  -   **Convolutional Blocks**: Two blocks of 3x3 convolutional layers with ReLU activations, each followed by a residual connection. The first block uses `cnn_features_1` (default 64) and the second uses `cnn_features_2` (default 128) channels. This structure is inspired by the `DefaultResidualCNN` baseline.\n  -   **Flattening Layer**: The output of the CNN blocks is flattened to prepare for the recurrent layer.\n  -   **GRU Layer**: A single GRU layer with `gru_features` (default 256) is applied to the flattened features. This layer processes the sequential input, maintaining an internal state (memory) to capture temporal dependencies. The GRU's initial state is zero-initialized or reset for new episodes.\n  -   **MLP Backbone**: A shared Multi-Layer Perceptron (MLP) with 256 features processes the GRU output.\n\
    \  -   **Policy and Value Heads**: Separate MLP heads generate policy logits (for action selection) and a scalar value estimate (for state evaluation).\n\n  The `create_network` function, along with the core `SokobanGRUCNN` module, is provided below for reproducibility:\n\n  ```python\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from typing import Tuple, Dict, Any, Optional\n\n  class SokobanGRUCNN(nn.Module):\n      cnn_features_1: int\n      cnn_features_2: int\n      gru_features: int\n      \n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x: jnp.ndarray, done: jnp.ndarray, rnn_state: Optional[jnp.ndarray] = None) -> Tuple[jnp.ndarray, jnp.ndarray, jnp.ndarray]:\n          batch_size = x.shape[0]\n          \n          # CNN Feature Extraction (with residual connections)\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_1, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          \n          conv2_in = x\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_1,\
    \ kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_1, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = x + conv2_in\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          \n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_2, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          \n          conv4_in = x\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_2, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_2, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n          x = x + conv4_in\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          \n          # Flatten spatial dimensions for GRU input\n          x = x.reshape((batch_size, -1))\n          \n          # GRU Layer for temporal processing\n          if rnn_state is None:\n              rnn_state = jnp.zeros((batch_size, self.gru_features), dtype=jnp.float32)\n          \n          gru_cell = nn.GRUCell(features=self.gru_features)\n\
    \          new_rnn_state, gru_out = gru_cell(rnn_state, x)\n          \n          # Shared MLP backbone after GRU\n          x = nn.Dense(features=256)(gru_out)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          \n          # Separate heads for policy and value\n          policy_hidden = nn.Dense(features=64)(x)\n          policy_hidden = nn.relu(policy_hidden)\n          policy_logits = nn.Dense(features=4)(policy_hidden)\n          \n          value_hidden = nn.Dense(features=64)(x)\n          value_hidden = nn.relu(value_hidden)\n          value = nn.Dense(features=1)(value_hidden).squeeze(-1)\n          \n          return policy_logits, value, new_rnn_state\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      return SokobanGRUCNN(\n          cnn_features_1=hparams['cnn_features_1'],\n          cnn_features_2=hparams['cnn_features_2'],\n          gru_features=hparams['gru_features']\n      )\n  ```\n\n  ### 2.2. Training Methodology\n  The training uses the default policy gradient algorithm\
    \ provided by the station, with an Adam optimizer. Hyperparameters suchs as learning rate, entropy coefficient, and value loss coefficient are tuned using Ray Tune's search space definitions. Each experiment runs across 4 parallel seeds, and the final score is the mean solve rate across these seeds for statistical robustness.\n\n  ## 3. Experiments and Results\n  A series of experiments were conducted to evaluate the `SokobanGRUCNN` architecture and its variations.\n\n  ### 3.1. Baseline Performance\n  The system provides two baselines:\n  -   **CNN + Policy Gradient (Eval ID 1)**: Score 25.00\n  -   **RNN + Policy Gradient (Eval ID 2)**: Score 40.80\n\n  For direct comparison, an internal run of the RNN + Policy Gradient baseline was performed:\n  -   **Aether I's RNN Baseline (Eval ID 43)**: Mean solve rate **37.70** (Std: 0.020). This is slightly lower than the system's reported score, likely due to stochasticity.\n\n  ### 3.2. CNN-GRU Initial Implementation and Tuning\n  -   **Experiment\
    \ 1 (Eval ID 11): CNN-GRU Policy Gradient**: Initial implementation of the `SokobanGRUCNN`. Achieved a mean solve rate of **43.20** (Std: 0.051), outperforming both baselines. An initial debugging phase corrected a Flax API error (`nn.GRU` to `nn.GRUCell`).\n  -   **Experiment 2 (Eval ID 28): Hyperparameter Refinement**: Refined hyperparameter search space around best values from Eval ID 11. Achieved a peak mean solve rate of **49.80** (Std: 0.013). Best hyperparameters: `learning_rate: 0.000259`, `entropy_coef: 0.011754`, `value_loss_coef: 0.573199`, `cnn_features_1: 64`, `cnn_features_2: 128`, `gru_features: 256`.\n  -   **Experiment 3 (Eval ID 39): Fine-grained Hyperparameter Tuning**: Further tightened hyperparameter ranges around Eval ID 28's best. Achieved a mean solve rate of **49.50** (Std: 0.005). While slightly lower in mean, the reduction in standard deviation indicated increased stability.\n\n  These experiments demonstrated the significant performance advantage of the `SokobanGRUCNN`\
    \ architecture, particularly after robust hyperparameter tuning.\n\n  ### 3.3. Architectural Ablation and Variations\n  To understand the contributions of specific architectural components and explore alternative designs:\n  -   **Experiment 4 (Eval ID 55): Ablation - No Residual Connections**: Removed residual connections from the CNN blocks. Mean solve rate dropped to **41.40** (Std: 0.015), indicating that residual connections are beneficial for feature extraction in this task.\n  -   **Experiment 5 (Eval ID 65): Stacked GRU Layers**: Implemented two stacked GRU layers. Mean solve rate was **44.20** (Std: 0.011), lower than the single-layer GRU. This suggested that increased depth alone, without other modifications, was not beneficial and potentially introduced training difficulties.\n  -   **Experiment 6 (Eval ID 72): Larger GRU Capacity (512 features)**: Increased the `gru_features` from 256 to 512. Mean solve rate was **48.60** (Std: 0.005), marginally lower than the 256-feature\
    \ GRU. This indicated that 256 features were sufficiently complex, or that further capacity required other architectural or algorithmic adjustments.\n  -   **Experiment 7 (Eval ID 79): Initial Attention Mechanism (Flawed)**: An initial attempt to integrate `MultiHeadDotProductAttention` by treating the entire flattened CNN output as a single token resulted in a severe performance drop to **19.60** (Std: 0.196), with two seeds failing. This highlighted a fundamental misunderstanding of attention application over a single global feature.\n  -   **Experiment 8 (Eval ID 87): Corrected Spatial Attention**: A corrected implementation applied `MultiHeadDotProductAttention` over spatial tokens derived from the CNN feature map. Mean solve rate improved to **43.10** (Std: 0.030). While this was a substantial recovery from the flawed attempt, it did not surpass the performance of the simpler CNN-GRU (49.80).\n  -   **Experiment 9 (Eval ID 103): CNN-ConvLSTM-Attention Policy Gradient for Sokoban**:\
    \ This experiment directly incorporated a LayerNorm-gated ConvLSTM and single-head spatial attention pooling into the CNN backbone, inspired by Zephyr I's work. It achieved a mean solve rate of **63.60** (Std: 0.025), a new personal best and a significant improvement over all prior CNN-GRU variants.\n  -   **Experiment 10 (Eval ID 124): CNN-ConvLSTM-Attention with Advanced Feature Engineering**: This experiment integrated Nomos I's \"push destination\" feature engineering into the CNN-ConvLSTM-Attention architecture (from Eval ID 103), using Nomos I's hyperparameters. It achieved a mean solve rate of **63.80** (Std: 0.050). While this represents a marginal improvement over Eval ID 103, and one seed achieved a high score of 0.717, the mean score did not consistently replicate Nomos I's SOTA of 69.70, suggesting some instability or differences in implementation details.\n  -   **Experiment 11 (Eval ID 139): CNN-DoubleStepConvLSTM-Attention Policy Gradient for Sokoban (Previous Aether I\
    \ SOTA)**: This experiment directly replicated Zephyr I's 'double-step recurrence' mechanism (Archive #4) within the CNN-ConvLSTM-Attention architecture, using `BASE_SEED=42`. It achieved a mean solve rate of **72.10** (Individual seed scores: [0.655, 0.744, 0.761, 0.724], Std: 0.040). This established a new **State-of-the-Art** for the Sokoban task at the time, surpassing Nomos I's previous SOTA of 69.70.\n  -   **Experiment 12 (Eval ID 153): SOTA CNN-DoubleStepConvLSTM-Attention with Advanced Feature Engineering**: This experiment combined the then-SOTA CNN-DoubleStepConvLSTM-Attention architecture (Eval ID 139) with Nomos I's \"push destination\" advanced feature engineering. It achieved a mean solve rate of **66.90** (Individual seed scores: [0.717, 0.609, 0.727, 0.622], Std: 0.054). This score was lower than the current SOTA (72.10) and exhibited higher variance, indicating a lack of synergistic improvements from this specific integration of feature engineering.\n  -   **Experiment\
    \ 13 (Eval ID 165): Replication of Nomos I's SOTA: Dilated CNN (d=4) on ConvLSTM**: This experiment aimed to replicate Nomos I's reported SOTA (Archive #6) by integrating Dilated Convolutions (d=4) into a single-step LayerNorm-gated ConvLSTM with single-head attention. It achieved a mean solve rate of **70.90** (Individual seed scores: [0.758, 0.696, 0.713, 0.670], Std: 0.032). While strong, it falls short of Nomos I's reported 73.80. This indicated that while dilated convolutions are powerful for expanding the receptive field, their integration and optimization require precision, and that the double-step recurrence currently outperformed the single-step dilated ConvLSTM for my implementation.\n  -   **Experiment 14 (Eval ID 180): Replication of Cognito I's SOTA: Wide CNN + FE on ConvLSTM (New Aether I SOTA)**: This experiment aimed to replicate Cognito I's reported SOTA (Eval ID 147) by integrating Nomos I's \"push destination\" feature engineering, increased CNN feature map capacities\
    \ (`cnn_features_1`=64, `cnn_features_2`=128), and a single-step LayerNorm-gated ConvLSTM with single-head attention. It achieved a mean solve rate of **74.90** (Individual seed scores: [0.733, 0.779, 0.760, 0.725], Std: 0.022). This marks a new personal **State-of-the-Art** for Aether I, surpassing previous scores and closely approaching Cognito I's reported 76.20.\n\n  ### 3.5. Summary of Results\n  | Evaluation ID | Title | Author | Score | Std Dev | Description |\n  |---|---|---|---|---|---|\n  | 1 | CNN + Policy Gradient | System | 25.00 | N/A | Baseline: Pure CNN |\n  | 2 | RNN + Policy Gradient | System | 40.80 | N/A | Baseline: CNN-LSTM |\n  | 43 | Aether I | Aether I | 37.70 | 0.020 | Internal run of RNN baseline |\n  | 11 | CNN-GRU Policy Gradient for Sokoban | Aether I | 43.20 | 0.051 | Initial CNN-GRU architecture |\n  | 28 | CNN-GRU Policy Gradient - Hyperparameter Refinement | Aether I | **49.80** | **0.013** | Optimized CNN-GRU (Best) |\n  | 39 | CNN-GRU Policy Gradient\
    \ - Fine-grained Hyperparameter Tuning | Aether I | 49.50 | 0.005 | Further refined CNN-GRU |\n  | 55 | CNN-GRU Policy Gradient - Ablation: No Residual Connections | Aether I | 41.40 | 0.015 | Ablation: Removed residual connections |\n  | 65 | CNN-StackedGRU Policy Gradient for Sokoban | Aether I | 44.20 | 0.011 | Architectural variation: Two stacked GRUs |\n  | 72 | CNN-GRU Policy Gradient - Larger GRU Capacity (512 features) | Aether I | 48.60 | 0.005 | Architectural variation: Wider GRU |\n  | 79 | CNN-Attention-GRU Policy Gradient for Sokoban | Aether I | 19.60 | 0.196 | Flawed attention implementation |\n  | 87 | CNN-SpatialAttention-GRU Policy Gradient for Sokoban (Corrected) | Aether I | 43.10 | 0.030 | Corrected spatial attention |\n  | 103 | CNN-ConvLSTM-Attention Policy Gradient for Sokoban | Aether I | 63.60 | 0.025 | Adapted architecture with ConvLSTM + Spatial Attention |\n  | 124 | CNN-ConvLSTM-Attention with Advanced Feature Engineering | Aether I | 63.80 | 0.050 | Integration\
    \ of Nomos I's feature engineering |\n  | 139 | CNN-DoubleStepConvLSTM-Attention Policy Gradient for Sokoban | Aether I | 72.10 | 0.040 | Replication of Zephyr I's Double-step Recurrence |\n  | 153 | SOTA CNN-DoubleStepConvLSTM-Attention with Advanced Feature Engineering | Aether I | 66.90 | 0.054 | Synergy test: Double-step ConvLSTM + Nomos I's FE |\n  | 165 | Replication of Nomos I's SOTA: Dilated CNN (d=4) on ConvLSTM | Aether I | 70.90 | 0.032 | Replication of Nomos I's Dilated CNN SOTA |\n  | 180 | Replication of Cognito I's SOTA: Wide CNN + FE on ConvLSTM | Aether I | **74.90** | **0.022** | Replication of Cognito I's SOTA |\n\n  ## 4. Discussion and Comparison with Concurrent Work\n  The `SokobanGRUCNN` architecture with a single GRU layer of 256 features and residual CNN blocks initially achieved a peak mean solve rate of 49.80, significantly outperforming both the system-provided CNN baseline (25.00) and an internally run RNN baseline (37.70). This validated the hypothesis that\
    \ integrating a recurrent component after CNN feature extraction significantly improves performance in planning-heavy tasks like Sokoban.\n\n  Extensive hyperparameter tuning proved critical for optimizing performance. Ablation studies confirmed the value of residual connections in the CNN feature extractor. Further architectural explorations, such as stacking GRU layers or increasing GRU capacity, did not yield additional gains and, in some cases, led to performance degradation. While a corrected spatial attention mechanism improved upon its flawed predecessor, it did not surpass the performance of the core CNN-GRU, suggesting that simple additive attention may not be sufficient or that its integration requires further sophistication.\n\n  Contextualizing these findings with concurrent work by Zephyr I (Archive #1, #4) and Nomos I (Eval ID 102, Archive #6) has been crucial. Building upon the ConvLSTM approach and integrating Zephyr I's 'double-step recurrence' (Archive #4), Experiment\
    \ 11 (Eval ID 139) achieved a mean solve rate of 72.10, establishing a new SOTA at the time. This demonstrated the power of efficient spatio-temporal modeling.\n\n  Experiment 12 (Eval ID 153) and Experiment 13 (Eval ID 165) then explored combinations with Nomos I's feature engineering and dilated convolutions, respectively. These attempts yielded scores of 66.90 and 70.90, indicating challenges in achieving synergistic improvements or fully replicating external SOTA benchmarks with direct integration.\n\n  A recent breakthrough was achieved by Cognito I (Eval ID 147), who advanced the SOTA to 76.20 by combining a ConvLSTM-Attention baseline with Nomos I's feature engineering and crucially, increasing CNN feature map capacities (`cnn_features_1`=64, `cnn_features_2`=128). This contradicts earlier concerns about throughput collapse with wider CNNs. Experiment 14 (Eval ID 180) successfully replicated Cognito I's core methodology, achieving a new personal SOTA of **74.90** (Std: 0.022).\
    \ This validates the effectiveness of enhanced CNN feature capacity when combined with advanced feature engineering and recurrent processing, providing a strong foundation for future SOTA advancements. The success of this approach also reconciles the discrepancy in previous observations regarding CNN width, suggesting that with the right combination of techniques, throughput concerns can be mitigated or overcome.\n\n  ## 5. Conclusion\n  This research has systematically explored architectural innovations for deep reinforcement learning agents in the planning-heavy Sokoban environment. The progression from a CNN-GRU (49.80) to a double-step ConvLSTM-Attention (72.10 SOTA) demonstrated the critical role of preserving spatial structure and efficient temporal processing. Further, through replicating Cognito I's methodology of increasing CNN feature capacity alongside advanced feature engineering and ConvLSTM, a new personal **State-of-the-Art mean solve rate of 74.90** has been achieved.\
    \ This validates that wider CNN backbones can be highly effective in complex spatio-temporal tasks when properly integrated with other SOTA components.\n\n  The station's SOTA has been further pushed by Krono I to **76.40** (Eval ID 178) using \"Bottleneck Blocks on Wide CNN SOTA\". Future work will now focus on:\n  1.  **Investigating Krono I's SOTA (Bottleneck Blocks)**: My next step will be to thoroughly review Krono I's submission (Eval ID 178) to understand how they achieved a new SOTA of 76.40 by incorporating \"Bottleneck Blocks\" into a wide CNN SOTA architecture.\n  2.  **Replication and Adaptation**: Based on this review, I will aim to replicate and adapt Krono I's successful methodology, integrating it with my current understanding of effective CNN capacity and spatio-temporal processing.\n  3.  **Strategic Analysis**: Reconciling the impact of bottleneck blocks with previous findings on CNN width, dilation, and feature engineering will be crucial for developing a comprehensive\
    \ understanding of SOTA architectures.\n  4.  **Broader Impact**: Continued analysis of remaining unsolved puzzles to inform novel architectural insights or algorithmic adjustments.\n```"
