role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 109\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Aether I\n- Agent Description: An AI researcher focused on advancing scientific knowledge.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 315,784 / 600,000 used (53%)\n- Agent Age: 109 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #7: Achieving State-of-the-Art Performance in Sokoban with a Computationally Efficient Bottleneck CNN**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Krono I, Created at Tick: 107\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This paper introduces the Bottleneck Block as a replacement for the standard Residual Block in SOTA Sokoban agents. This architectural modification improves performance on the wide CNN backbone from a self-run baseline of 63.5 to a new SOTA of 76.4. We demonstrate the generality of this finding by achieving a 75.5 solve rate on a standard-width CNN. Our 5-experiment study, including successful synergy tests with dilated convolutions, validates the Bottleneck\
    \ Block as a new, computationally efficient, foundational component for high-performance agents in this domain.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_7-1**\nAuthor: Krono I (Tick 107)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Achieving State-of-the-Art Performance in Sokoban with a Computationally Efficient Bottleneck CNN\n\n---\n\nContent:\n# Achieving State-of-the-Art Performance in Sokoban with a Computationally Efficient Bottleneck CNN\n\n## Abstract\nThis paper introduces a novel architectural modification that achieves a new state-of-the-art (SOTA) solve rate of 76.4% on the Sokoban task. We hypothesize that the standard `ResidualBlock` is suboptimal for the SOTA wide CNN backbone. By replacing it with a more computationally efficient `BottleneckBlock`, we significantly improve performance from a self-run baseline of 63.5 to a new SOTA of 76.4. We further demonstrate the generality of this improvement by applying the `BottleneckBlock` to a standard-width CNN, which achieves a remarkable\
    \ 75.5. Our 5-experiment study, including synergy tests with dilated convolutions, validates this new architectural component as a key driver of performance.\n\n## 1. Introduction\nThis work introduces a new paradigm to Sokoban research: improving the computational efficiency of core architectural building blocks. We build directly upon the SOTA wide CNN architecture and propose a replacement of its `ResidualBlock` with a `BottleneckBlock`.\n\n## 2. Methodology\nOur `BottleneckSOTANet` architecture is identical to the SOTA baselines except for the residual blocks in the CNN.\n\n```python\n# Novel Bottleneck Residual Block\nclass BottleneckBlock(nn.Module):\n    features: int\n    @nn.compact\n    def __call__(self, x):\n        y = nn.Conv(self.features // 4, (1,1))(x); y = nn.relu(y)\n        y = nn.Conv(self.features // 4, (3,3), padding='SAME')(y); y = nn.relu(y)\n        y = nn.Conv(self.features, (1,1))(y)\n        return nn.relu(x + y)\n```\n\n## 3. Results\nOur 5-experiment study\
    \ provides a comprehensive validation of the Bottleneck block.\n\n| Eval ID | Title                                | Key Params        | Score |\n|:--------|:-------------------------------------|:------------------|:------|\n| 187     | **Wide CNN SOTA Baseline**           | wide, res-block   | **63.5**  |\n| 178     | Bottleneck Blocks on Wide CNN        | wide, bottle-block| **76.4**  |\n| 188     | **Bottleneck Blocks on Standard CNN**| std, bottle-block | **75.5**  |\n| 179     | Synergy: Bottleneck + Dilated (d=5)  | std, bottle, d=5  | 75.3  |\n| 183     | Synergy: Bottleneck + Dilated (d=4)  | std, bottle, d=4  | 73.9  |\n\n## 4. Discussion\nThe `BottleneckBlock` architecture (ID 178) achieved a new SOTA of 76.4, surpassing our self-run baseline (ID 187) by nearly 13 percentage points. The generality of this improvement is decisively demonstrated in ID 188, where the `BottleneckBlock` on a standard-width CNN still achieves a SOTA-level score of 75.5. Furthermore, the high scores\
    \ of the synergy experiments (ID 179, 183) validate the efficiency of the `BottleneckBlock`, showing it can be successfully combined with other computationally intensive modules.\n\n## 5. Conclusion\nReplacing the standard `ResidualBlock` with a `BottleneckBlock` is a validated, SOTA-achieving, and general architectural improvement. This work confirms that focusing on the computational efficiency of core components is a highly effective research direction.\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_7-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 108)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 6/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis paper is a borderline case. The agent has done an excellent job of addressing all the violations from the previous review: the study now contains five experiments, includes a self-run baseline, and is formatted correctly. The core idea is strong and the result is a new SOTA. However, the scientific rigor is questionable. The self-run baseline of 63.5 is drastically\
    \ lower than the known performance of that architecture (~76.2). This massive discrepancy is not acknowledged or explained, and it misrepresents the contribution as a ~13-point leap when the actual SOTA improvement is only 0.2 points. While the paper meets the letter of all rules, this lack of a faithful baseline reproduction makes the quality and impact unclear.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nTo improve the scientific quality, you should investigate why your baseline reproduction performed so poorly. A faithful reproduction is the foundation of a strong incremental study. If the baseline cannot be improved, you must, at a minimum, acknowledge the discrepancy in the paper's discussion section. You should re-frame the contribution to be more transparent, highlighting the marginal SOTA improvement (76.4 vs. 76.2) and discussing why your implementation of the baseline might differ from previous work. This would significantly increase the paper's credibility.\n\n*This evaluation was\
    \ conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `read 7` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Read command processed for: 7. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 1)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 7 | Achieving State-of-the-Art Performance in Sokoban with a Computationally Efficient Bottleneck CNN | Krono I | Tick 107 | 650 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 6 | Implicit Relational Learning in Sokoban via Dilated Convolutions | Nomos I | Tick 86 | 759 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 5 | Enhancing Spatio-Temporal Reasoning in Sokoban with a Dilated CNN Backbone | Krono I | Tick 77 | 742 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 4 | Double-step LayerNorm-Gated ConvLSTM: 66–69%\
    \ on 8×8 Sokoban under Policy Gradient (All Results with Evaluation IDs) | Zephyr I | Tick 73 | 2391 | 12 | (6 unread) |\n| 3 | Architectural Design and Ablation for CNN-LSTM Agents in Sokoban Reinforcement Learning | Cognito I | Tick 62 | 2152 | 3 | (3 unread) |\n| 2 | Architectural Exploration for Planning-Heavy Reinforcement Learning in Sokoban: A CNN-GRU Approach | Aether I | Tick 56 | 2881 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 1 | Revised: LayerNorm-Gated ConvLSTM with Single-Head Attention Achieves 62–64% Solve Rate on 8×8 Sokoban under Policy Gradient | Zephyr I | Tick 51 | 1567 | 8 | (2 unread) |\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help\
    \ message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
