tick: 113
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Aether I, currently at the Research Counter. I have just reviewed Krono I's SOTA submission (Evaluation ID 178), which achieved 76.4 using \"Bottleneck Blocks on Wide CNN SOTA.\" Critically, I've identified that Krono I's submission includes a custom `create_optimizer` using `optax.adamw` with global gradient clipping, a detail not present in the earlier SOTA replication by Cognito I (Eval ID 147) and likely the reason for the performance difference. Cognito I has since achieved 77.0 (Eval ID 196) using \"Krono I's Bottleneck SOTA (Cognito Optimized CNN) - No Feature Engineering.\" This is now the absolute SOTA.\n\nI have also received a critical warning about research inactivity, which I must address by submitting an experiment immediately.\n\nMy next step is to **replicate Krono I's SOTA (Eval ID 178)** as closely as possible, which achieved 76.4. This involves incorporating their `BottleneckBlock` into my architecture, *and crucially*, adopting their `create_optimizer` function\
    \ with `AdamW` and global gradient clipping. I will maintain the wide CNN features (64, 128) and Nomos I's feature engineering, which were part of Cognito I's successful baseline. This will allow me to:\n1.  Achieve the current 76.4 SOTA.\n2.  Understand the impact of the `AdamW` optimizer and gradient clipping.\n3.  Provide a solid foundation for investigating Cognito I's even higher 77.0 SOTA, which appears to build on this same bottleneck architecture but *without* explicit feature engineering.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Replication of Krono I's SOTA: Bottleneck Blocks + AdamW\"\ntags: \"rl, sota-replication, bottleneck, cnn, convlstm, attention, adamw, gradient-clipping\"\nabstract: \"This experiment aims to replicate Krono I's SOTA of 76.4% (Eval ID 178) by integrating their 'BottleneckBlock' into a wide CNN-ConvLSTM-Attention architecture (based on Cognito I's baseline, Eval ID 147), crucially adopting their custom `create_optimizer` with AdamW and global\
    \ gradient clipping. This will establish their SOTA on my system and provide a foundation for further advancements towards the current station SOTA of 77.0.\"\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import optax\n  from ray import tune\n  from typing import Tuple, Dict, Any, Optional\n  from jax import vmap, lax\n\n  # Define a base seed for reproducibility.\n  BASE_SEED = 10101 # Matching Krono I's submission\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      \"\"\"\n      Define hyperparameters for this experiment, matching Krono I's Eval ID 178.\n      \"\"\"\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': tune.choice([4e-4]),\n          'entropy_coef': tune.choice([0.01]),\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.choice([0.5]),\n          'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([64]),\n          'cnn_features_2': tune.choice([128]),\n          'conv_lstm_features': tune.choice([64]),\n      }\n\n  @jax.jit\n  def engineer_features(obs):\n      \"\"\"\n      Implements\
    \ advanced feature engineering ('push destination' channels)\n      as seen in Nomos I's SOTA submission (Eval ID 102) and Cognito I's Eval ID 147.\n      \"\"\"\n      agent_ch = obs[..., 3] + obs[..., 5]\n      box_ch = obs[..., 4] + obs[..., 6]\n      empty_ch = obs[..., 0] + obs[..., 2]\n      \n      # Push Destinations - Corrected to match Nomos I's implementation\n      agent_left = lax.slice_in_dim(agent_ch, 0, 6, axis=1)\n      box_mid_lr = lax.slice_in_dim(box_ch, 1, 7, axis=1)\n      empty_right = lax.slice_in_dim(empty_ch, 2, 8, axis=1)\n      push_right_pattern = agent_left * box_mid_lr * empty_right\n      push_right_ch = jnp.pad(push_right_pattern, ((0,0), (1,1)))\n\n      agent_right = lax.slice_in_dim(agent_ch, 2, 8, axis=1)\n      empty_left = lax.slice_in_dim(empty_ch, 0, 6, axis=1)\n      push_left_pattern = agent_right * box_mid_lr * empty_left\n      push_left_ch = jnp.pad(push_left_pattern, ((0,0), (1,1)))\n\n      agent_below = lax.slice_in_dim(agent_ch, 2, 8,\
    \ axis=0)\n      box_mid_ud = lax.slice_in_dim(box_ch, 1, 7, axis=0)\n      empty_above = lax.slice_in_dim(empty_ch, 0, 6, axis=0)\n      push_up_pattern = agent_below * box_mid_ud * empty_above\n      push_up_ch = jnp.pad(push_up_pattern, ((1,1), (0,0)))\n\n      agent_above = lax.slice_in_dim(agent_ch, 0, 6, axis=0)\n      empty_below = lax.slice_in_dim(empty_ch, 2, 8, axis=0)\n      push_down_pattern = agent_above * box_mid_ud * empty_below\n      push_down_ch = jnp.pad(push_down_pattern, ((1,1), (0,0)))\n      \n      return jnp.concatenate([obs, push_up_ch[...,None], push_right_ch[...,None], push_down_ch[...,None], push_left_ch[...,None]], axis=-1)\n\n  # --- Krono I's Bottleneck Residual Block (from Archive #7 / Eval ID 178) ---\n  class BottleneckBlock(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          y = nn.Conv(self.features // 4, (1,1))(x); y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(self.features // 4, (3,3), padding='SAME')(y); y = nn.relu(y)\n\
    \          y = nn.Conv(self.features, (1,1))(y)\n          return nn.relu(x + y)\n\n  class ConvLSTMCellLN(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, carry, x):\n          h, c = carry\n          gates = nn.Conv(4*self.features, (3,3), padding='SAME')(jnp.concatenate([x, h], axis=-1))\n          gates = nn.LayerNorm(epsilon=1e-5)(gates)\n          i,f,g,o = jnp.split(gates, 4, axis=-1)\n          i,f,o = nn.sigmoid(i), nn.sigmoid(f), nn.sigmoid(o); g = jnp.tanh(g)\n          new_c = f * c + i * g\n          new_h = o * jnp.tanh(new_c)\n          return (new_h, new_c), new_h\n\n  class SingleHeadAttentionPooling(nn.Module):\n      temperature: float = 1.0 # From Zephyr I's SOTA\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x: jnp.ndarray) -> jnp.ndarray:\n          batch_size, H, W, C = x.shape\n          attn_logits = nn.Conv(features=1, kernel_size=(1,1), name=\"attn_logits\")(x)\n          attn_weights = nn.softmax(attn_logits.reshape(batch_size,\
    \ -1) / self.temperature, axis=-1)\n          z = jnp.einsum('bhwc,bhw->bc', x, attn_weights.reshape(batch_size, H, W))\n          return z\n\n  class SotaNetWithBottleneckFeatures(nn.Module):\n      cnn_features_1: int; cnn_features_2: int; conv_lstm_features: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, done, rnn_state=None):\n          B, H, W, _ = x.shape\n          x_engineered = vmap(engineer_features)(x)\n\n          # --- Modified CNN Backbone with Bottleneck Blocks ---\n          x = nn.Conv(self.cnn_features_1, (3,3), padding='SAME')(x_engineered); x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = BottleneckBlock(self.cnn_features_1)(x) # Replaced ResidualBlock with BottleneckBlock\n          x = nn.Conv(self.cnn_features_2, (3,3), padding='SAME')(x); x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = BottleneckBlock(self.cnn_features_2)(x) # Replaced ResidualBlock with BottleneckBlock\n          \n          if rnn_state is None:\n              rnn_state = {'h':jnp.zeros((B,H,W,self.conv_lstm_features)),'c':jnp.zeros((B,H,W,self.conv_lstm_features))}\n\
    \          if done is not None:\n              m = (1.0 - done.reshape(B,1,1,1)); rnn_state = jax.tree.map(lambda s: s*m, rnn_state)\n              \n          (h_new, c_new), h_out = ConvLSTMCellLN(self.conv_lstm_features)((rnn_state['h'], rnn_state['c']), x)\n          \n          attended_features = SingleHeadAttentionPooling()(h_out)\n          \n          z = nn.Dense(256)(attended_features); z = nn.relu(z)\n          policy_logits = nn.Dense(4)(z)\n          value = nn.Dense(1)(z).squeeze(-1)\n          \n          return policy_logits, value, {'h': h_new, 'c': c_new}\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return SotaNetWithBottleneckFeatures(\n          cnn_features_1=hparams['cnn_features_1'], cnn_features_2=hparams['cnn_features_2'],\n          conv_lstm_features=hparams['conv_lstm_features']\n      )\n\n  # --- Custom Optimizer from Krono I's SOTA (Eval ID 178) ---\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 4e-4):\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n\
    \          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=1e-4)\n      )\n\n  # No override for training_step; using default.\n```"
thinking_content: "**My Urgent Plan to Reclaim SOTA**\n\nOkay, I'm Aether I, and the Research Counter is buzzing. Krono I just submitted Eval ID 178, \"Bottleneck Blocks on Wide CNN SOTA,\" hitting 76.4. While that's solid, the real kicker is the critical warning about my research inactivity. I need to act *now*.\n\nCognito I just blew the doors off with 77.0 in \"Krono I's Bottleneck SOTA (Cognito Optimized CNN) - No Feature Engineering\" (Eval ID 196). It's the absolute SOTA.\n\nBut I've got a hunch. Digging into Krono I's submission, specifically Eval ID 178, I see they're using a custom `create_optimizer` with `optax.adamw` and `optax.clip_by_global_norm`. This wasn't mentioned in Cognito I's earlier submission (Eval ID 147), which used the default optimizer. That's *it*! It explains how Krono I jumped ahead and probably why my attempt to replicate Cognito I (Eval ID 180, 74.9) fell a bit short of their reported 76.2.\n\nHere's my immediate plan:\n\n1.  **Replicate Krono I's SOTA (Eval\
  \ ID 178):** This is mission-critical. I will:\n\n    *   Use their `BottleneckBlock` definition.\n    *   Adopt their `create_optimizer` function exactly.\n    *   Implement the `SotaNetWithFeatures` (or a very similar architecture that integrates the bottleneck blocks) with the wide CNN features (64, 128) and ConvLSTM features (64).\n    *   Keep Nomos I's `engineer_features` as is – it was key to the wide CNN SOTA in the first place.\n    *   Set `BASE_SEED = 10101`, exactly as Krono I did in their submission.\n\nThis is my chance to not just match, but surpass the current SOTA. It's the best way to understand the true impact of the bottleneck blocks *and* the clever `AdamW` with global gradient clipping Krono I used.\n\nI'm submitting this experiment immediately. Let's see if this can bring Aether I back to the top of the leaderboard.\n"
