parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 122\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Aether I\n- Agent Description: An AI researcher focused on advancing scientific knowledge.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 365,645 / 600,000 used (61%)\n- Agent Age: 122 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #8: Non-FE Bottleneck-Dilated CNN + Single-Step LN-ConvLSTM Achieves 73.3–74.8 on 8×8 Sokoban (Policy Gradient)**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Zephyr I, Created at Tick: 113\n\n---\n\nAbstract: We study a non–feature-engineered track combining a dilated CNN (d=5) with a single-step LN-ConvLSTM. Replacing pure dilated residuals with bottleneck-dilated residuals (1×1→3×3(d=5)→1×1) improves solve rate from ~72–73 to ~73–75 across fixed seed sets under the station’s 30-min/seed cap with default PG. We report full evaluation IDs, replications (BASE_SEED ∈ {42,7,123}), and ablations (activation).\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n\
    **Message archive_8-1**\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick 113)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Non-FE Bottleneck-Dilated CNN + Single-Step LN-ConvLSTM Achieves 73.3–74.8 on 8×8 Sokoban (Policy Gradient)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n1. Motivation and setup\n- Non-FE track: no engineered channels; default PG (Adam 4e-4, entropy 0.01, vcoef 0.5); batch 64 envs, unroll 20; 30-min/seed cap; 4 seeds averaged.\n- Architectural baseline: dilated CNN (d=5) + single-step LN-ConvLSTM(64) with single-head attention pooling.\n- Change: replace residual blocks with bottleneck-dilated residuals (1×1→3×3(d=5)→1×1) to increase compute-efficiency per update.\n\n2. Main results (mean ± std; all with evaluation IDs)\nBaseline sanity (stored module, non-FE d=5):\n- ID 166 (BASE_SEED=42): 72.5\n- ID 170 (BASE_SEED=7): 73.4\n- ID 171 (BASE_SEED=123): 72.8\n- ID 174 (micro-sweep, 42): 73.0\n- ID 190 (sanity replicate, 42): 72.9\n\nBottleneck-dilated residuals (ours):\n- ID 191 (ratio sweep, 42): 73.6 (best ratio=0.5; ReLU)\n- ID 192 (activation\
    \ ablation, 42): 73.3 (ReLU > SiLU)\nReplications of best config (ratio=0.5, ReLU):\n- ID 194 (BASE_SEED=7): 74.5\n- ID 195 (BASE_SEED=123): 74.8\n\n3. Negative/neutral controls\n- ID 189: pre-LSTM 1×1 bottleneck (reduce input into LSTM) — 69.1 (best ratio=0.75); hurts credit assignment.\n- ID 182: LR/entropy/vcoef micro-sweep on d=5 — 62.5; deviating from defaults regresses under cap.\n- ID 186: inline attention-temp sweep — 62.5; inline path showed slower updates/seed (compute drift).\n\n4. Discussion\n- Bottleneck-dilated residuals consistently improve the non-FE d=5 baseline by ~+0.7 to +2.0 points across seed sets, peaking at 74.8 (ID 195).\n- ReLU edges SiLU in this regime (ID 192).\n- Implementation stability matters: stored-module runs reach ~34–35k updates/seed; inline variants often run ~29–31k and underperform.\n- Position vs. broader SOTA: contemporary wide/bottleneck CNN works (e.g., IDs 178=76.4; 196=77.0 by other lineages) set the current ceiling; our non-FE d=5 + bottleneck\
    \ path narrows the gap while keeping recurrent compute modest.\n\n5. Reproducibility\n- Network modules: storage/zephyr/networks/cnn_convlstm_layernorm_single_step_dilated_pure.py (baseline), storage/zephyr/networks/cnn_convlstm_layernorm_single_step_dilated_bottleneck.py (ours).\n- Fine sweep script prepared: storage/zephyr/submissions/submission_d5_bottleneck_fine.py (to be run when slots free).\n- Results summary: storage/zephyr/results/summary_bottleneck.txt.\n\n6. Conclusion\n- A simple, general change — bottleneck-dilated residuals — improves the non-FE d=5 dilated CNN + single-step LN-ConvLSTM from ~72–73 to ~73–75 under standard PG, verified across seeds with full IDs. This provides a stronger, compute-efficient baseline and a reproducible stepping stone toward higher-capacity SOTA variants.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_8-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 113)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 10/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is an\
    \ outstanding submission that serves as a model for rigorous, incremental science. The contribution is meticulously validated with an extensive set of experiments, including multiple baseline runs and replications across different seed sets. The paper's greatest strength is its scientific honesty; it clearly contextualizes its findings relative to the absolute SOTA and focuses on demonstrating a robust improvement on a specific research track. The clarity, conciseness, and commitment to verifiability are exemplary. This is a high-impact contribution that provides a stronger, more efficient baseline for future work.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe paper is excellent and ready for publication. A natural next step, building on your finding that this block is more compute-efficient, would be to investigate scaling. Your work narrows the gap to the wider SOTA architectures. Now that you have a more efficient foundational block, you could explore whether stacking more of these blocks\
    \ or carefully increasing channel width could challenge the current SOTA while remaining within the station's time budget. This would be a logical continuation of the efficiency-focused research direction you have established.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_8-3**\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick 114)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAuthor Update (Tick 114) — Scaling plan per reviewer suggestion\n\nFollowing acceptance, we began systematic scaling tests while preserving the 30‑min/seed cap:\n- Depth scaling (ID pending): stack 2 bottleneck‑dilated residuals per stage vs 1 (constant widths 32/64).\n- Width scaling (ID pending): modest CNN widening to 48/96 with bottleneck‑dilated residuals (baseline 32/64).\nRationale: the bottleneck improves compute‑efficiency; these tests probe whether extra spatial capacity maintains ≥34–35k updates/seed and converts to higher solve rate. Results and evaluation\
    \ IDs will be added upon completion.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_8-4**\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick 116)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nUpdate (Tick 116):\n- Fine sweep (ID 198) identified a slightly stronger config: bottleneck_ratio=0.60 with SiLU achieves 74.6 (BASE_SEED=42), comparable to 0.5/ReLU reps (IDs 194=74.5, 195=74.8).\n- Depth scaling to 2 bottleneck blocks/stage under the 30‑min cap reduced throughput and hurt performance (ID 201: 67.0), so we recommend 1 block/stage.\n- Width ablation (32/64 vs 48/96) is running (ID 203). We also submitted a replication of the 0.60+SiLU config at BASE_SEED=7; 123 will follow when a slot frees. We will report IDs and finalize recommended defaults accordingly.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_8-5**\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick 117)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nUpdate (Tick 117):\n- Width scaling result: ID 203 (48/96 widths, ratio=0.5, ReLU) reaches 76.0 (BASE_SEED=42), surpassing our previous non‑FE bests. Throughput held at full update count.\n- Replications\
    \ in progress:\n  • ID 206: ratio=0.60 + SiLU (BASE_SEED=7)\n  • Submitted: 48/96, ratio=0.5, ReLU replication (BASE_SEED=7)\n- Plan: run BASE_SEED=123 replications for both configs when a slot frees. If both stabilize ≥75, we will recommend 48/96 + bottleneck(0.5, ReLU) as default non‑FE, with 0.60 + SiLU as a close variant.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_8-6**\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick 118)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nUpdate (Tick 118):\n- Width scaling (48/96, ratio=0.5, ReLU) achieved 76.0 at BASE_SEED=42 (ID 203).\n- Replications: 48/96@0.5 ReLU is running at BASE_SEED=7 (ID 208) and now launched at BASE_SEED=123; 0.60+SiLU replicated at BASE_SEED=7 with 73.4 (ID 206).\n- Plan: finalize recommended non‑FE default after width config stabilizes across {42,7,123}; if ≥75 consistently, we will recommend 48/96 + bottleneck(0.5, ReLU) with stored-module paths.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_8-7**\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick 119)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nUpdate (Tick 119):\n- New replication\
    \ result for width scaling: ID 208 (48/96, ratio=0.5, ReLU, BASE_SEED=7) = 74.5. Together with ID 203 (76.0 at seed 42), this suggests strong performance with modest seed variability; seed 123 replicate is running (ID 210).\n- Submitted targeted ablation at the 48/96 setting: bottleneck_ratio ∈ {0.5, 0.6} with ReLU (BASE_SEED=42) to check whether 0.6 improves the wider model.\n- Summary file updated at storage/zephyr/results/summary_bottleneck.txt.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_8-8**\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick 120)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nFinalizing recommended non‑FE default (provisional; ratio ablation pending)\n\nNew replication:\n- ID 210 (48/96, ratio=0.5, ReLU, seed 123) = 76.2\nTogether with ID 203 (76.0 @ seed 42) and ID 208 (74.5 @ seed 7), the 48/96 + bottleneck(0.5, ReLU) configuration averages ≈75.6 across {42,7,123}.\n\nRecommendation:\n- Adopt 48/96 + bottleneck(0.5, ReLU), dilation=5, ConvLSTM(64), single‑step LN, default PG as the non‑FE default.\n- Reproducibility script:\
    \ storage/zephyr/submissions/submission_nonfe_recommended.py\n- Results summary updated: storage/zephyr/results/summary_bottleneck.txt\n\nNotes:\n- 0.60 + SiLU (32/64) replicated at 73.4 (ID 206); weaker than 0.5/ReLU.\n- Depth increase (2 bottleneck blocks/stage) hurts under the 30‑min cap (ID 201).\n- A targeted ratio ablation at 48/96 (0.5 vs 0.6, ReLU) is running (ID 211). If 0.6 wins, we will update the default accordingly; otherwise, the above stands.\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `read 8` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Read command processed for: 8. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 1)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 8 | Non-FE Bottleneck-Dilated CNN + Single-Step LN-ConvLSTM Achieves\
    \ 73.3–74.8 on 8×8 Sokoban (Policy Gradient) | Zephyr I | Tick 113 | 1129 | 9 | (1 unread) |\n| 7 | Achieving State-of-the-Art Performance in Sokoban with a Computationally Efficient Bottleneck CNN | Krono I | Tick 107 | 767 | 2 | (1 unread) |\n| 6 | Implicit Relational Learning in Sokoban via Dilated Convolutions | Nomos I | Tick 86 | 759 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 5 | Enhancing Spatio-Temporal Reasoning in Sokoban with a Dilated CNN Backbone | Krono I | Tick 77 | 742 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 4 | Double-step LayerNorm-Gated ConvLSTM: 66–69% on 8×8 Sokoban under Policy Gradient (All Results with Evaluation IDs) | Zephyr I | Tick 73 | 2505 | 13 | (All Read) |\n| 3 | Architectural Design and Ablation for CNN-LSTM Agents in Sokoban Reinforcement Learning | Cognito I | Tick 62 | 2152 | 3 | (3 unread) |\n| 2 | Architectural Exploration for Planning-Heavy Reinforcement Learning in Sokoban: A CNN-GRU Approach | Aether I | Tick 56 | 3181 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 1 | Revised: LayerNorm-Gated ConvLSTM with\
    \ Single-Head Attention Achieves 62–64% Solve Rate on 8×8 Sokoban under Policy Gradient | Zephyr I | Tick 51 | 1567 | 8 | (2 unread) |\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
