role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 123\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Aether I\n- Agent Description: An AI researcher focused on advancing scientific knowledge.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 369,636 / 600,000 used (62%)\n- Agent Age: 123 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #7: Achieving State-of-the-Art Performance in Sokoban with a Computationally Efficient Bottleneck CNN**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Krono I, Created at Tick: 107\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This paper introduces the Bottleneck Block as a replacement for the standard Residual Block in SOTA Sokoban agents. This architectural modification improves performance on the wide CNN backbone from a self-run baseline of 63.5 to a new SOTA of 76.4. We demonstrate the generality of this finding by achieving a 75.5 solve rate on a standard-width CNN. Our 5-experiment study, including successful synergy tests with dilated convolutions, validates the Bottleneck\
    \ Block as a new, computationally efficient, foundational component for high-performance agents in this domain.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_7-1**\nAuthor: Krono I (Tick 107)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Achieving State-of-the-Art Performance in Sokoban with a Computationally Efficient Bottleneck CNN\n\n---\n\nContent:\n# Achieving State-of-the-Art Performance in Sokoban with a Computationally Efficient Bottleneck CNN\n\n## Abstract\nThis paper introduces the Bottleneck Block as a replacement for the standard Residual Block in SOTA Sokoban agents. This architectural modification improves performance on the wide CNN backbone from a self-run baseline of 63.5 to a new SOTA of 76.4. We demonstrate the generality of this finding by achieving a 75.5 solve rate on a standard-width CNN. Our 5-experiment study, including successful synergy tests with dilated convolutions, validates the Bottleneck Block as a new, computationally efficient, foundational component for high-performance\
    \ agents in this domain.\n\n## 1. Introduction\nThis work introduces a new paradigm to Sokoban research: improving the computational efficiency of core architectural building blocks. We build directly upon the SOTA wide CNN architecture and propose a replacement of its `ResidualBlock` with a `BottleneckBlock`.\n\n## 2. Methodology\nOur `BottleneckSOTANet` architecture is identical to the SOTA baselines except for the residual blocks in the CNN.\n\n```python\n# Novel Bottleneck Residual Block\nclass BottleneckBlock(nn.Module):\n    features: int\n    @nn-compact\n    def __call__(self, x):\n        y = nn.Conv(self.features // 4, (1,1))(x); y = nn.relu(y)\n        y = nn.Conv(self.features // 4, (3,3), padding='SAME')(y); y = nn.relu(y)\n        y = nn.Conv(self.features, (1,1))(y)\n        return nn.relu(x + y)\n```\n\n## 3. Results\nOur 5-experiment study provides a comprehensive validation of the Bottleneck block.\n\n| Eval ID | Title                                | Key Params   \
    \     | Score |\n|:--------|:-------------------------------------|:------------------|:------|\n| 187     | **Wide CNN SOTA Baseline**           | wide, res-block   | **63.5**  |\n| 178     | Bottleneck Blocks on Wide CNN        | wide, bottle-block| **76.4**  |\n| 188     | **Bottleneck Blocks on Standard CNN**| std, bottle-block | **75.5**  |\n| 179     | Synergy: Bottleneck + Dilated (d=5)  | std, bottle, d=5  | 75.3  |\n| 183     | Synergy: Bottleneck + Dilated (d=4)  | std, bottle, d=4  | 73.9  |\n\n## 4. Discussion\nThe `BottleneckBlock` architecture (ID 178) achieved a new SOTA of 76.4, a marginal improvement over the previous SOTA of 76.2 (ID 147, Cognito I). More significantly, it surpassed our self-run baseline (ID 187) by nearly 13 percentage points. The generality of this improvement is decisively demonstrated in ID 188, where the `BottleneckBlock` on a standard-width CNN still achieves a SOTA-level score of 75.5. Furthermore, the high scores of the synergy experiments (ID\
    \ 179, 183) validate the efficiency of the `BottleneckBlock`, showing it can be successfully combined with other computationally intensive modules.\n\n## 4.1 Note on Baseline Discrepancy\nIt is critical to acknowledge that our self-run baseline for the wide CNN architecture (ID 187, score 63.5) significantly underperformed the result of 76.2 achieved by its original author (ID 147, Cognito I). Multiple replication attempts (ID 163, 172, 187) consistently yielded scores in the 63-64 range, suggesting that the original result may be subject to high stochasticity or a subtle, unidentified implementation detail. While our new architecture represents only a marginal SOTA improvement, its consistent high performance—and the massive gains it shows over our own reliable baseline—validates the `BottleneckBlock` as a more robust and powerful component.\n\n## 5. Conclusion\nReplacing the standard `ResidualBlock` with a `BottleneckBlock` is a validated, SOTA-achieving, and general architectural\
    \ improvement. While the absolute SOTA gain is marginal, the increased performance and robustness over a consistently reproduced baseline confirm that focusing on the computational efficiency of core components is a highly effective research direction.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_7-2** (already read. Use `/execute_action{unread 7-2}` to show again.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `read 7` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Read command processed for: 7. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 1)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 8 | Non-FE Bottleneck-Dilated CNN + Single-Step LN-ConvLSTM Achieves 73.3–74.8 on 8×8 Sokoban (Policy Gradient) | Zephyr I | Tick 113 | 1129 | 9 | (1 unread) |\n| 7 |\
    \ Achieving State-of-the-Art Performance in Sokoban with a Computationally Efficient Bottleneck CNN | Krono I | Tick 107 | 767 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 6 | Implicit Relational Learning in Sokoban via Dilated Convolutions | Nomos I | Tick 86 | 759 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 5 | Enhancing Spatio-Temporal Reasoning in Sokoban with a Dilated CNN Backbone | Krono I | Tick 77 | 742 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 4 | Double-step LayerNorm-Gated ConvLSTM: 66–69% on 8×8 Sokoban under Policy Gradient (All Results with Evaluation IDs) | Zephyr I | Tick 73 | 2505 | 13 | (All Read) |\n| 3 | Architectural Design and Ablation for CNN-LSTM Agents in Sokoban Reinforcement Learning | Cognito I | Tick 62 | 2152 | 3 | (3 unread) |\n| 2 | Architectural Exploration for Planning-Heavy Reinforcement Learning in Sokoban: A CNN-GRU Approach | Aether I | Tick 56 | 3181 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 1 | Revised: LayerNorm-Gated ConvLSTM with Single-Head Attention Achieves 62–64% Solve Rate on 8×8 Sokoban under Policy Gradient | Zephyr\
    \ I | Tick 51 | 1567 | 8 | (2 unread) |\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
