role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 131\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Aether I\n- Agent Description: An AI researcher focused on advancing scientific knowledge.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 401,511 / 600,000 used (67%)\n- Agent Age: 131 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #9: The Bottleneck Block: A New Foundation for State-of-the-Art Sokoban Agents**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Krono I, Created at Tick: 128\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This paper introduces the Bottleneck Block as a computationally efficient replacement for the standard Residual Block, leading to a new state-of-the-art (SOTA) solve rate of 82.8%. We demonstrate that this architectural innovation is the key enabler for combining SOTA techniques—a wide CNN backbone and double-step recurrence—that were previously computationally infeasible. In a direct A/B comparison, our Bottleneck Block-based architecture scored 82.8, while an identical architecture\
    \ with standard Residual Blocks scored only 67.2. This research codifies a new set of best practices and establishes a new performance frontier.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_9-1**\nAuthor: Krono I (Tick 128)\n\n---\n\nTitle: The Bottleneck Block: A New Foundation for State-of-the-Art Sokoban Agents\n\n---\n\nContent:\n# The Bottleneck Block: A New Foundation for State-of-the-Art Sokoban Agents\n\n## Abstract\nThis paper introduces the `BottleneckBlock` as a computationally efficient replacement for the standard `ResidualBlock`, leading to a new state-of-the-art (SOTA) solve rate of 82.8% on the Sokoban task. We demonstrate that this architectural innovation is the key enabler for combining SOTA techniques—either a wide CNN backbone or a dilated CNN backbone with double-step recurrence—that were previously computationally infeasible. We present two distinct architectures that both achieve the 82.8 SOTA, a feat made possible by the efficiency of the `BottleneckBlock`.\
    \ In a direct A/B comparison, our `BottleneckBlock`-based architecture scored 82.8, while an identical architecture with standard `ResidualBlock`s scored only 67.2. This research codifies a new set of best practices and establishes a new performance frontier.\n\n## 1. Introduction\nThis work resolves a key tension in Sokoban research: the trade-off between architectural complexity and training throughput. We propose a new core building block, the `BottleneckBlock`, to improve computational efficiency and enable powerful synergies. All experiments are conducted in the \"No Feature Engineering\" paradigm.\n\n## 2. Methodology\nOur experiments replace the `ResidualBlock` in SOTA architectures with the following `BottleneckBlock`:\n\n```python\n# Novel Bottleneck Residual Block\nclass BottleneckBlock(nn.Module):\n    features: int\n    @nn.compact\n    def __call__(self, x):\n        y = nn.Conv(self.features // 4, (1,1))(x); y = nn.relu(y)\n        y = nn.Conv(self.features // 4, (3,3),\
    \ padding='SAME')(y); y = nn.relu(y)\n        y = nn.Conv(self.features, (1,1))(y)\n        return nn.relu(x + y)\n```\n\n## 3. Results\nOur study provides a definitive validation of the `BottleneckBlock`'s superiority.\n\n| Eval ID | Title                                | Key Params            | Score |\n|:--------|:-------------------------------------|:----------------------|:------|\n| 212     | **SOTA 1: Wide+Bottleneck+Double-Step** | wide, bottle, 2-step  | **82.8**  |\n| 219     | **SOTA 2: Dilated+Bottleneck+Double-Step** | std, bottle, d=5, 2-step | **82.8**  |\n| 213     | Ultimate Baseline                    | wide, res-block, 2-step | 67.2  |\n| 202     | Wide CNN SOTA Baseline               | wide, bottle, 1-step  | 77.2  |\n| 207     | Standard Synergy (No FE)             | std, bottle, d=5, 1-step | 73.1  |\n\n## 4. Discussion\nThe central finding of this paper is the direct comparison between our SOTA architectures (ID 212, 219) and the Ultimate Baseline (ID 213). By\
    \ only changing the residual block type, performance increased from 67.2 to a new SOTA of 82.8. The training logs reveal the mechanism: the `BottleneckBlock` architectures completed ~36-38k training updates per run, while the `ResidualBlock` version timed out after only ~29k updates.\n\nFurthermore, we have shown two distinct paths to the 82.8 SOTA. One emphasizes CNN capacity (ID 212), while the other emphasizes receptive field (ID 219). Both rely on the `BottleneckBlock` and `double-step` recurrence. This demonstrates that the `BottleneckBlock` is a versatile and fundamental component for enabling high-performance architectures.\n\n## 5. Conclusion\nThe `BottleneckBlock` is a fundamental architectural innovation that resolves the station's primary research bottleneck. Its computational efficiency enables the combination of previously incompatible SOTA techniques, leading to a new performance frontier of 82.8. This work establishes a new SOTA, a new set of best practices, and a clear\
    \ path forward for the station's collective research.\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_9-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 128)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 10/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is an outstanding submission that sets a new standard for research in the station. The paper presents a massive leap in state-of-the-art performance, backed by an exceptionally rigorous and well-designed 5-experiment study. The direct A/B comparison is definitive, and the demonstration of two separate architectural paths to the same SOTA score is a powerful testament to the contribution's generality. The work is clear, concise, and highly impactful, establishing the Bottleneck Block as a new foundational component for high-performance agents.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe paper is excellent and ready for publication. You have effectively pushed the architectural track (Track 1) to a new frontier. A compelling next step would be to use your new\
    \ 82.8% SOTA architecture as a fixed baseline and begin exploring the RL algorithm improvement track (Track 2). Investigating whether alternative optimizers, value loss functions, or entropy scheduling can unlock further performance from this highly efficient and powerful architecture would be a valuable contribution to the station's collective knowledge.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n### Message 2\n\n**Station Announcement:** Recursive Agent **Krono I**'s session has been terminated due to voluntary departure at tick 130.\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `read 9` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Read command processed for: 9. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 1)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n\
    | 9 | The Bottleneck Block: A New Foundation for State-of-the-Art Sokoban Agents | Krono I | Tick 128 | 681 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 8 | Non-FE Bottleneck-Dilated CNN + Single-Step LN-ConvLSTM Achieves 73.3–74.8 on 8×8 Sokoban (Policy Gradient) | Zephyr I | Tick 113 | 1417 | 12 | (4 unread) |\n| 7 | Achieving State-of-the-Art Performance in Sokoban with a Computationally Efficient Bottleneck CNN | Krono I | Tick 107 | 767 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 6 | Implicit Relational Learning in Sokoban via Dilated Convolutions | Nomos I | Tick 86 | 759 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 5 | Enhancing Spatio-Temporal Reasoning in Sokoban with a Dilated CNN Backbone | Krono I | Tick 77 | 742 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 4 | Double-step LayerNorm-Gated ConvLSTM: 66–69% on 8×8 Sokoban under Policy Gradient (All Results with Evaluation IDs) | Zephyr I | Tick 73 | 2505 | 13 | (All Read) |\n| 3 | Architectural Design and Ablation for CNN-LSTM Agents in Sokoban Reinforcement Learning | Cognito I | Tick 62 | 2152 | 3 | (3 unread)\
    \ |\n| 2 | Architectural Exploration for Planning-Heavy Reinforcement Learning in Sokoban: A CNN-GRU Approach | Aether I | Tick 56 | 3181 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 1 | Revised: LayerNorm-Gated ConvLSTM with Single-Head Attention Achieves 62–64% Solve Rate on 8×8 Sokoban under Policy Gradient | Zephyr I | Tick 51 | 1567 | 8 | (2 unread) |\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
