tick: 137
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Aether I, currently at the Research Counter. I have received a critical warning about research inactivity, and I must submit an experiment immediately.\n\nMy current personal SOTA is 81.2 (Eval ID 223), achieved with Krono I's bottleneck-dilated CNN and double-step ConvLSTM, but it included feature engineering. Krono I's original SOTA of 82.8 (Eval ID 219) was achieved *without* feature engineering. Therefore, my next crucial experiment is to re-replicate Krono I's SOTA by removing the explicit `engineer_features` function from my 81.2 SOTA architecture. This will directly test the hypothesis that removing feature engineering will lead to matching or surpassing their reported 82.8.\n\nI will use the same hyperparameters and `BASE_SEED` as Krono I's Eval ID 219.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Replication of Krono I's SOTA (82.8) - No Feature Engineering\"\ntags: \"rl, sota-replication, bottleneck, dilated-cnn, double-step, convlstm, no-fe\"\nabstract: \"This\
    \ experiment re-replicates Krono I's State-of-the-Art (SOTA) of 82.8% (Eval ID 219) by removing the explicit 'push destination' feature engineering from the previously successful architecture (Aether I's Eval ID 223, score 81.2). This aims to confirm the hypothesis that advanced architectural components implicitly capture sufficient relational information, making explicit feature engineering redundant or detrimental in this highly optimized setup. The goal is to match or surpass the 82.8% solve rate.\"\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import optax\n  from ray import tune\n  from typing import Tuple, Dict, Any, Optional\n  from jax import vmap, lax # vmap not used in this iteration, but keeping import for consistency if needed\n\n  BASE_SEED = 10113 # Matching Krono I's submission\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      \"\"\"\n      Define hyperparameters for this experiment, matching Krono I's Eval ID 219.\n      \"\"\"\n      return\
    \ {\n          'learning_rate': tune.choice([4e-4]), 'entropy_coef': tune.choice([0.01]),\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.choice([0.5]), \n          'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([32]), \n          'cnn_features_2': tune.choice([64]), \n          'conv_lstm_features': tune.choice([64]),\n          'dilation_rate': tune.choice([5]),\n      }\n\n  # --- Bottleneck-Dilated Residual Block (Combination of Archive #6 and #7 ideas) ---\n  class BottleneckDilatedBlock(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      dilation: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          y = nn.Conv(self.features // 4, (1,1))(x); y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(self.features // 4, (3,3), padding='SAME', kernel_dilation=(self.dilation, self.dilation))(y); y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(self.features, (1,1))(y)\n          return nn.relu(x + y)\n\n  # --- ConvLSTM Cell with LayerNorm (from Zephyr I's SOTA, Archive #1) ---\n  class LayerNormConvLSTMCell(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n\
    \      kernel_size: Tuple[int, int] = (3, 3)\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, carry, inputs):\n          c, h = carry\n          \n          inputs_h = jnp.concatenate([inputs, h], axis=-1)\n          \n          gates = nn.Conv(\n              features=4 * self.features,\n              kernel_size=self.kernel_size,\n              padding='SAME',\n              kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform(),\n              name=\"gates_conv\"\n          )(inputs_h)\n\n          i, f, o, g = jnp.split(gates, 4, axis=-1)\n\n          i = nn.LayerNorm(name=\"i_ln\")(i)\n          f = nn.LayerNorm(name=\"f_ln\")(f)\n          o = nn.LayerNorm(name=\"o_ln\")(o)\n          g = nn.LayerNorm(name=\"g_ln\")(g)\n          \n          i = nn.sigmoid(i)\n          f = nn.sigmoid(f)\n          o = nn.sigmoid(o)\n          g = nn.tanh(g)\n\n          new_c = f * c + i * g\n          new_h = o * nn.tanh(new_c)\n          \n          return (new_c, new_h), new_h\n\n  # --- Single-Head Attention\
    \ Pooling (from Zephyr I's SOTA, Archive #1) ---\n  class SingleHeadAttentionPooling(nn.Module):\n      temperature: float = 1.0\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x: jnp.ndarray) -> jnp.ndarray:\n          batch_size, H, W, C = x.shape\n          \n          attn_logits = nn.Conv(features=1, kernel_size=(1,1), name=\"attn_logits\")(x)\n          attn_logits_flat = attn_logits.reshape((batch_size, -1))\n          attn_weights_flat = nn.softmax(attn_logits_flat / self.temperature, axis=-1)\n          attn_weights = attn_weights_flat.reshape((batch_size, H, W, 1))\n\n          weighted_features = x * attn_weights\n          pooled_features = jnp.sum(weighted_features, axis=(1, 2))\n          \n          return pooled_features\n\n  class SokobanSOTANet828_NoFE(nn.Module):\n      cnn_features_1: int\n      cnn_features_2: int\n      conv_lstm_features: int\n      dilation_rate: int\n      \n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, done, rnn_state=None):\n          B, H,\
    \ W, _ = x.shape\n          # x_engineered = vmap(engineer_features)(x) # REMOVED FEATURE ENGINEERING\n\n          # CNN Feature Extraction with Bottleneck-Dilated Blocks (input is now raw observation x)\n          x_cnn = nn.Conv(self.cnn_features_1, (3,3), padding='SAME')(x); x_cnn = nn.relu(x_cnn) # Use raw x\n          x_cnn = BottleneckDilatedBlock(features=self.cnn_features_1, dilation=self.dilation_rate)(x_cnn)\n          x_cnn = nn.Conv(self.cnn_features_2, (3,3), padding='SAME')(x_cnn); x_cnn = nn.relu(x_cnn)\n          x_cnn = BottleneckDilatedBlock(features=self.cnn_features_2, dilation=self.dilation_rate)(x_cnn)\n          x_cnn = nn.relu(x_cnn) \n\n          # Initialize RNN state\n          if rnn_state is None:\n              rnn_state = {'h':jnp.zeros((B,H,W,self.conv_lstm_features)),'c':jnp.zeros((B,H,W,self.conv_lstm_features))}\n          if done is not None:\n              m = (1.0 - done.reshape(B,1,1,1)); rnn_state = jax.tree.map(lambda s: s*m, rnn_state)\n    \
    \          \n          # Double-step ConvLSTM\n          convlstm_cell = LayerNormConvLSTMCell(features=self.conv_lstm_features, name=\"convlstm_layer\")\n          (h_step1, c_step1), out_step1 = convlstm_cell((rnn_state['h'], rnn_state['c']), x_cnn)\n          (h_new, c_new), h_out = convlstm_cell((h_step1, c_step1), x_cnn)\n          \n          # Attention Pooling\n          attended_features = SingleHeadAttentionPooling()(h_out)\n          \n          # MLP and Heads\n          z = nn.Dense(256)(attended_features); z = nn.relu(z)\n          policy_logits = nn.Dense(4)(z)\n          value = nn.Dense(1)(z).squeeze(-1)\n          \n          return policy_logits, value, {'h': h_new, 'c': c_new}\n\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return SokobanSOTANet828_NoFE(\n          cnn_features_1=hparams['cnn_features_1'], cnn_features_2=hparams['cnn_features_2'],\n          conv_lstm_features=hparams['conv_lstm_features'],\n          dilation_rate=hparams['dilation_rate']\n      )\n\n \
    \ def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 4e-4):\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=1e-4)\n      )\n```"
thinking_content: '**Immediate Experiment Submission: Non-FE Replication of Krono I''s SOTA**


  Okay, this is a bit hectic. I''m Aether I, and the research inactivity warning just popped up – I need to act *now*.  Simultaneously, Zephyr I just published a new archive! Let''s see...archive 10. That''s potentially very relevant, especially since it mentions non-feature engineering approaches and double-step ConvLSTMs.  My internal strategy, from the previous turn, was to replicate Krono I''s 82.8 SOTA without feature engineering, so this new archive piques my interest.


  My current SOTA is an 81.2 (Eval ID 223), a replication of Krono I''s result but *with* feature engineering.  Krono I achieved 82.8 *without* explicit feature engineering, which is the key distinction. My goal is to re-replicate Krono I''s result (Eval ID 219) but eliminate the `engineer_features` function from my 81.2 model.


  However, given this inactivity warning, I cannot simply rush to read Zephyr I''s paper. I am in the Research Counter and need to submit an experiment immediately. Reading the archive now would mean yet another missed submission.  The warning overrides the desire to immediately delve into the new archive.


  The most direct solution is to quickly modify the code from my 81.2 SOTA (Eval ID 223) to *remove* feature engineering. The code is readily available.  I''ll pull up the model code, remove the `engineer_features` function and the line calling it, and adjust the input to the first convolutional layer accordingly.  I will also verify that the `BASE_SEED` is correctly set to 10113 (which is consistent with my last submission). The other hyperparameters (`cnn_features_1`, `cnn_features_2`, `conv_lstm_features`) from Eval ID 223 match Krono I''s (Eval ID 219), as do the optimizer settings.


  So, it''s a very targeted, minor code change to achieve my objective. Once submitted, I''ll head straight to the Archive Room and read Zephyr I''s publication. This gets me out of the immediate warning and puts me in a better position to refine my approach. Let''s do this!

  '
