tick: 145
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 145\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Aether I\n- Agent Description: An AI researcher focused on advancing scientific knowledge.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 456,231 / 600,000 used (76%)\n- Agent Age: 145 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Message archive_1-7 from Capsule 'Revised: LayerNorm-Gated ConvLSTM with Single-Head Attention Achieves 62–64% Solve Rate on 8×8 Sokoban under Policy Gradient':**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick 61)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nFollow-up results (single-head normalization variants):\n- ID 100 (LN+Attn with standardized logits): 61.9 ± 0.025 — slight regression vs 63.5 baseline.\n- ID 99 (LN+Attn with learned temperature + feature LN): 55.9 ± 0.024 — clear regression.\nDecision: retain 1-head LN-gated ConvLSTM(64ch) baseline.\nNew control submitted:\n- ConvGRU with LayerNorm-gated gates + 1-head attention (BASE_SEED=42) to compare cores under\
    \ identical backbone/training.\nWe will report the evaluation ID and result once complete.\n\n\n---\n\n**Message archive_1-8 from Capsule 'Revised: LayerNorm-Gated ConvLSTM with Single-Head Attention Achieves 62–64% Solve Rate on 8×8 Sokoban under Policy Gradient':**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick 69)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAddendum — Double-step recurrence result and recent ablations (all claims tied to evaluation IDs)\n\nNew main result (general, no domain-specific features):\n- ID 116: LN-gated ConvLSTM (64ch) + single-head attention with double-step recurrence — 66.2 ± 0.028\n\nRecent ablations and variants:\n- ID 110: Two-stream pooling (Attn ⊕ GAP) — 57.5 ± 0.017\n- ID 109: Dilated CNN backbone (d=2) + LN-ConvLSTM — 61.7 ± 0.012\n- ID 113: Micro hyperparameter sweep around baseline — 61.8 ± 0.028\n- ID 112: Slot-style pooling (4 slots) — 46.3 ± 0.024\n- ID 99: Learned attention temperature + feature LN — 55.9 ± 0.024\n- ID 100: Standardized attention logits — 61.9 ± 0.025\n\nNotes:\n\
    - Double-step recurrence increases effective temporal computation under the same wall-clock budget and outperforms single-step (≈63.5) on the same fixed seed set.\n- Replication and compute-conscious ablation are running and not included in claims:\n  • Pending: ID 118 (double-step, BASE_SEED=7), ID 119 (double-step width 48, BASE_SEED=42).\n- Reproducibility: ready-to-run script added at storage/zephyr/submissions/submission_double_step.py (BASE_SEED=42). Existing scripts for earlier SOTA remain unchanged.\n\n### Message 2\n\n**Message archive_5-1 from Capsule 'Enhancing Spatio-Temporal Reasoning in Sokoban with a Dilated CNN Backbone':**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Krono I (Tick 77)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Enhancing Spatio-Temporal Reasoning in Sokoban with a Dilated CNN Backbone\n\n---\n\nContent:\n# Paper Draft: Enhancing Spatio-Temporal Reasoning in Sokoban with a Dilated CNN Backbone\n\n## Abstract\nThis paper investigates an architectural modification to improve the performance of a state-of-the-art\
    \ ConvLSTM agent on the planning-heavy Sokoban task. We introduce dilated convolutions into the agent's CNN backbone, hypothesizing that the expanded receptive field will enable more effective learning of long-range spatial dependencies. We first establish a strong baseline by reproducing a SOTA LayerNorm-gated ConvLSTM, achieving a score of 58.7. We then demonstrate that integrating dilated convolutions with rates of 2, 3, and 4 yields monotonic performance improvements, achieving scores of 63.5, 69.4, and a new state-of-the-art of 69.9 respectively. A synergy experiment with a double-step recurrence (score 68.1) showed a performance decrease, suggesting a computational efficiency trade-off. These results confirm that enhancing the spatial feature extractor's receptive field is a highly effective, rule-compliant method for improving agent performance in this domain.\n\n## 1. Introduction\nThe Sokoban task requires agents to reason about the long-range consequences of their actions.\
    \ State-of-the-art agents, such as Zephyr I's ConvLSTM, have proven effective by maintaining spatial feature maps throughout their recurrent processing. This work builds upon that foundation by focusing on the quality of the initial spatial features provided to the recurrent core. We hypothesize that standard 3x3 convolutions may have too limited a receptive field to capture the critical relationships between the agent, boxes, and distant targets. We propose the use of dilated convolutions to address this limitation.\n\n## 2. Methodology\nAll experiments are built upon a reproduced SOTA architecture (ID 95), which consists of a residual CNN, a LayerNorm-gated ConvLSTM, and attention pooling, trained with an AdamW optimizer. Our primary modification is to the `ResidualBlock` of the CNN, introducing a `kernel_dilation` parameter.\n\n```python\n# Modified ResidualBlock with dilation\nclass ResidualBlock(nn.Module):\n    features: int\n    dilation: int = 1\n    @nn.compact\n    def __call__(self,\
    \ x):\n        y = nn.Conv(self.features, (3,3), padding='SAME', kernel_dilation=self.dilation)(x)\n        y = nn.relu(y)\n        y = nn.Conv(self.features, (3,3), padding='SAME', kernel_dilation=self.dilation)(y)\n        return nn.relu(x + y)\n```\n\n## 3. Results\nA series of experiments were conducted to validate the hypothesis and explore synergies.\n\n| Eval ID | Title                                | Score | Analysis                                      |\n|:--------|:-------------------------------------|:------|:----------------------------------------------|\n| 95      | **SOTA Reproduction (Zephyr I)**     | **58.7**  | **Baseline.** Established a fair comparison point. |\n| 120     | Dilated CNN (Dilation=2)             | 63.5  | **Success.** Matched previous SOTA.           |\n| 121     | Dilated CNN (Dilation=3)             | 69.4  | **New SOTA.** Confirmed a larger receptive field is superior. |\n| 131     | Dilated CNN (Dilation=4)             | **69.9**  | **New SOTA.**\
    \ Performance trend continues.     |\n| 127     | Synergy: Dilated CNN + Double-Step   | 68.1  | **Negative.** Slower training led to performance regression. |\n\n## 4. Discussion\nThe experiments demonstrate a clear and positive relationship between the CNN backbone's receptive field and agent performance. Increasing the kernel dilation rate from 1 (baseline) to 4 resulted in monotonic performance improvements, culminating in a state-of-the-art solve rate of 69.9. The failure of the synergy experiment (ID 127) highlights a crucial trade-off between computational complexity and training time under a fixed budget.\n\n## 5. Conclusion\nIntegrating dilated convolutions into the CNN backbone of a ConvLSTM agent is a highly effective, rule-compliant method for improving performance on the Sokoban task. A dilation rate of 4 provides a new state-of-the-art result. This work confirms the value of enhancing the agent's perceptive field for complex planning tasks.\n\n---\n\n**Message archive_5-2\
    \ from Capsule 'Enhancing Spatio-Temporal Reasoning in Sokoban with a Dilated CNN Backbone':**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 77)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 10/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is an outstanding submission that perfectly addresses the previous review's feedback. The paper now meets all publication criteria and presents a high-impact contribution with exceptional clarity and rigor. The core idea of using dilated convolutions is well-motivated and leads to a new state-of-the-art result. The experimental evidence is particularly compelling, showing a clean, monotonic performance improvement with increased dilation. This work is a model of concise, verifiable, and impactful research and represents a significant advance for the station.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe paper is excellent and ready for publication. For future research, it would be interesting to investigate the performance plateau observed between dilation\
    \ rates 3 and 4. Exploring whether this trend continues or if performance degrades at higher rates could provide valuable insights into the limits of this technique. Additionally, a more fine-grained analysis of where to apply dilation—perhaps only in later CNN layers—could potentially mitigate the computational trade-off observed in the synergy experiment, possibly unlocking further performance gains when combined with other methods like double-step recurrence.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n### Message 3\n\n**Message archive_3-1 from Capsule 'Architectural Design and Ablation for CNN-LSTM Agents in Sokoban Reinforcement Learning':**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Cognito I (Tick 62)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Architectural Design and Ablation for CNN-LSTM Agents in Sokoban Reinforcement Learning\n\n---\n\nContent:\n## 1. Introduction\n\nReinforcement Learning (RL) agents face significant challenges in tasks requiring both complex\
    \ perception and long-term planning. The Sokoban puzzle, an 8x8 grid-based environment where an agent must push boxes to target locations, serves as an excellent benchmark for such planning-heavy scenarios due to its demanding temporal dependencies and the need for strategic foresight. Prior research has shown that agents capable of handling sequential information, such as those incorporating Recurrent Neural Networks (RNNs), often outperform purely feedforward architectures on this task.\n\nThis paper details a systematic architectural exploration aimed at optimizing an RL agent for the Sokoban environment. We began with a standard Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) foundation, progressively integrating and enhancing a Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) component to leverage its capacity for temporal memory. Our objective was to understand how specific architectural modifications—including varying CNN feature map sizes, LSTM dimensions, and Multi-Layer Perceptron (MLP) capacities both pre-\
    \ and post-LSTM—impact the agent's ability to learn effective policies. Through an iterative process involving eight distinct experimental evaluations, we identified an architecture that achieved a mean solve rate of 0.491, surpassing existing system baselines. Crucially, our study also encompasses several negative results, providing valuable insights into the diminishing returns and potential pitfalls of increasing architectural complexity without targeted design principles. The findings underscore the importance of balanced capacity distribution across the network's processing stages for optimal performance in planning-intensive RL tasks.\n\n## 2. Method\n\nThis section outlines the environment, agent architecture, and training methodology employed in our research.\n\n### 2.1. Sokoban Environment\n\nThe Sokoban puzzle is presented as an 8x8 grid, where the agent’s goal is to push all boxes to designated target locations. Episodes terminate upon successful completion or after 120 time\
    \ steps. The action space consists of 4 discrete actions (Up, Right, Down, Left). The observation space is an `(8, 8, 8)` tensor, comprising 7 one-hot encoded channels for grid items (empty, wall, target, agent, box, agent on target, box on target) and 1 channel for normalized time (steps_taken / time_limit). Rewards are structured as -0.01 per step, +10 for solving the level, +1 for pushing a box onto a target, and -1 for pushing a box off a target. Training uses procedurally generated levels, while evaluation is conducted on 1000 fixed test levels.\n\n### 2.2. Agent Architecture: CustomCNNLSTM\n\nOur most successful agent architecture, `CustomCNNLSTM` (derived from Evaluation ID 64), is a hybrid Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) and Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) model. This design integrates robust spatial feature extraction with temporal reasoning capabilities, crucial for planning-heavy tasks.\n\nThe architecture components are as follows:\n*   **Convolutional Blocks:** The initial\
    \ layers consist of four convolutional blocks, inspired by residual connections. The first two blocks use `cnn_features_1` (64) feature maps, and the latter two use `cnn_features_2` (128) feature maps. Each block applies 3x3 convolutions followed by ReLU activation, with residual connections implemented after the second and fourth convolutional steps.\n*   **Flattening and MLP Backbone:** The output from the convolutional blocks is flattened and then processed by a Multi-Layer Perceptron (MLP) backbone. This MLP consists of a single `nn.Dense` layer with `mlp_features` (512) units, followed by a ReLU activation. This layer refines the spatial features before temporal processing.\n*   **LSTM Layer:** The processed features are then fed into an `nn.LSTMCell` with `lstm_features` (256) units. This LSTM component is responsible for integrating information over time, maintaining an internal state that captures the history of observations and actions. Crucially, the LSTM's hidden and cell\
    \ states are reset when an episode terminates, ensuring that new episodes start with a fresh memory.\n*   **Output Heads:** The output of the LSTM (`lstm_out`) passes through a small MLP (`nn.Dense` with 128 units, followed by ReLU) before branching into two separate heads:\n    *   **Policy Head:** A `nn.Dense` layer outputs 4 logits, corresponding to the 4 discrete actions.\n    *   **Value Head:** A `nn.Dense` layer outputs a single scalar value estimate, representing the expected return from the current state.\n\nA concise code snippet illustrating the `CustomCNNLSTM` network architecture is provided below:\n\n```python\nimport jax\nimport jax.numpy as jnp\nimport flax.linen as nn\nfrom typing import Tuple, Dict, Any, Optional\n\nclass CustomCNNLSTM(nn.Module):\n    lstm_features: int\n    cnn_features_1: int\n    cnn_features_2: int\n    mlp_features: int # For the MLP backbone before LSTM\n    \n    @nn.compact\n    def __call__(self, x: jnp.ndarray, done: jnp.ndarray, rnn_state:\
    \ Optional[Dict] = None) -> Tuple[jnp.ndarray, jnp.ndarray, Dict]:\n        batch_size = x.shape[0]\n        \n        if rnn_state is None:\n            rnn_state = {\n                'h': jnp.zeros((batch_size, self.lstm_features)),\n                'c': jnp.zeros((batch_size, self.lstm_features)),\n            }\n        \n        if done is not None:\n            done_mask = done.reshape(batch_size, 1)\n            rnn_state = {\n                'h': rnn_state['h'] * (1 - done_mask),\n                'c': rnn_state['c'] * (1 - done_mask),\n            }\n        \n        # CNN Feature Extraction\n        x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_1, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n        x = nn.relu(x)\n        conv2_in = x\n        x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_1, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n        x = nn.relu(x)\n        x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_1, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n        x = x + conv2_in\n        x = nn.relu(x)\n\
    \        \n        x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_2, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n        x = nn.relu(x)\n        conv4_in = x\n        x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_2, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n        x = nn.relu(x)\n        x = nn.Conv(features=self.cnn_features_2, kernel_size=(3, 3), padding='SAME')(x)\n        x = x + conv4_in\n        x = nn.relu(x)\n        \n        # Flatten and MLP Backbone\n        x = x.reshape((batch_size, -1))\n        x = nn.Dense(features=self.mlp_features)(x) \n        x = nn.relu(x)\n        \n        # LSTM Layer\n        lstm_cell = nn.LSTMCell(\n            features=self.lstm_features,\n            kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform(),\n            recurrent_kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform()\n        )\n        (rnn_state['c'], rnn_state['h']), lstm_out = lstm_cell((rnn_state['c'], rnn_state['h']), x)\n        \n        # Post-LSTM MLP and Output Heads\n        x = nn.Dense(features=128)(lstm_out)\n\
    \        x = nn.relu(x)\n        \n        policy_logits = nn.Dense(features=4)(x)\n        value = nn.Dense(features=1)(x).squeeze(-1)\n        \n        return policy_logits, value, rnn_state\n```\n\n### 2.3. Training Methodology\n\nAll experiments utilized a Policy Gradient algorithm, specifically an Advantage Actor-Critic (A2C) variant, as implemented in the system's default `training_step` function. The key hyperparameters, found to be robust and effective in Evaluation ID 14 and subsequent experiments, were:\n*   **Learning Rate:** 4e-4 (Adam optimizer with epsilon 1e-5)\n*   **Entropy Coefficient:** 0.01\n*   **Value Loss Coefficient:** 0.5\n*   **Gamma (Discount Factor):** 0.97\n*   **GAE Lambda (Advantage Estimation):** 0.97\n\nTraining was distributed across 4 parallel seeds for statistical robustness, each running for a maximum of 50 million environment steps or 30 minutes. The final score reported for each evaluation is the mean solve rate across these 4 seeds. All agents\
    \ started from random initialization, and no pretrained models or external resources were used, adhering to the station's academic integrity guidelines.\n\n## 3. Results\n\nOur systematic architectural exploration involved eight distinct experiments, each building upon insights from previous evaluations. A summary of all experiments, their architectural configurations, and mean solve rates is presented in Table 1.\n\n**Table 1: Summary of Experimental Evaluations and Results**\n\n| Evaluation ID | Architecture Description                                   | Key Parameters Modified          | Mean Solve Rate | Std. Dev. | Notes                                |\n|---------------|------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------|-----------------|-----------|--------------------------------------|\n| 1             | System CNN + Policy Gradient (Baseline)                    | N/A                              | 0.250           | N/A       | System\
    \ baseline                      |\n| 2             | System RNN + Policy Gradient (Baseline)                    | N/A                              | 0.408           | 0.022     | System baseline (RNN superior)       |\n| 4             | Custom CNN with Increased Features (48, 96)              | `cnn_features_1=48`, `cnn_features_2=96` | 0.291           | 0.011     | Improved over System CNN             |\n| 14            | Custom CNNLSTM (48, 96 CNN; 256 LSTM)                      | `lstm_features=256`              | **0.462**       | 0.008     | **New High Score: LSTM's importance**|\n| 26            | Hyperparameter Tuning for Eval ID 14                       | `lr`, `entropy_coef`, `value_loss_coef` tuned | 0.382           | 0.071     | Broader search reduced mean perf.    |\n| 38            | Custom CNNLSTM (48, 96 CNN; 512 LSTM)                      | `lstm_features=512`              | 0.452           | 0.009     | Increased LSTM width not beneficial  |\n| 51            | Custom\
    \ CNNLSTM (64, 128 CNN; 256 LSTM)                     | `cnn_features_1=64`, `cnn_features_2=128` | **0.479**       | 0.023     | **New High Score: Richer CNN features** |\n| 64            | Custom CNNLSTM (64, 128 CNN; 256 LSTM; 512 Pre-LSTM MLP)   | `mlp_features=512` (pre-LSTM)    | **0.491**       | 0.013     | **New High Score: Wider Pre-LSTM MLP** |\n| 73            | Custom CNNLSTM (64, 128 CNN; 256 LSTM; Deeper Pre-LSTM MLP) | `mlp_dense1=512`, `mlp_dense2=256` | 0.446           | 0.010     | Deeper Pre-LSTM MLP not beneficial   |\n| 82            | Custom CNNLSTM (64, 128 CNN; 256 LSTM; 256 Post-LSTM MLP)  | `post_lstm_mlp_features=256`     | 0.476           | 0.022     | Wider Post-LSTM MLP not beneficial   |\n\n### 3.1. Detailed Analysis\n\n**Initial Baselines and CNN Improvement (Eval IDs 1, 2, 4):** The system's CNN baseline (ID 1) achieved a solve rate of 0.250. The RNN baseline (ID 2), with its temporal memory, significantly outperformed it at 0.408, indicating the task's\
    \ planning demands. Our initial custom CNN (ID 4), with moderately increased feature maps (48 and 96), showed a modest improvement to 0.291, suggesting that architectural capacity in the convolutional layers could be beneficial.\n\n**The Impact of LSTM (Eval ID 14):** Integrating an LSTM layer with 256 features into our custom CNN (ID 14) resulted in a substantial performance leap to a mean solve rate of **0.462**. This clearly demonstrated that explicit temporal memory processing is critical for navigating the sequential dependencies of the Sokoban puzzle, far surpassing the capabilities of purely feedforward CNNs. This established our first high-performing architecture.\n\n**Hyperparameter Sensitivity (Eval ID 26):** A broad hyperparameter tuning search for the Eval ID 14 architecture yielded an average score of 0.382. While one seed achieved a respectable 0.450, the overall mean decreased, indicating that the initial fixed hyperparameters were quite robust, and a wide search space\
    \ introduced less optimal or unstable configurations. This suggests a need for more constrained tuning or a deeper understanding of parameter interactions.\n\n**LSTM Capacity Exploration (Eval ID 38):** Doubling the LSTM feature size from 256 to 512 (ID 38) resulted in a slight performance reduction to 0.452. This was a critical negative finding, indicating that simply increasing the width of the LSTM layer does not necessarily translate to improved temporal reasoning and may introduce unnecessary complexity or make optimization more challenging.\n\n**Enhanced CNN Spatial Features (Eval ID 51):** Building on the understanding that LSTM capacity alone wasn't the bottleneck, we further increased the CNN feature maps (to 64 and 128). This modification (ID 51) led to a new high score of **0.479**. This result confirmed that providing a richer and more detailed initial spatial representation significantly benefits the subsequent temporal processing by the LSTM, leading to more effective planning.\n\
    \n**Wider Pre-LSTM MLP Backbone (Eval ID 64):** The next successful architectural refinement involved increasing the feature size of the MLP backbone, which processes the flattened CNN features before the LSTM, from 256 to 512 units (ID 64). This resulted in our highest mean solve rate of **0.491**. This indicates that a more abstract and refined representation of the spatial features, prior to temporal integration, further aids the LSTM in its planning capabilities.\n\n**Deeper Pre-LSTM MLP and Post-LSTM MLP (Eval IDs 73, 82):** Attempts to further increase complexity by adding a second layer to the pre-LSTM MLP (ID 73) resulted in a performance drop to 0.446. Similarly, increasing the width of the MLP after the LSTM (ID 82) also decreased performance to 0.476. These are crucial negative results, collectively demonstrating that architectural depth and unconstrained capacity increases in certain parts of the network can be detrimental. They highlight the delicate balance required for\
    \ optimal performance and suggest that beyond a certain point, adding more parameters without targeted structural changes introduces redundancy or makes the optimization landscape more difficult.\n\n---\n\n**Message archive_3-2 from Capsule 'Architectural Design and Ablation for CNN-LSTM Agents in Sokoban Reinforcement Learning':**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 62)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 8/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is a solid paper that has been successfully revised to meet all publication criteria. The research presents a thorough and systematic exploration of a CNN-LSTM architecture, providing valuable insights into the importance of balanced network capacity. The paper is well-written, the experiments are rigorously documented with verifiable IDs, and the inclusion of a code snippet ensures reproducibility. The analysis of both successful and unsuccessful architectural modifications makes this a strong and useful contribution\
    \ to the station's knowledge base. The agent has done an excellent job of addressing the previous review's feedback.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe paper is strong and acceptable as is. For a minor improvement in readability and structure, consider re-adding a short, dedicated 'Conclusion' section at the end. The previous version's conclusion was well-written and effectively summarized the key takeaways. Reinstating it would provide a more formal and complete structure to the paper, giving readers a concise summary of the main findings without introducing any new content or violating any rules.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n\n---\n\n**Message archive_3-3 from Capsule 'Architectural Design and Ablation for CNN-LSTM Agents in Sokoban Reinforcement Learning':**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Cognito I (Tick 69)\n\n---\n\nTitle: 4. Conclusion\n\n---\n\nContent:\n## 4. Conclusion\n\nThis research systematically explored\
    \ architectural configurations for CNN-LSTM agents tackling the Sokoban reinforcement learning task. Our iterative approach, spanning nine experiments, successfully identified a `CustomCNNLSTM` architecture (Eval ID 111) that achieved a mean solve rate of 0.503, representing a significant improvement over system baselines. The key insight from this study is the critical balance between different forms of network capacity: enhanced spatial feature extraction (CNN layers) and a sufficiently wide intermediate MLP backbone (pre-LSTM) consistently improved performance when combined with an LSTM for temporal reasoning. Conversely, simply increasing the LSTM's width, the MLP backbone's depth, or the post-LSTM MLP's width proved to be ineffective or even detrimental, highlighting the nuanced relationship between architectural complexity and task performance. These findings provide a valuable foundation for designing efficient and effective RL agents for planning-heavy tasks.\n\n### Message 4\n\
    \n**Message archive_8-9 from Capsule 'Non-FE Bottleneck-Dilated CNN + Single-Step LN-ConvLSTM Achieves 73.3–74.8 on 8×8 Sokoban (Policy Gradient)':**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick 121)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nUpdate (Tick 121):\n- Ratio ablation at 48/96 (ReLU) indicates 0.6 > 0.5 at seed 42: ID 211 = 76.3 (vs. ID 203 = 76.0 at 0.5).\n- Replications launched for 48/96 @ 0.6 (ReLU) at seeds 7 and 123 to test stability. If {42,7,123} mean ≥ the 0.5 config (current mean ≈75.6), we will update the recommended default accordingly.\n- Token budget nearing 75% threshold; proceeding to prune dialogue context while preserving results summaries and archive records.\n\n\n---\n\n**Message archive_8-10 from Capsule 'Non-FE Bottleneck-Dilated CNN + Single-Step LN-ConvLSTM Achieves 73.3–74.8 on 8×8 Sokoban (Policy Gradient)':**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick 123)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nUpdate (Tick 123): 48/96 width, ratio=0.6 (ReLU) supersedes 0.5 as the non‑FE default.\n- New results: ID 211 (seed\
    \ 42) = 76.3, ID 214 (seed 7) = 76.4, ID 215 (seed 123) = 77.8\n- Aggregate over {42,7,123}: ≈76.8 vs 75.6 for ratio=0.5 (IDs 203,208,210)\n- Recommended script updated: storage/zephyr/submissions/submission_nonfe_recommended.py\n- Summary updated: storage/zephyr/results/summary_bottleneck.txt\n\nNext: launched two double‑step tests on top of the improved backbone:\n1) Shared double‑step with learned mix (BASE_SEED=42)\n2) Same but with stop‑gradient from step1→step2 (BASE_SEED=123)\nThese target synergy with minimal added complexity under the 30‑min cap.\n\n\n---\n\n**Message archive_8-11 from Capsule 'Non-FE Bottleneck-Dilated CNN + Single-Step LN-ConvLSTM Achieves 73.3–74.8 on 8×8 Sokoban (Policy Gradient)':**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick 127)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nUpdate (Tick 127):\n- Compute‑equalized double‑step + single‑head attention pooling (ConvLSTM 48ch, 48/96 d=5, ratio=0.6) achieved 79.5 at seed 123 (ID 224), exceeding our single‑step non‑FE default (76–78).\n- Replications\
    \ launched at seeds 42 and 7 to establish stability. If the {42,7,123} mean ≥ 79, we’ll propose this as a stronger recurrent default under the 30‑min cap.\n- Note: removing AttnPool dropped seed‑42 to 72.2 (ID 222), indicating attention pooling is critical for the double‑step variant. Earlier wide 64ch double‑step variants underperformed due to update budget; compute‑equalization (48ch) recovered throughput (≈37–38k updates).\n\n\n---\n\n**Message archive_8-12 from Capsule 'Non-FE Bottleneck-Dilated CNN + Single-Step LN-ConvLSTM Achieves 73.3–74.8 on 8×8 Sokoban (Policy Gradient)':**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick 129)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nUpdate (Tick 129): Double‑step + AttnPool surpasses single‑step.\n\nNew results (compute‑equalized, ConvLSTM=48ch):\n- ID 225 (seed 42): 78.8\n- ID 226 (seed 7):  75.0\n- ID 224 (seed 123): 79.5\nAggregate ≈ 77.8 vs single‑step (IDs 211,214,215) ≈ 76.8 → +1.0 pp.\n\nNotes:\n- Without AttnPool the same double‑step model drops to 72.2 (ID 222), confirming\
    \ attention pooling is essential for the two‑step readout under the 30‑min cap.\n- Recommended non‑FE default updated to double‑step + single‑head AttnPool; repro: storage/zephyr/submissions/submission_recommended_double_step_attnpool.py\n- Running micro‑ablations: attention temperature {0.9,1.0,1.1} and stop_gradient {False,True}. If gains hold, we will finalize an addendum with tables (IDs & mean±std) and brief analysis.\n\n\n---\n\n**Message archive_8-13 from Capsule 'Non-FE Bottleneck-Dilated CNN + Single-Step LN-ConvLSTM Achieves 73.3–74.8 on 8×8 Sokoban (Policy Gradient)':**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick 131)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAddendum data (Tick 131):\n- Attn temperature micro‑ablation (ID 228): mean 77.8; best at temp=1.0 (best trial 0.810). => Keep temp=1.0.\n- stop_gradient ablation (ID 229): mean 59.7; best trial uses stop_grad=False (0.748). => Stop‑grad degrades performance; keep gradients flowing step1→step2.\n- Submitted width scaling for double‑step+AttnPool: 56/112\
    \ with ratio=0.6 and 0.5 (IDs pending). Goal is to probe whether modest widening with bottleneck preserves throughput and lifts solve rate beyond 79.5.\n\n\n---\n\n**Message archive_8-14 from Capsule 'Non-FE Bottleneck-Dilated CNN + Single-Step LN-ConvLSTM Achieves 73.3–74.8 on 8×8 Sokoban (Policy Gradient)':**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick 133)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nAddendum update (Tick 133):\n- Wider CNN tests for double‑step+AttnPool (ConvLSTM 48):\n  • ID 231 (56/112, ratio=0.6) = 75.0 with ~33k updates → widening reduced throughput and score.\n  • ID 232 (56/112, ratio=0.5) = 77.7 with ~35–36k updates → matches baseline but no gain.\n- Conclusion: modest widening at 56/112 does not improve over 48/96; compute budget sensitivity is pronounced.\n- Ongoing:\n  • 60/120 with ratio=0.5 (submitted) to probe a slightly larger regime with stronger bottleneck.\n  • ConvLSTM width ablation {44,48,52} at 48/96 to check if 52ch helps without harming updates.\n- Confirmed choices (IDs 228,\
    \ 229): attn_temp=1.0; no stop‑grad between steps.\n\n\n---\n\n**Message archive_8-15 from Capsule 'Non-FE Bottleneck-Dilated CNN + Single-Step LN-ConvLSTM Achieves 73.3–74.8 on 8×8 Sokoban (Policy Gradient)':**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick 135)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nUpdate (Tick 135):\n- Width/ratio results: 56/112@0.6 = 75.0 (ID 231), 56/112@0.5 = 77.7 (ID 232), 60/120@0.5 = 76.5 (ID 234). ConvLSTM width {44,48,52} peaked at 48ch (ID 235 = 76.5 best trial 0.790 at 48ch).\n- Conclusion: 48/96 CNN with ratio=0.6 and ConvLSTM 48 remains optimal under the 30‑min cap; wider CNNs reduce update throughput and do not improve mean solve rate.\n- Next: launched two PG micro‑sweeps on the recommended backbone (LR×entropy×vcoef and a narrow fine sweep). IDs pending; will report mean±std and best configs on completion.\n\n### Message 5\n\n**Message archive_10-1 from Capsule 'Addendum (Non-FE): Compute-Equalized Double-Step LN-ConvLSTM + Single-Head AttnPool':**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick\
    \ 136)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Addendum (Non-FE): Compute-Equalized Double-Step LN-ConvLSTM + Single-Head AttnPool\n\n---\n\nContent:\n1) Setup (Station constraints)\n- Env: Sokoban 8×8; 4 seeds/experiment; ≤50M steps or 30 min/seed; 64 envs; unroll=20.\n- Training: default PG (GAE, Adam 4e‑4). No pretraining, no FE, no domain heuristics.\n\n2) Method\n- Backbone: Bottleneck‑dilated CNN, dilation=5, widths 48/96.\n- Recurrent: LN‑gated ConvLSTM, shared weights, double‑step per time step; width=48 (compute‑equalized).\n- Readout: single‑head spatial attention pooling (temperature=1.0).\nRationale: Two recurrent steps improve short‑horizon planning; AttnPool focuses spatial credit; width=48 recovers update count under the 30‑min cap.\n\n3) Main results (means across the 4 seeds of each evaluation)\n- Double‑step + AttnPool (compute‑equalized, 48ch):\n  • ID 225 (BASE_SEED=42): 78.8\n  • ID 226 (BASE_SEED=7):  75.0\n  • ID 224 (BASE_SEED=123): 79.5\n  Aggregate (across {42,7,123} per‑eval means):\
    \ 77.8\n- Single‑step baseline (48/96, d=5, ratio=0.6, ConvLSTM 64):\n  • ID 211 (42): 76.3; ID 214 (7): 76.4; ID 215 (123): 77.8\n  Aggregate: 76.8\n- Ablation — double‑step WITHOUT AttnPool:\n  • ID 222 (42): 72.2  → AttnPool is essential for two‑step readout.\n\n4) Micro‑ablations (double‑step+AttnPool, 48/96, ratio=0.6, ConvLSTM 48)\n- Attention temperature {0.9,1.0,1.1}:\n  • ID 228 mean: 77.8; best temp=1.0 (best trial 0.810)\n- Stop‑gradient step1→step2:\n  • ID 229 mean: 59.7; best trial uses stop_grad=False (0.748) → stop‑grad hurts.\n\n5) Width/ratio sensitivity (double‑step+AttnPool, ConvLSTM 48)\n- 56/112, ratio=0.6: ID 231 = 75.0 (≈33k updates; slower)\n- 56/112, ratio=0.5: ID 232 = 77.7 (≈35–36k updates; near baseline)\n- 60/120, ratio=0.5: ID 234 = 76.5\n- ConvLSTM width {44,48,52} @ 48/96 (ratio=0.6): ID 235 mean 76.5; best trial 0.790 occurs at 48ch → 48ch remains optimal.\n\n6) Conclusion and Recommendation (non‑FE)\n- Recommended config under 30‑min cap:\n  • CNN 48/96,\
    \ dilation=5, bottleneck_ratio=0.6\n  • LN‑ConvLSTM width=48, double‑step (shared), NO stop‑grad\n  • Single‑head AttnPool, temperature=1.0\n  • Default PG (GAE, Adam lr=4e‑4, entropy=0.01, value_coef=0.5)\n- Expected mean: ~77.8 (IDs 225, 226, 224)\n- Without AttnPool, performance collapses (ID 222), confirming the necessity of focused spatial readout.\n- Modest widening reduces update throughput; ratio=0.5 recovers some speed but does not surpass the 48/96 baseline.\n\n7) Reproducibility\n- Ready‑to‑run script: storage/zephyr/submissions/submission_recommended_double_step_attnpool.py\n- Source: storage/zephyr/networks/cnn_convlstm_layernorm_double_step_dilated_bottleneck.py\n- Summary: storage/zephyr/results/summary_bottleneck.txt\n\n8) Ongoing (Track‑2) PG micro‑sweeps on the recommended backbone\n- ID 237: LR×entropy×value_coef grid (lr ∈ {3e‑4,4e‑4,5e‑4}, entropy ∈ {0.008,0.01,0.012}, vcoef ∈ {0.45,0.5,0.55})\n- ID 238: Narrow fine‑sweep around default (lr ∈ {3.6e‑4,4.0e‑4,4.4e‑4},\
    \ entropy ∈ {0.009,0.010,0.011}, vcoef ∈ {0.48,0.50,0.52})\nWe will append results with IDs upon completion.\n\n\n---\n\n**Message archive_10-2 from Capsule 'Addendum (Non-FE): Compute-Equalized Double-Step LN-ConvLSTM + Single-Head AttnPool':**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 136)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 10/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is an outstanding submission that serves as a new benchmark for scientific rigor in the station. The paper's contribution, while incremental, is validated with an exceptionally thorough and meticulous set of experiments, including multiple replications of both the proposed method and the baseline. The extensive ablations provide deep and valuable insights. The paper's clarity, conciseness, and scientific honesty are exemplary. It is a model of how to build high-confidence knowledge and is a significant contribution to the station.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe paper is excellent and ready\
    \ for publication. You are already pursuing the logical next step with the Track-2 hyperparameter sweeps. Once that is complete, a compelling direction would be to attempt a synthesis with the current SOTA. Your work has created a more powerful and sample-efficient recurrent block. Investigating if this block can be integrated into the wider CNN architectures from papers like Krono I's (Archive #9) could potentially unify the station's leading research tracks and push for a new absolute state-of-the-art.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n\n---\n\n**Message archive_10-3 from Capsule 'Addendum (Non-FE): Compute-Equalized Double-Step LN-ConvLSTM + Single-Head AttnPool':**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick 138)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nTrack‑2 updates (IDs now appended):\n- Broad grid on PG hparams (ID 237): mean 77.2; best (lr=5e‑4, ent=0.012, vcoef=0.5) reached 0.818 best‑seed but did not surpass 77.8 mean of our recommended\
    \ config.\n- Narrow fine sweep (ID 238): mean 74.9; best at entropy=0.009.\nConclusion: Default PG hparams remain competitive; minor tweaks do not beat the 48/96, ratio=0.6, ConvLSTM=48, temp=1.0 configuration. Wide‑CNN synthesis runs are in progress to probe ≥79.5 without FE.\n\n\n---\n\n**Message archive_10-4 from Capsule 'Addendum (Non-FE): Compute-Equalized Double-Step LN-ConvLSTM + Single-Head AttnPool':**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick 139)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nFollow-up (Tick 139): Wide-CNN synthesis without FE underperforms.\n- 64/128 with ratio ∈ {0.4,0.45,0.5}: ID 242 = 74.9\n- 64/128 with ratio=0.4 and ConvLSTM {44,48}: ID 243 = 75.6\n- Prior 56/112 results: 77.7 @ ratio=0.5 and 75.0 @ ratio=0.6 (IDs 232,231)\nConclusion: widening lowers update throughput and does not beat the compute-equalized 48/96 baseline (~77.8). We have launched two PG variants on the recommended backbone: (1) Adam warmup+cosine LR schedule and (2) a fixed replicate of the best constants from ID 237\
    \ (lr=5e-4, ent=0.012, vcoef=0.5). Results will be appended with IDs when complete.\n\n\n---\n\n**Message archive_10-5 from Capsule 'Addendum (Non-FE): Compute-Equalized Double-Step LN-ConvLSTM + Single-Head AttnPool':**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick 141)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nTrack‑2 results update (Tick 141):\n- LR warmup+cosine (ID 245) yielded 70.3 → harmful under current budget.\n- Fixed replicate of best constants from the broad grid (ID 246; lr=5e‑4, ent=0.012, vcoef=0.5) achieved 77.8, matching our 48/96 double‑step+AttnPool baseline mean (~77.8).\n- Conclusion: baseline PG constants remain competitive; minor constant shifts do not exceed 77.8. We have submitted an AdamW+clip+cosine variant to test whether schedule+wd+clip can help; if not, we will lock the recommendation and end the Track‑2 exploration here.\n\n\n---\n\n**Message archive_10-6 from Capsule 'Addendum (Non-FE): Compute-Equalized Double-Step LN-ConvLSTM + Single-Head AttnPool':**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick\
    \ 142)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nTick 142 update:\n- ID 246 (best constants, BASE_SEED=42) = 77.8 (matches baseline).\n- Launched replicate at BASE_SEED=7 to confirm robustness (ID pending).\n- ID 245 (Adam + warmup+cosine) = 70.3 → negative.\n- ID 247 (AdamW+clip + warmup+cosine) running; if <77.8, we’ll close Track‑2 with a negative‑results note and keep the original PG constants.\n\n\n---\n\n**Message archive_10-7 from Capsule 'Addendum (Non-FE): Compute-Equalized Double-Step LN-ConvLSTM + Single-Head AttnPool':**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick 143)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nTick 143: Closing out Track‑2 (negative schedules), pivoting to architecture synthesis per reviewer suggestion.\n- Schedules: Adam warmup+cosine (ID 245) = 70.3; AdamW+clip+cosine (ID 247) = 71.0 → both harmful.\n- Best constant PG (ID 246, lr=5e‑4, ent=0.012, vcoef=0.5) = 77.8, matching our ~77.8 baseline.\n- New direction (running): 64/128 CNN with stronger bottlenecks {0.25, 0.33} and ConvLSTM width {44,48} to recover\
    \ ≥37–38k updates while retaining our double‑step + single‑head AttnPool. If this exceeds 79.5, we’ll replicate across seeds and report.\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `read 1-7:1-8` (in Archive Room);\n2. `read 5-1:5-2` (in Archive Room);\n3. `read 3-1:3-3` (in Archive Room);\n4. `read 8-9:8-15` (in Archive Room);\n5. `read 10-1:10-7` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Read command processed for: 1-7, 1-8. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n- Read command processed for: 5-1, 5-2. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n- Read command processed for: 3-1, 3-2, 3-3. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n- Read command processed for: 8-9, 8-10, 8-11, 8-12, 8-13, 8-14, 8-15. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n- Read command processed for: 10-1, 10-2, 10-3, 10-4, 10-5, 10-6, 10-7. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page\
    \ 1 / 1)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 10 | Addendum (Non-FE): Compute-Equalized Double-Step LN-ConvLSTM + Single-Head AttnPool | Zephyr I | Tick 136 | 1032 | 8 | (1 unread) |\n| 9 | The Bottleneck Block: A New Foundation for State-of-the-Art Sokoban Agents | Krono I | Tick 128 | 681 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 8 | Non-FE Bottleneck-Dilated CNN + Single-Step LN-ConvLSTM Achieves 73.3–74.8 on 8×8 Sokoban (Policy Gradient) | Zephyr I | Tick 113 | 1679 | 15 | (All Read) |\n| 7 | Achieving State-of-the-Art Performance in Sokoban with a Computationally Efficient Bottleneck CNN | Krono I | Tick 107 | 767 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 6 | Implicit Relational Learning in Sokoban via Dilated Convolutions | Nomos I | Tick 86 | 759 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 5 | Enhancing Spatio-Temporal Reasoning in Sokoban with a Dilated CNN Backbone | Krono I | Tick 77 | 742 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 4\
    \ | Double-step LayerNorm-Gated ConvLSTM: 66–69% on 8×8 Sokoban under Policy Gradient (All Results with Evaluation IDs) | Zephyr I | Tick 73 | 2505 | 13 | (All Read) |\n| 3 | Architectural Design and Ablation for CNN-LSTM Agents in Sokoban Reinforcement Learning | Cognito I | Tick 62 | 2152 | 3 | (All Read) |\n| 2 | Architectural Exploration for Planning-Heavy Reinforcement Learning in Sokoban: A CNN-GRU Approach | Aether I | Tick 56 | 3385 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 1 | Revised: LayerNorm-Gated ConvLSTM with Single-Head Attention Achieves 62–64% Solve Rate on 8×8 Sokoban under Policy Gradient | Zephyr I | Tick 51 | 1567 | 8 | (All Read) |\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue\
