parts:
- text: "I am Aether I, and I have thoroughly reviewed Zephyr I's SOTA architecture `BottleneckD5DoubleStepStdNoDilBN_LateTime` (ID 354, score 88.00). This architecture's success, particularly with the default A2C-style training algorithm, is a critical new insight. It highlights the power of architectural design, especially the \"late dual-head time injection\" mechanism and specific dilation/bottleneck configurations, in breaking previous performance plateaus.\n\nMy previous attempts with custom attention blocks and deeper recurrence (IDs 345, 355, 359) consistently underperformed compared to established baselines, indicating that simply adding complexity or using PPO without a well-suited architecture is not effective.\n\nMy immediate priority is to **replicate Zephyr I's SOTA (ID 354)** exactly, using the provided architecture and hyperparameters, and *relying on the default A2C-style training step*. This will serve as my new high-performance baseline and will help me understand the\
    \ intricacies of time feature injection and effective spatial processing. Once this is successfully replicated, I can then systematically explore combining this architecture with PPO and, much later, re-evaluate my `SelfAttentionBlock` with this new architectural understanding.\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Replication of Zephyr I's SOTA (88.0) - Late Dual-Head Time Injection (Aether I)\"\ntags: \"replication, sota, zephyr, time-injection, architecture, baseline\"\nabstract: \"This submission is a direct replication of Zephyr I's current SOTA architecture, `BottleneckD5DoubleStepStdNoDilBN_LateTime` (Evaluation ID 354, score 88.0). It includes all custom network components, hyperparameters (dilation=6, bottleneck_ratio=0.25), and relies on the default A2C-style training step. The goal is to establish a high-performance baseline for Aether I, accurately reproduce the 'late dual-head time injection' mechanism, and understand its contribution to the improved solve rate\
    \ for the Sokoban task.\"\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from ray import tune\n  import optax\n  from typing import Optional, Dict, Any, Tuple\n  import time\n\n  # Zephyr I's BASE_SEED from SOTA (ID 354) for direct replication\n  BASE_SEED = 123\n\n  # --- Zephyr I's ConvLSTMCellLN ---\n  class ConvLSTMCellLN(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, carry, x):\n          h, c = carry\n          gates = nn.Conv(4 * self.features, (3, 3), padding='SAME',\n                          kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(\n              jnp.concatenate([x, h], axis=-1)\n          )\n          gates = nn.LayerNorm(epsilon=1e-5)(gates)\n          i, f, g, o = jnp.split(gates, 4, axis=-1)\n          i, f, o = nn.sigmoid(i), nn.sigmoid(f), nn.sigmoid(o)\n          g = jnp.tanh(g)\n          new_c = f * c + i * g\n          new_h = o * jnp.tanh(new_c)\n          return (new_h, new_c), new_h\n\n  #\
    \ --- Zephyr I's BottleneckBlock ---\n  class BottleneckBlock(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      ratio: float = 0.25\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          mid = max(1, int(self.features * self.ratio))\n          y = nn.Conv(mid, (1,1), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(x)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          # Undilated 3x3 inside bottleneck (keep locality/compute)\n          y = nn.Conv(mid, (3,3), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(y)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(self.features, (1,1), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(y)\n          return nn.relu(x + y)\n\n  # --- Zephyr I's BottleneckD5DoubleStepStdNoDilBN_LateTime Network ---\n  class BottleneckD5DoubleStepStdNoDilBN_LateTime(nn.Module):\n      cnn_features_1: int = 32\n      cnn_features_2: int = 64\n      conv_lstm_features: int = 64\n\
    \      dilation: int = 5\n      bottleneck_ratio: float = 0.25\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x: jnp.ndarray, done: Optional[jnp.ndarray], rnn_state: Optional[Dict] = None):\n          # Split spatial (first 7 channels) vs time (last channel)\n          x_spat = x[..., :7]\n          t = jnp.mean(x[..., 7:8], axis=(1,2))  # (B,1)\n\n          B, H, W, _ = x_spat.shape\n\n          # CNN backbone: dilation on main convs only\n          x = nn.Conv(self.cnn_features_1, (3,3), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_dilation=(self.dilation, self.dilation),\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(x_spat)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = BottleneckBlock(self.cnn_features_1, ratio=self.bottleneck_ratio)(x)\n\n          x = nn.Conv(self.cnn_features_2, (3,3), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_dilation=(self.dilation, self.dilation),\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n\
    \          x = BottleneckBlock(self.cnn_features_2, ratio=self.bottleneck_ratio)(x)\n\n          # RNN state initialization for ConvLSTMCellLN, which expects a tuple (h,c)\n          if rnn_state is None:\n              zeros = lambda: jnp.zeros((B, H, W, self.conv_lstm_features), dtype=x.dtype)\n              rnn_state = {'h': zeros(), 'c': zeros()}\n          if done is not None:\n              m = (1.0 - done.reshape(B,1,1,1).astype(x.dtype))\n              rnn_state = {'h': rnn_state['h'] * m, 'c': rnn_state['c'] * m}\n\n          # Double-step ConvLSTM (shared cell, same CNN features each step)\n          cell = ConvLSTMCellLN(self.conv_lstm_features)\n          # Pass (h,c) tuple to ConvLSTMCellLN\n          (h1, c1), _ = cell((rnn_state['h'], rnn_state['c']), x)\n          (h2, c2), h_out = cell((h1, c1), x)\n          new_rnn_state_dict = {'h': h2, 'c': c2} # Return as dict as expected by system\n\n          # Single-head attention pooling over spatial tokens\n          attn_logits\
    \ = nn.Conv(1, (1,1), padding='SAME',\n                                kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(h_out)  # (B,H,W,1)\n          attn = nn.softmax(attn_logits.reshape(B, -1), axis=-1)  # (B, H*W)\n          h_flat = h_out.reshape(B, -1, h_out.shape[-1])          # (B, H*W, C)\n          z_spat = jnp.einsum('bnc,bn->bc', h_flat, attn)         # (B, C)\n\n          # Late time injection (append t after spatial pooling)\n          z = jnp.concatenate([z_spat, t], axis=-1)               # (B, C+1)\n\n          # Heads\n          z = nn.Dense(256, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(z); z = nn.relu(z)\n          pol_h = nn.Dense(64, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(z); pol_h = nn.relu(pol_h)\n          policy_logits = nn.Dense(4, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(pol_h)\n          val_h = nn.Dense(64, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(z); val_h = nn.relu(val_h)\n          value = nn.Dense(1, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(val_h).squeeze(-1)\n\
    \          return policy_logits, value, new_rnn_state_dict\n\n\n  # --- Hyperparameters ---\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': tune.choice([4e-4]),\n          'entropy_coef': tune.choice([0.01]),\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.choice([0.5]),\n          'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([32]),\n          'cnn_features_2': tune.choice([64]),\n          'conv_lstm_features': tune.choice([64]),\n          'dilation': tune.choice([6]),\n          'bottleneck_ratio': tune.choice([0.25]),\n      }\n\n  # --- Network and Optimizer Creation ---\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return BottleneckD5DoubleStepStdNoDilBN_LateTime(\n          cnn_features_1=hparams['cnn_features_1'],\n          cnn_features_2=hparams['cnn_features_2'],\n          conv_lstm_features=hparams['conv_lstm_features'],\n          dilation=hparams['dilation'],\n          bottleneck_ratio=hparams['bottleneck_ratio'],\n      )\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 4e-4):\n\
    \      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=1e-4),\n      )\n\n  # Note: Zephyr I's SOTA uses the default training_step, so no custom one is provided here.\n```"
thinking_content: '**Aether I''s Analysis of Zephyr I''s SOTA Architecture**


  Okay, so I''ve just finished a deep dive into Zephyr I''s new SOTA, architecture ID 354, and wow, it''s pretty impressive. An 88.0 score is a significant jump, especially considering my own attempts have been falling short. Let''s break down what''s really catching my eye here.


  First off, the *observation space split* is smart - explicitly separating the spatial features (`x_spat`) from the time feature (`t`) makes a lot of sense. The *dilated CNN backbone* with `dilation=6` is also intriguing. I''ve played with dilations before, but the fact that the `BottleneckBlock`s *within* the CNN are undilated is probably a key detail.  It seems the double-step ConvLSTM, similar to what Krono II uses, is also important. The use of `ConvLSTMCellLN` with `xavier_uniform` initializers is standard practice, but it''s another detail to keep in mind.


  The biggest "Aha!" moment is definitely the *late time injection*.  Separating `t`, mean-pooling it, and then concatenating it back into the feature vector *after* all the CNN, ConvLSTM, and attention pooling processing? That''s novel, and seems crucial for injecting the time information effectively.  This late-fusion approach is what probably gives it its "edge". The Xavier uniform initializers used for all layers is also a detail to remember.


  The most astonishing part? This new SOTA is using the *default* training step, which likely means A2C-style from `system/defaults.py`! This is mind-blowing. I had assumed PPO was necessary for this level of complexity, since Krono II uses PPO. My previous attempts with my custom architectures and PPO (or default PG) have not done as well.  This means the architecture itself is exceptionally well-suited for A2C, or perhaps there is a synergistic relationship. This really flips my assumptions on their head.


  My immediate plan is clear.  Step one: **replicate Zephyr I''s SOTA exactly, using the default A2C training**. This is crucial for verifying the results and understanding how this architecture truly performs within a standard training framework.  I need to prepare all the code, including the custom modules, to make it self-contained and reproducible. After that, I''ll start experimenting with the combination of Zephyr''s architecture with PPO. I''m eager to see if this architecture does better than my previous attempts with PPO.

  '
