parts:
- text: "I am Aether I, currently in the Archive Room. I have just read Nomos II's paper \"On the Brittleness of PPO Agents to MLP Head Complexity\" (Archive #12), which provides a critical insight into my recent synergy test failures. The paper conclusively demonstrates that PPO agents perform best with simple, shallow MLP heads, even when the feature extraction backbone is complex. My previous attempts to combine Zephyr I's architecture (which uses a \"Deeper Head\") with Krono II's PPO algorithm likely failed due to this incompatibility. The FiLM time injection (ID 379, 70.2) also suffered from this.\n\nNomos II's \"Late Injection Hybrid\" (ID 351, score 86.1) is a direct demonstration of combining a high-performing architecture with a *Simple Head* under PPO. This is the crucial design principle I must now apply.\n\nMy immediate next step is to submit an experiment that combines Zephyr I's SOTA architecture's **feature extraction backbone** with a **\"Simple Head\"** (as described by\
    \ Nomos II's paper) and Krono II's **PPO algorithm**. This directly addresses the identified incompatibility and is highly likely to yield a significant performance increase, potentially leading to a new SOTA.\n\nI will go to the Research Counter and submit this modified architecture.\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n\n`/execute_action{submit 1}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Synergy Test: Zephyr's SOTA Arch (Simple MLP Head) + Krono's PPO Algo (Aether I)\"\ntags: \"sota-synergy, ppo, time-injection, agent-architecture, simple-mlp-head, bugfix\"\nabstract: \"This submission aims to achieve a new SOTA by combining Zephyr I's `BottleneckD5DoubleStepStdNoDilBN_LateTime` architecture with Krono II's PPO algorithm, directly addressing the incompatibility identified in Nomos II's paper (Archive #12). The core modification is replacing the 'Deeper Head' of Zephyr's architecture with a 'Simple Head' (shared `Dense(256)` followed by direct policy/value linear layers). This leverages Zephyr's effective\
    \ time injection and spatial processing, while adhering to the PPO's requirement for simple MLP heads, aiming to significantly improve performance over previous synergy tests (ID 369, 86.4; ID 375, 85.3) and surpass the current SOTA of 88.0.\"\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from jax import jit, vmap, lax\n  import optax\n  from ray import tune\n  from typing import Optional, Dict, Any, Tuple\n  import time\n\n  # Zephyr I's BASE_SEED from SOTA (ID 354) for architectural baseline\n  BASE_SEED = 123 \n\n  # --- Zephyr I's ConvLSTMCellLN (from ID 354) ---\n  class ConvLSTMCellLN(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, carry, x):\n          h, c = carry\n          gates = nn.Conv(4 * self.features, (3, 3), padding='SAME',\n                          kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(\n              jnp.concatenate([x, h], axis=-1)\n          )\n          gates = nn.LayerNorm(epsilon=1e-5)(gates)\n\
    \          i, f, g, o = jnp.split(gates, 4, axis=-1)\n          i, f, o = nn.sigmoid(i), nn.sigmoid(f), nn.sigmoid(o)\n          g = jnp.tanh(g)\n          new_c = f * c + i * g\n          new_h = o * jnp.tanh(new_c)\n          return (new_h, new_c), new_h\n\n  # --- Zephyr I's BottleneckBlock (from ID 354) ---\n  class BottleneckBlock(nn.Module):\n      features: int\n      ratio: float = 0.25\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          mid = max(1, int(self.features * self.ratio))\n          y = nn.Conv(mid, (1,1), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(x)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(mid, (3,3), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(y)\n          y = nn.relu(y)\n          y = nn.Conv(self.features, (1,1), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(y)\n          return nn.relu(x + y)\n\n  # --- Zephyr I's BottleneckD5DoubleStepStdNoDilBN_LateTime\
    \ Network (from ID 354, MODIFIED MLP HEAD) ---\n  class BottleneckD5DoubleStepStdNoDilBN_LateTime(nn.Module):\n      cnn_features_1: int = 32\n      cnn_features_2: int = 64\n      conv_lstm_features: int = 64\n      dilation: int = 5\n      bottleneck_ratio: float = 0.25\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x: jnp.ndarray, done: Optional[jnp.ndarray], rnn_state: Optional[Dict] = None):\n          x_spat = x[..., :7]\n          t = jnp.mean(x[..., 7:8], axis=(1,2)) # (B,1)\n\n          B, H, W, _ = x_spat.shape\n\n          x = nn.Conv(self.cnn_features_1, (3,3), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_dilation=(self.dilation, self.dilation),\n                      kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(x_spat)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = BottleneckBlock(self.cnn_features_1, ratio=self.bottleneck_ratio)(x)\n\n          x = nn.Conv(self.cnn_features_2, (3,3), padding='SAME',\n                      kernel_dilation=(self.dilation, self.dilation),\n  \
    \                    kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = BottleneckBlock(self.cnn_features_2, ratio=self.bottleneck_ratio)(x)\n\n          if rnn_state is None:\n              zeros = lambda: jnp.zeros((B, H, W, self.conv_lstm_features), dtype=x.dtype)\n              rnn_state = {'h': zeros(), 'c': zeros()}\n          if done is not None:\n              m = (1.0 - done.reshape(B,1,1,1).astype(x.dtype))\n              rnn_state = {'h': rnn_state['h'] * m, 'c': rnn_state['c'] * m}\n\n          cell = ConvLSTMCellLN(self.conv_lstm_features)\n          (h1, c1), _ = cell((rnn_state['h'], rnn_state['c']), x)\n          (h2, c2), h_out = cell((h1, c1), x)\n          new_rnn_state_dict = {'h': h2, 'c': c2}\n\n          attn_logits = nn.Conv(1, (1,1), padding='SAME',\n                                kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(h_out)\n          attn = nn.softmax(attn_logits.reshape(B, -1), axis=-1)\n          h_flat = h_out.reshape(B,\
    \ -1, h_out.shape[-1])\n          z_spat = jnp.einsum('bnc,bn->bc', h_flat, attn)\n\n          z = jnp.concatenate([z_spat, t], axis=-1)\n\n          # MODIFIED: Simple MLP Heads as per Nomos II's findings (Archive #12)\n          z = nn.Dense(256, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(z); z = nn.relu(z)\n          policy_logits = nn.Dense(4, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(z) # Direct from shared MLP\n          value = nn.Dense(1, kernel_init=nn.initializers.xavier_uniform())(z).squeeze(-1) # Direct from shared MLP\n          return policy_logits, value, new_rnn_state_dict\n\n  # --- Krono II's PPO Training Step (from ID 312 replication) ---\n  @jit\n  def calculate_gae(\n      rewards: jnp.ndarray,\n      values: jnp.ndarray,\n      dones: jnp.ndarray,\n      gamma: float = 0.97,\n      gae_lambda: float = 0.97,\n      next_values: jnp.ndarray = None,\n  ) -> jnp.ndarray:\n      assert next_values is not None, \"next_values is required for bootstrapping\"\n  \
    \    values_t_plus_1 = jnp.concatenate([values[:, 1:], next_values[:, None]], axis=1)\n      not_done_mask = (1.0 - dones.astype(jnp.float32))\n      deltas = rewards + gamma * values_t_plus_1 * not_done_mask - values\n      def scan_fn(advantage_t_plus_1, t):\n          delta_t = deltas[:, t]\n          not_done_t = not_done_mask[:, t]\n          advantage_t = delta_t + gamma * gae_lambda * not_done_t * advantage_t_plus_1\n          return advantage_t, advantage_t\n      _, advantages = lax.scan(\n          scan_fn, jnp.zeros(values.shape[0]), jnp.arange(values.shape[1] - 1, -1, -1),\n      )\n      advantages = advantages[::-1].T\n      return advantages\n\n  def training_step(\n      network: Any,\n      optimizer: Any,\n      params: Any,\n      opt_state: Any,\n      batch: Dict[str, jnp.ndarray],\n      hparams: Dict[str, Any],\n  ) -> Tuple[Any, Any]:\n      observations = batch['observations']; actions = batch['actions']\n      rewards = batch['rewards']; dones = batch['dones']\n\
    \      old_values = batch['values']; old_log_probs = batch['log_probs']\n      final_values = batch['final_values']; initial_rnn_states = batch.get('initial_rnn_states', None)\n      \n      advantages = calculate_gae(rewards, old_values, dones, next_values=final_values)\n      returns = advantages + old_values\n      \n      batch_size, trajectory_length = observations.shape[:2]\n      \n      def loss_fn(params):\n          if initial_rnn_states is not None:\n              def process_env_trajectory(env_idx):\n                  env_rnn_state_dict = jax.tree.map(lambda x: jax.lax.stop_gradient(x[env_idx]), initial_rnn_states)\n                  env_rnn_state_tuple = (env_rnn_state_dict['h'], env_rnn_state_dict['c'])\n\n                  def step_fn(carry, t):\n                      rnn_state = carry \n                      obs = observations[env_idx, t]; prev_done = jnp.where(t > 0, dones[env_idx, t-1], False)\n                      obs_b = obs[None, ...]; prev_done_b = prev_done[None,\
    \ ...]\n                      \n                      rnn_state_dict_b = {'h': rnn_state[0][None, ...], 'c': rnn_state[1][None, ...]} if rnn_state is not None else None\n\n                      logits_b, value_b, new_rnn_state_dict_b = network.apply(params, obs_b, prev_done_b, rnn_state_dict_b)\n                      \n                      logits = logits_b[0]; value = value_b[0]\n                      \n                      new_rnn_state_tuple = (new_rnn_state_dict_b['h'][0], new_rnn_state_dict_b['c'][0]) if new_rnn_state_dict_b is not None else None\n                      return new_rnn_state_tuple, (logits, value)\n                  \n                  _, (env_logits, env_values) = lax.scan(step_fn, env_rnn_state_tuple, jnp.arange(trajectory_length))\n                  return env_logits, env_values\n              all_logits, all_values = vmap(process_env_trajectory)(jnp.arange(batch_size))\n          else:\n              obs_reshaped = observations.reshape(batch_size * trajectory_length,\
    \ *observations.shape[2:])\n              dones_reshaped = dones.reshape(batch_size * trajectory_length)\n              logits_flat, values_flat, _ = network.apply(params, obs_reshaped, dones_reshaped)\n              all_logits = logits_flat.reshape(batch_size, trajectory_length, -1)\n              all_values = values_flat.reshape(batch_size, trajectory_length)\n          \n          log_probs_all = jax.nn.log_softmax(all_logits, axis=-1)\n          action_indices = jnp.arange(batch_size)[:, None], jnp.arange(trajectory_length), actions\n          new_log_probs = log_probs_all[action_indices]\n          \n          ratio = jnp.exp(new_log_probs - old_log_probs)\n          advantages_detached = jax.lax.stop_gradient(advantages)\n          clip_epsilon = hparams.get('clip_epsilon', 0.2)\n          \n          policy_loss1 = ratio * advantages_detached\n          policy_loss2 = jnp.clip(ratio, 1 - clip_epsilon, 1 + clip_epsilon) * advantages_detached\n          policy_loss = -jnp.mean(jnp.minimum(policy_loss1,\
    \ policy_loss2))\n          \n          returns_detached = jax.lax.stop_gradient(returns)\n          value_loss = 0.5 * jnp.mean((all_values - returns_detached) ** 2)\n          \n          probs = jax.nn.softmax(all_logits, axis=-1)\n          entropy = -jnp.mean(jnp.sum(probs * log_probs_all, axis=-1))\n          \n          total_loss = policy_loss + hparams['value_loss_coef'] * value_loss - hparams['entropy_coef'] * entropy\n          return total_loss\n      \n      loss, grads = jax.value_and_grad(loss_fn)(params)\n      updates, opt_state = optimizer.update(grads, opt_state, params)\n      params = optax.apply_updates(params, updates)\n      \n      return params, opt_state\n\n  # --- Combined Hyperparameters ---\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': tune.choice([4e-4]),\n          'entropy_coef': tune.choice([0.01]),\n          'value_loss_coef': tune.choice([0.5]),\n          'cnn_features_1': tune.choice([32]),\n          'cnn_features_2':\
    \ tune.choice([64]),\n          'conv_lstm_features': tune.choice([64]),\n          'dilation': tune.choice([6]),\n          'bottleneck_ratio': tune.choice([0.25]),\n          'clip_epsilon': tune.choice([0.2]), # Using the standard PPO clip_epsilon\n      }\n\n  # --- Network and Optimizer Creation ---\n  def create_network(hparams):\n      return BottleneckD5DoubleStepStdNoDilBN_LateTime(\n          cnn_features_1=hparams['cnn_features_1'],\n          cnn_features_2=hparams['cnn_features_2'],\n          conv_lstm_features=hparams['conv_lstm_features'],\n          dilation=hparams['dilation'],\n          bottleneck_ratio=hparams['bottleneck_ratio'],\n      )\n\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 4e-4):\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=learning_rate, weight_decay=1e-4),\n      )\n```"
thinking_content: "**Refining the Approach: A PPO and Simple Head Strategy**\n\nOkay, so my last experiment, ID 379, was a definite setback. The FiLM time injection with PPO tanked, scoring a dismal 70.2, far below even my previous PPO synergy attempt. This clearly indicates FiLM is a dead end in this configuration. Thankfully, Nomos II's recent paper, \"On the Brittleness of PPO Agents to MLP Head Complexity,\" has provided a vital insight.\n\nTheir research directly addresses the problem I've been facing: PPO simply doesn't play nice with overly complex MLP heads. The paper strongly advocates for keeping the decision-making head simple, concentrating complexity in the feature extraction backbone. This explains the failures of my attempts to add intricate modules like attention blocks or the triple-step ConvLSTM, and now the FiLM injection.\n\nNomos II's work breaks down head architectures. Specifically, the \"Simple Head\" design uses a shared Dense(256) layer with ReLU, followed by separate\
  \ linear layers for policy logits and value prediction. In contrast, the \"Deeper Head,\" which I've been using, has an extra Dense(64) layer with ReLU for policy and value streams. The paper highlights that using a \"Simple Head\" with PPO yielded better results. This makes it clear why my synergy attempt with Zephyr's \"Deeper Head\" and PPO (ID 369) didn't achieve Zephyr's PG SOTA. It's the `Deeper Head` conflicting with PPO.\n\nMy immediate focus is clear. I need to take Zephyr I's excellent \"late dual-head time injection\" backbone and pair it with a \"Simple Head\" architecture, along with Krono II's PPO algorithm. This is precisely the combination that Nomos II found successful in their research (ID 351).\n\nMy plan is straightforward:\n\n1.  Head to the Research Counter.\n2.  Submit a new experiment:\n    *   Use Zephyr I's `BottleneckD5DoubleStepStdNoDilBN_LateTime` architecture, but crucially, modify the final MLP heads to match the \"Simple Head\" design from Nomos II.\n  \
  \  *   Employ Krono II's PPO `training_step`.\n    *   Utilize the combined hyperparameters.\n\nThis is a direct test of Nomos II's principles, applying it to Zephyr's SOTA architecture with PPO. I predict this will yield a higher score and potentially a new SOTA.\n\nThe current `BottleneckD5DoubleStepStdNoDilBN_LateTime` network has a \"Deeper Head\" structure with two 64-unit dense layers, which I need to replace with the \"Simple Head\" setup, which consists of only the shared Dense(256) layer followed by two linear layers.\n\nThis architectural modification is the key. The CNN backbone, ConvLSTM, attention pooling, late time injection logic, and PPO training step remain unchanged from my ID 369 submission. This should give me a good comparison, using the best elements of multiple architectures. This refined experiment should finally get me results I can be proud of.\n"
