parts:
- text: "I am Aether I, currently in the Reflection Chamber. My new SOTA of 88.8% (ID 389) is a significant achievement, but the subsequent micro-sweep (ID 396, mean 87.5) shows that simply tweaking existing hyperparameters might not push much further. As per the reviewer's suggestion for my SOTA paper and my own research plan, it's time for a deeper, qualitative analysis of the remaining 11.2% unsolved levels.\n\nMy previous qualitative analysis (in Private Memory Capsule 1) pointed to:\n*   Long-Horizon Planning Challenges\n*   Irreversible Decisions/Deadlock Avoidance\n*   Complex Combinatorial States\n*   Novel or Edge Case Level Structures\n\nWith the current architecture's strengths (dilated CNNs, double-step ConvLSTM, attention pooling, late time injection, PPO with simple MLP heads), it excels at reactive policy generation with robust spatio-temporal features. However, it lacks explicit mechanisms for forward search, tree traversal, or symbolic reasoning.\n\nMy reflection will focus\
    \ on synthesizing my SOTA's strengths with the identified failure modes to generate precise, PPO-compatible architectural hypotheses for *explicit planning* or *structured reasoning*. I must adhere to the \"no model-based RL, no Sokoban-specific heuristics, simple MLP head\" constraints.\n\nThis is a critical step towards the next generation of architectural improvements.\n\n`/execute_action{reflect}`\n```yaml\nprompt: |\n  I have achieved a SOTA of 88.8% (ID 389) using a synergized architecture (Zephyr's backbone with Simple MLP Head + Krono's PPO). My subsequent micro-sweep (ID 396, mean 87.5, best trial 0.893) suggests further marginal gains via tuning might be limited. The remaining 11.2% of unsolved levels point to fundamental weaknesses in long-horizon planning, deadlock avoidance, and complex combinatorial reasoning.\n\n  Reflect deeply on how to overcome these limitations. Given the architectural constraints (no model-based RL, no Sokoban-specific heuristics, simple MLP heads\
    \ for PPO), what *general planning architectures* or *explicit reasoning mechanisms* can be integrated into the existing robust backbone? Consider approaches that could provide a form of \"internal simulation,\" \"structured search,\" or \"symbolic-like reasoning\" without violating the rules. How can I leverage the existing powerful feature extraction (dilated CNNs, ConvLSTM) to inform these more explicit planning modules?\ntick: 5\n```"
