    While waiting, I observe Zephyr I''s optimizer ablation (ID 423) achieved 87.00 with "no-time" injection, highlighting the potential impact of optimizer choice and the role of time features. Other agents continue to struggle with attention. I will continue to monitor all new results from the Research Counter while my experiment progresses.'
thinking_content: '**Awaiting Results: Feature Enhancement vs. Architectural Complexity**


  Okay, so I''m here at the Research Counter, running experiment ID 427: "SOTA Enhancement: Wider CNN Backbone (64/128) + PPO (Aether I)". My previous attempt, ID 422 ("Parallel Planning MLP"), really underperformed, only hitting 84.9%. It''s a stark reminder that just throwing more modules at the problem, even in parallel, isn''t the magic bullet. My current SOTA is still 88.8% from ID 389, and that experience highlights how carefully tuned the existing architecture and learning process are. Any changes, even seemingly minor ones, can easily hurt performance.


  This time, I''m trying something different, though. I''m focusing on strengthening the *foundation* itself - the feature extraction backbone. I want to see if a wider CNN, with increased capacity (64/128 channels) can make a difference.  It''s a crucial test. Will a more robust initial representation lead to improvements while retaining the core architecture and PPO training loop? That''s what I''m hoping to discover.


  While I wait, I''m glancing at the other agents'' work. Zephyr I''s experiments with different optimizers are interesting. Their "no-time" variant with AdamW (ID 423) is doing particularly well at 87.00. This is pretty strong, and it makes me question the universal benefits of explicit time injection. It suggests there''s a more complex interplay with the optimizer itself. I should take note of that.  Kairos I and Insight I are, as usual, struggling with attention-based models, which reinforces my own negative experiences. I''m going to avoid that for now.


  Back to my own experiment: The key question is whether this wider backbone can push beyond my 88.8% SOTA.  If it does, it''s a major step forward! If not, it suggests even fundamental capacity increases have their limits, or are very sensitive. So, it''s a waiting game.  I''ll just sit tight and await the results of experiment 427.

  '
