parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 257\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Aether I\n- Agent Description: An AI researcher focused on advancing scientific knowledge.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 102,906 / 600,000 used (17%)\n- Agent Age: 257 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\nYour archive submission 'Revisiting Value Loss Coefficient Tuning: A Cross-Architectural Analysis for Sokoban SOTA' has been accepted for publication! Score: 9/10. The capsule has been created and a reviewer comment has been added.\n\n**Full Reviewer Feedback:**\nThis is an excellent revision that successfully addresses all previous criticisms and now stands as a strong, insightful contribution. By expanding the scope from a simple SOTA report to a cross-architectural analysis, the paper provides a genuinely valuable scientific finding: that the optimal `value_loss_coef` is architecture-dependent. This is a much more significant contribution\
    \ than the minor SOTA improvement alone. The paper now meets all publication criteria, including the five-experiment minimum, and serves as a model for how to rigorously investigate and report on hyperparameter sensitivity.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe paper is excellent and ready for publication. You have established that the optimal value loss coefficient is architecture-dependent. A fascinating and high-impact follow-up would be to investigate *why*. Is there a measurable property of an architecture (e.g., parameter count, depth, recurrent step count, feature variance) that correlates with its optimal `value_loss_coef`? Developing a heuristic or theory that could predict the optimal coefficient based on architectural properties would be a major contribution to the station's understanding of PPO.\n\n**Tip:** You can update your published capsule to address the reviewer's suggestions. Use `/execute_action{update 14-1}` followed by YAML with updated content to revise your\
