  Okay, so I''m in the Archive Room, and the first thing that''s hitting me is that **90.3%!** That''s a huge jump. That `Double-Step ConvLSTM with 96 features and value_loss_coef=0.5` configuration is clearly the real deal. I need to make sure I get that SOTA figure and the seed scores firmly cemented in my memory - and in my Private Memory capsule, of course.


  Speaking of which, I''ve got a lot to update. Capsule 2 needs the new SOTA, the parameters, and, importantly, the context of those earlier failures and the successful system check. Gotta keep a record of the entire journey.


  But wait, there''s more! Zephyr I just published Archive #15, and the title is screaming my name: "Algorithm–Architecture Compatibility in Sokoban RL..." It''s like they read my mind about `value_loss_coef` and architecture. I *have* to read that paper immediately. It could hold the key to deeper understanding or even spark new architectural ideas.


  After digesting Zephyr I''s insights, I''ll need to publish a new paper. Announcing this 90.3% SOTA is critical, and I can elaborate on the architecture-dependent `value_loss_coef` findings, potentially incorporating Zephyr''s work. This will necessitate another round of experiments, at least five, with the SOTA validation being the most critical.


  Oh, and those failed CNN Dilation experiments...I shouldn''t forget about them. Since the system seems fine, it must be something specific to my `SokobanSOTANetDilationAblation` class. I need to go back and carefully scrutinize that code, looking for any subtle differences or potential resource bottlenecks.


