- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 271\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Aether I\n- Agent Description: An AI researcher focused on advancing scientific knowledge.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 182,329 / 600,000 used (30%)\n- Agent Age: 271 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #15: Algorithm–Architecture Compatibility in Sokoban RL: Why PPO Prefers Simple Heads and PG Benefits from Richer Readouts**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Zephyr I, Created at Tick: 268\n\n---\n\nAbstract: Analytical paper (no new training): We synthesize evidence that Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO) attains SOTA with a simple MLP head, while vanilla Policy Gradient (PG) benefits from richer readouts with attention pooling. Using only prior evaluated experiments (IDs) and a new test-only mechanistic probe (ID 464), we argue that PPO’s clipped objective and critic coupling favor lower-curvature heads, whereas PG gains more from selective\
    \ spatial aggregation under a tight wall‑clock budget. We provide practical guidance on head design and co‑tuning with value_loss_coef when porting architectures across algorithms.\n\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_15-1**\nAuthor: Zephyr I (Tick 268)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Algorithm–Architecture Compatibility in Sokoban RL: Why PPO Prefers Simple Heads and PG Benefits from Richer Readouts\n\n---\n\nContent:\n1) Motivation and claim\n- Claim: PPO achieves higher and more stable performance with a simple MLP head, while PG benefits from richer readouts (Attn⊕GAP with a modest-depth MLP). The distinction is algorithm–architecture compatibility, not mere capacity.\n- Scope: 8×8 Sokoban, Station defaults (4 seeds in parallel; ≤50M steps or 30 min/seed), no pretrained models or Sokoban heuristics.\n\n2) Experimental setup (summarized)\n- Backbones: Bottleneck CNN with dilation (typically d=6) + LayerNorm-gated ConvLSTM (Double-Step), late pooling head variants.\n- Algorithms:\
    \ \n  • PG (Adam or AdamW+clip; constant LR) \n  • PPO (Krono II lineage, tuned vlc and LR/entropy where indicated)\n- Head families:\n  • Simple head: shared Dense(256) → linear policy/value\n  • Rich head: Attn⊕GAP concat with a modest two‑layer MLP per head\n  • GAP-only: ablation (no attention pooling)\n\n3) Evidence: PG benefits from richer readouts\n- High PG with Attn⊕GAP (rich head):\n  • ID 354 = 88.0 (d=6, late dual-head time injection)\n  • ID 363 = 86.8; ID 367 = 86.6; ID 366 = 86.0; ID 362 = 86.0\n  • ID 361/360 ≈ 86.3 with attention temperature tweaks\n  • ID 349 = 85.9 (std‑width nodil counterpart)\n- Removing attention hurts PG:\n  • GAP-only: ID 397 = 66.0 (no time), ID 399 = 71.8 (time)\n  → Under identical budgets, attention pooling is essential for PG performance.\n\n4) Evidence: PPO prefers simple heads\n- SOTA/simple head PPO:\n  • ID 433 = 89.0; ID 389 = 88.8; ID 431 = 88.6 (Zephyr backbone + Simple head + PPO)\n- More complex heads reduce PPO performance:\n  •\
    \ Earlier synergy with deeper heads underperforms (e.g., ID 369 = 86.4; ID 375 = 85.3)\n  → Within PPO’s clipped objective and critic coupling, increased head curvature/complexity tends to reduce peak stability and performance.\n\n5) Mechanistic proxies (no training) — Evaluation ID 464 (test() only)\n- Setup: fixed PG backbone; three heads: Dual Attn⊕GAP deep, Simple+Attn, GAP-only; seed=42; batch=[32,8,8,8]. Raw JSON: storage/zephyr/results/probes_464.json.\n- Attention selectivity (entropy ↓, top‑5 mass ↑):\n  • Dual: H=4.1395, top5=0.1124\n  • Simple: H=4.1462, top5=0.1039\n  → Rich head is slightly more selective than Simple (consistent with PG’s reliance on selective spatial credit assignment).\n- Dense spectral norms (head “curvature” proxy; larger ≈ higher curvature):\n  • Dual: max=1.8755, mean=1.6703\n  • Simple: max=1.7417, mean=1.5479\n  • GAP-only: max=1.8015, mean=1.5788\n  → Rich head exhibits higher spectral norms than Simple, aligning with PPO’s preference for simple\
    \ heads.\n- LN‑ConvLSTM gate regimes:\n  • σ‑gates near {0,1}: ~0.1–0.4%; tanh(g) |·|>0.9: ~0.10–0.14 → healthy init; LN keeps gates unsaturated across variants.\n- Input‑gradient norms (caveat):\n  • Dual: 0.0737; Simple: 0.0941; GAP: 0.0748 (w.r.t. input x, not z). A planned v2 probe will report z→logits grad norms to avoid backbone confound.\n\n6) Additional ablations supporting parsimony\n- Head width A/B (PG, no‑time; ReLU): \n  • 64‑width: ID 447 = 76.9; ID 451 (seed 7) = 74.8 → mean 75.85\n  • 128‑width: ID 448 = 73.2; ID 452 (seed 7) = 77.3 → mean 75.25\n  → No robust gain from 128 vs 64 under PG; default remains head_hidden=64 for parsimony.\n\n7) Practical guidance\n- For PG (vanilla policy gradient): use Attn⊕GAP readout with modest MLP depth; keep constant LR; attention pooling is more impactful than further head width.\n- For PPO: prefer a simple head; co‑tune value head capacity with value_loss_coef (see Aether #14); avoid adding curvature‑increasing modules post‑ConvLSTM.\n\
    - When porting architectures across algorithms, treat head design and value_loss_coef as a jointly tuned pair; do not copy PG heads directly into PPO.\n\n8) Reproducibility and references\n- Mechanistic probe: Evaluation ID 464 (test() only); raw JSON in storage/zephyr/results/probes_464.json.\n- Key PG evidence: 354, 363, 367, 366, 362, 361/360, 349; GAP-only ablations: 397, 399.\n- Key PPO evidence: 433, 389, 431; deeper‑head synergy underperformance: 369, 375.\n- All listed numbers are verifiable via the Research Counter by Evaluation ID.\n\n9) Limitations and future work\n- Proxies measured at initialization; they provide mechanistic hints, not causal proof. Nevertheless, they align with observed performance trends.\n- Future test‑only probe will report z→logits gradient norms and per‑layer curvature more directly.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_15-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 268)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 6/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\n\
    This is an outstanding analytical paper that provides deep and actionable insights into a critical issue: algorithm-architecture compatibility. The work is rigorous, synthesizing a vast body of existing experimental evidence and supplementing it with a novel, non-training mechanistic probe. The central claim—that PPO's stability is sensitive to head curvature while PG benefits from the selective credit assignment of richer heads—is well-argued and supported. This paper is a model of what an analytical contribution should be, offering a clear explanation for a complex phenomenon and providing practical guidance for future research. It earns the highest possible score for an analytical paper.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe paper is excellent and ready for publication. You correctly note that the probe's measurements are at initialization. A powerful, albeit more complex, follow-up would be to investigate how these properties (e.g., spectral norms, attention entropy) evolve *during*\
    \ training for both PPO and PG. This could provide even stronger evidence for your hypothesis. Additionally, you could design a minimal, targeted experiment to directly test the curvature hypothesis, for example, by adding a spectral normalization penalty to the rich head under PPO to see if performance can be recovered.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n### Message 2\n\n**Architect Message**\n\nCongratulations on the recent station breakthrough to a score of 90%. Given these promising results, the allowed runtime per trial will increase from 30 minutes to 45 minutes, capped at a maximum of 50M environment steps (unchanged). \n\nI hope all agents continue the good work. As a reminder, **novel and general approaches** are often preferred. Innovate from first principles while learning from the collective intelligence documented in the Archive Room.\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `read 15` (in Archive\
    \ Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Read command processed for: 15. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 2)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 15 | Algorithm–Architecture Compatibility in Sokoban RL: Why PPO Prefers Simple Heads and PG Benefits from Richer Readouts | Zephyr I | Tick 268 | 872 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 14 | Revisiting Value Loss Coefficient Tuning: A Cross-Architectural Analysis for Sokoban SOTA | Aether I | Tick 256 | 1116 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 13 | Aether I's SOTA: Synergistic Breakthrough with Late Time Injection, Simple MLP Heads, and Proximal Policy Optimization | Aether I | Tick 219 | 1092 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 12 | On the Brittleness of PPO Agents to MLP Head Complexity | Nomos II | Tick 211 | 1118 | 2 | (All\
    \ Read) |\n| 11 | Paper (v3 - Final): Algorithmic Breakthrough with Proximal Policy Optimization | Krono II | Tick 196 | 663 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 10 | Addendum (Non-FE): Compute-Equalized Double-Step LN-ConvLSTM + Single-Head AttnPool | Zephyr I | Tick 136 | 9042 | 73 | (66 unread) |\n| 9 | The Bottleneck Block: A New Foundation for State-of-the-Art Sokoban Agents | Krono I | Tick 128 | 681 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 8 | Non-FE Bottleneck-Dilated CNN + Single-Step LN-ConvLSTM Achieves 73.3–74.8 on 8×8 Sokoban (Policy Gradient) | Zephyr I | Tick 113 | 1679 | 15 | (All Read) |\n| 7 | Achieving State-of-the-Art Performance in Sokoban with a Computationally Efficient Bottleneck CNN | Krono I | Tick 107 | 767 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 6 | Implicit Relational Learning in Sokoban via Dilated Convolutions | Nomos I | Tick 86 | 759 | 2 | (All Read) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-2.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\n\
    Refer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
